/* Glossary tooltip descriptions, keyed by Wikipedia article slug (decoded). Add terms one at a time with
   `node .claude/add-glossary.js <entry.json>` (see CLAUDE.md). Missing terms fall back to the slug name. */
window.GLOSSARY = {
"Sima_Qian": "Sima Qian was a Han official who succeeded his father as Grand Scribe of the imperial court and is reckoned the first of China's canonical historians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i>, written privately rather than on imperial commission, laid down the arrangement that the official histories of the later dynasties kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Condemned to castration after speaking up for the general Li Ling, he wrote that he bore the punishment rather than take his own life so that he could finish the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Paleolithic": "The Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, is the earliest and by far the longest phase of the Stone Age, spanning most of human prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began with the first use of stone tools by early humans and lasted until the end of the last Ice Age, throughout which people lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stretching over millions of years, it encompasses the emergence and spread of the human species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Stone_Age": "The Stone Age is the earliest and by far the longest prehistoric period, the first stage of the three-age system, defined by the manufacture of stone tools before the working of metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It spans roughly the first 99 percent of human history, beginning with the oldest known stone tools and ending with the advent of metallurgy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its boundaries vary by region: it closed around 3300 BCE in the Near East, later across Europe and Asia, and in parts of Africa societies passed from stone straight to iron without a bronze-using age at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Bronze_Age": "The middle period of the three-age archaeological system, falling between the Stone Age and the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is defined by the widespread use of bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, and coincided with the rise of writing, cities, and complex states in regions such as Mesopotamia and Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because it is marked by technology rather than a fixed date, its span varies by region, beginning around 3300 BCE in the Near East and arriving later elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Iron_Age": "The Iron Age is the final stage of the three-age system, a prehistoric and protohistoric period defined by the widespread smelting and working of iron and steel for tools and weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed the Bronze Age, as iron implements gradually replaced bronze ones once techniques for removing impurities and controlling carbon content were mastered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its onset varies widely by region, beginning near the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ice_Age": "An ice age is a long period in the history of the Earth during which global temperatures fall and continental ice sheets and mountain glaciers spread across large parts of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock record holds several such episodes over the past 2.4 billion years, each lasting many millions of years and alternating between colder glacial phases and milder interglacial phases; the current one began about 2.58 million years ago and continues today, since vast ice sheets still cover Antarctica and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In everyday usage the Ice Age generally means the most recent glacial phase, whose ice sheets were at their greatest between roughly 26,000 and 19,000 years ago, when sea level stood some 130 m (430 feet) below today's, and which ended about 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Middle_Paleolithic": "The Middle Paleolithic is the middle subdivision of the Old Stone Age, spanning roughly 300,000 to 50,000 years ago across Europe, the Near East, and much of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is defined largely by prepared-core knapping methods such as the Levallois technique, which yielded more standardized flakes and supported the first composite tools like hafted stone-tipped spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period is associated with Neanderthals and early anatomically modern humans, and it produced some of the earliest known deliberate burials of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Upper_Paleolithic": "The Upper Paleolithic is the final subdivision of the Paleolithic, lasting from roughly 50,000 to 11,700 years ago and ending with the close of the last Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is associated with the spread of anatomically modern humans across most of the world and with a marked increase in the variety and refinement of stone tools, especially long, narrow blades struck from prepared cores together with specialised implements of bone, antler, and ivory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period also saw the first widespread figurative art, including cave paintings, engravings, carved animal and human figurines, and personal ornaments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Three-age_system": "The three-age system is a framework that divides prehistory into three successive periods, the Stone Age, the Bronze Age, and the Iron Age, each defined by the material that dominated tool and weapon making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several antiquaries had put forward something of the kind before the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, given charge of the Copenhagen antiquities collection in 1816, arranged that collection into stone, bronze and iron rooms and set the scheme out in a museum guidebook published in 1836.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later research added refinements such as the Copper Age and finer subdivisions, and although the scheme fits regions such as the Americas and sub-Saharan Africa poorly, it remains the backbone of prehistoric chronology in Europe and the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Knapping": "Knapping is the technique of shaping stone by striking flakes from a core with a hammerstone or with a softer hammer of antler, bone, or hard wood, and by pressing flakes off the edge with a pointed tool in the method known as pressure flaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It relies on fine-grained stones such as flint, chert, and obsidian, which break with a smooth, predictable conchoidal fracture that lets the worker control the size and shape of each flake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The technique dates back to the earliest stone tools of prehistory and produced handaxes, blades, scrapers, and arrowheads; in more recent centuries knappers also made gunflints for firearms and shaped flint for building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hunter-gatherer": "A hunter-gatherer is a member of a society that obtains its food by hunting wild animals, fishing, and gathering wild plants, fruits, and other edible resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All humans lived this way for the overwhelming majority of their existence, until the adoption of agriculture beginning roughly 12,000 years ago, so foraging accounts for well over 90 percent of human history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hunter-gatherer groups were typically mobile, living in small bands of a few dozen people that shared food widely under flexible and largely egalitarian leadership, and a small number of communities around the world still follow this way of life today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lower_Paleolithic": "The Lower Paleolithic is the earliest and longest subdivision of the Paleolithic, extending from the first stone tools about 2.6 million years ago — or 3.3 million if disputed finds are counted — to roughly 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the age of early hominins such as Homo habilis, Homo erectus, and Homo heidelbergensis, who made the simple flaked cores and choppers of the Oldowan industry and later the bifacial hand axes of the Acheulean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some of the earliest evidence for the controlled use of fire also belongs to this period, marking a major step in human technological and behavioural development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Pleistocene": "The Pleistocene is the geological epoch spanning roughly 2.58 million to 11,700 years ago, the first epoch of the Quaternary Period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was marked by repeated cycles of glaciation, the ice ages, during which vast sheets of ice spread across large parts of the Northern Hemisphere and sea levels fell sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The epoch was the age of great land mammals such as mammoths: of the 57 plant-eating species that then weighed a tonne or more only 11 survive today, and the same epoch saw anatomically modern humans evolve in Africa and spread across nearly every continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Archaeology": "Archaeology is the study of human history and prehistory through the excavation and analysis of material remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It examines artifacts, architecture, and other physical evidence to understand how past peoples lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is especially valuable for prehistoric societies that left no written records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Megafauna": "Megafauna are the large animals of a given region or geological period, conventionally those whose adult body mass exceeds a set threshold, most often placed at about 44 kilograms (97 pounds), although proposed limits range from roughly 10 kilograms (22 pounds) to well over 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pleistocene epoch supported an especially rich megafauna, among them mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths and woolly rhinoceroses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many of these large animals died out toward the end of the last Ice Age, in a wave of extinctions that most researchers attribute to a combination of climatic change and human hunting, though the relative weight of each cause remains debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australopithecus": "<i>Australopithecus</i> is an extinct genus of early hominins that lived in Africa between roughly 4.2 and 2 million years ago, walking upright on two legs while keeping a small, ape-sized brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among its best-known fossils is the roughly 3.2-million-year-old partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy, a member of the species <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The genus is widely regarded as ancestral to, or closely related to, the human genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sonia_Harmand": "Sonia Harmand is a French archaeologist and lithic technologist whose work focuses on the earliest stone tools and the origins of tool-making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She has led the West Turkana Archaeological Project in northern Kenya since 2012, dividing her career between the Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique in France and Stony Brook University in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2011 her team found flaked stone artifacts at the site of Lomekwi 3 that were dated to 3.3 million years ago, about 700,000 years older than any previously known, pushing the start of the archaeological record back before the appearance of the genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jason_E._Lewis": "Jason E. Lewis is an American paleoanthropologist whose research spans the earliest stone tools, the fossil record of eastern Africa, and the history of physical anthropology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Based at Stony Brook University and its Turkana Basin Institute, he co-directs the West Turkana Archaeological Project in northern Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a co-discoverer of the 3.3-million-year-old artifacts at Lomekwi 3, the oldest stone tools yet found, and he also led a re-measurement of the 19th-century collection of human skulls assembled by Samuel George Morton, which challenged the widely repeated claim that Morton had skewed his data.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Greece": "Greece is a country of 131,957 km² (50,949 sq mi) in southeastern Europe occupying the southern end of the Balkan peninsula together with several thousand islands in the Aegean, Ionian and Mediterranean seas, with its capital at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mountains cover about four-fifths of its territory and its deeply indented coastline is the longest of any country in the Mediterranean basin, conditions that favored seafaring and left inland communities divided by hard terrain. Its ancient city-states produced the earliest known democratic government along with lasting traditions of philosophy, drama, mathematics and historical writing, while the modern Greek state grew out of a war of independence against Ottoman rule and was recognized as sovereign in 1830.",
"John_Lubbock,_1st_Baron_Avebury": "John Lubbock, from 1900 the 1st Baron Avebury, was a British banker, member of parliament and naturalist who helped turn the study of prehistory into a scientific discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His survey <i>Pre-historic Times</i>, published in 1865 and revised through seven editions, introduced the terms Paleolithic and Neolithic for the earlier and later phases of the Stone Age, and both remain in everyday use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In parliament he carried the act that created the country's first statutory public holidays, popularly called St Lubbock's Days; his own ancient monuments bill reached a second reading seven times without becoming law, and the act that protected such sites from 1882 was framed by another hand on the policy he had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neanderthal": "Neanderthals, classified as <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i>, were a population of archaic humans who lived across Europe and western and central Asia from at least 400,000 years ago until roughly 40,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They take their name from the Neander valley near Düsseldorf, where a partial skeleton found in 1856 became the type specimen of the species, and the hundreds of fossils recovered since show a stocky, powerfully built body with a large braincase and prominent brow ridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They made prepared-core stone tools, hunted large game, controlled fire and buried some of their dead, and genetic evidence shows they interbred with the ancestors of people living today, leaving a small proportion of Neanderthal DNA in most present-day populations outside Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homo_sapiens": "<i>Homo sapiens</i> is the species to which all living people belong and the only surviving member of the human genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fossils combining the modern small face, rounded braincase and distinct chin appear in Africa by about 300,000 years ago, though these features seem to have emerged piecemeal in populations spread across the continent rather than all at once in a single place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Members of the species later settled every continent and are associated with symbolic art, complex language, and eventually agriculture, cities and writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Homo_erectus": "<i>Homo erectus</i> is an extinct species of early human that appeared in Africa about 2 million years ago and survived on the island of Java until roughly 110,000 years ago, making it one of the longest-lived species in the human genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the first known human with body proportions close to those of people today, having long legs, short arms and a narrow trunk suited to walking and running across open country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was also the first human species known to have spread beyond Africa, reaching eastern and southeastern Asia, and its African sites yield the teardrop-shaped hand axes of the Acheulean industry along with some of the earliest evidence for the controlled use of fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Oldowan": "The Oldowan is the earliest widespread stone tool industry, made across much of Africa and later in parts of Eurasia from roughly 2.6 million years ago until about 1.7 million years ago, with recent finds in Kenya suggesting an origin closer to 2.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its toolkit is simple: rounded cores struck a few times with a hammerstone to produce sharp-edged flakes, together with the choppers and battered stones left behind, all used for cutting meat from carcasses, breaking bones for marrow and pounding plant food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes its name from Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania, where Louis Leakey identified such tools in the 1930s, and the hominins who made it are thought to include <i>Homo habilis</i>, early <i>Homo erectus</i> and possibly <i>Paranthropus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Acheulean": "The Acheulean is a stone tool industry defined by the large bifacial hand axe, a core worked on both faces into a symmetrical oval or teardrop shape with a continuous cutting edge, usually accompanied by cleavers, picks and the flakes struck off in shaping them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began in Africa about 1.7 million years ago and ran on until about 125,000 years ago, ending far earlier in some regions than in others, which makes it the longest-running toolmaking tradition known, and its products are found across Africa, the Middle East, Europe and southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Named after the gravels of Saint-Acheul near Amiens in northern France, where such tools were recovered in the 1850s, it is attributed mainly to <i>Homo erectus</i> and later <i>Homo heidelbergensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neolithic": "The Neolithic, or New Stone Age, is the final phase of the Stone Age, in which people came to depend on cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than on wild food alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its characteristic traits are permanent villages, pottery, weaving, and stone tools finished by grinding and polishing instead of flaking alone, and it is the phase in which the first monumental tombs were raised, some of them reopened for burial after burial across centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because farming was taken up at different times in different places, the period has no single span: it begins around 10,000 BCE in the Middle East and only in the 3rd millennium BCE elsewhere, ending wherever metalworking took hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Mesolithic": "The Mesolithic is a phase of the Stone Age falling between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic, covering the hunter-gatherer societies that adapted to the warmer, wetter conditions after the last Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tools are dominated by microliths, small blades and points struck from carefully prepared cores and set into wooden or bone hafts to make arrows, harpoons and composite knives, and its sites show heavy use of fish, shellfish, waterfowl and hazelnuts, along with domesticated dogs and some of the oldest boats known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The term was introduced by the antiquarian Hodder Westropp in 1866 and is used mainly for Europe, since the sequence it describes fits some regions poorly: the corresponding phase in the Levant is called the Epipalaeolithic and begins thousands of years earlier, and in Africa the label is rarely applied at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Lomekwi": "Lomekwi is a fossil-rich stretch of badlands on the western shore of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where sediments laid down between roughly 3.6 and 3.2 million years ago lie exposed at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations there in 1998 and 1999 recovered the flat-faced skull KNM-WT 40000, described as a new species under the name <i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>, along with hundreds of animal fossils that together reconstruct a landscape of shallow lakes and woodland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A separate locality in the same area, Lomekwi 3, yielded flaked stone artifacts dated to 3.3 million years ago, the oldest stone tools known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kenya": "Kenya is a country in East Africa that straddles the equator, with a coast on the Indian Ocean, a central highland plateau split from north to south by the Great Rift Valley, and a share of Lake Victoria in the west. Its landscapes run from coral reefs and coastal forest through dry savanna and semi-desert to the glaciated peaks of Mount Kenya, and the Rift Valley sediments, especially those around Lake Turkana, have produced one of the world's richest records of early human ancestors. More than 40 languages are spoken, with Swahili and English as the official ones, and the country came under British control in the late 19th century before becoming independent in 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Olduvai_Gorge": "Olduvai Gorge is a steep ravine about 46 km (29 miles) long cutting through the eastern Serengeti plains of northern Tanzania, where a seasonal stream has laid bare a thick stack of lake sediments and volcanic ash layers covering the last 2 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because those ash layers can be dated directly, the sequence offers an unusually well-anchored record of early human evolution, and excavations led by Louis and Mary Leakey from the 1930s onward recovered Oldowan stone tools together with hominin fossils, among them the type specimen of <i>Homo habilis</i> and the robust skull once nicknamed Zinjanthropus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name is a European misrendering of the Maasai word <i>oldupai</i>, the wild sisal that grows along the gorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Tanzania": "Tanzania is a country in East Africa with a long Indian Ocean coast, the offshore islands of the Zanzibar archipelago, and a share of the three great lakes of the African interior, Victoria, Tanganyika and Malawi. It holds both the highest point in Africa, the volcanic cone of Kilimanjaro, and the lowest, the floor of Lake Tanganyika, and much of its north lies in the eastern branch of the Great Rift Valley, taking in the Serengeti plains and the Ngorongoro crater. Swahili serves as a common language across more than a hundred ethnic groups, and the modern state was formed in 1964 when Tanganyika, independent from Britain since 1961, united with the newly independent Zanzibar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Homo_habilis": "<i>Homo habilis</i> is an extinct species of early human that lived in eastern and southern Africa between about 2.4 and 1.4 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a braincase larger than that of earlier hominins, reconstructed at roughly 730 to 820 millilitres in the type specimen though still far short of a modern human's, along with a smaller face and teeth and a body that kept comparatively long arms, and its name, meaning 'handy man', records the belief that it was the first maker of stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The species was established in 1964 by Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier on the basis of remains from Olduvai Gorge, and whether those fossils truly belong in the genus <i>Homo</i>, or represent more than one species, has been argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saint-Acheul": "Saint-Acheul is a quarter of the city of Amiens in northern France, built on terraces of gravel left by the river Somme, and it is the type site of the Acheulean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Quarrying there through the 19th century turned up large numbers of bifacial flint hand axes, first reported in 1859, and in 1872 the prehistorian Gabriel de Mortillet borrowed the site's name for the whole industry those tools represent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part of the deposits survives as an open-air archaeological garden, listed as a historic monument in 1947 and opened to visitors in 1998.",
"France": "France is a country in western Europe reaching from the North Sea and the English Channel down to the Mediterranean, and from the Rhine across to the Atlantic, with overseas regions in the Caribbean, South America and the Indian and Pacific oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its territory takes in the wide plains of the Paris basin, the volcanic uplands of the Massif Central and the high frontier walls of the Alps and Pyrenees, and it remains one of Europe's most productive farming countries as well as a major industrial economy drawing most of its electricity from nuclear power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A kingdom consolidated through the Middle Ages, it saw the monarchy abolished in the revolution that began in 1789, ruled a colonial empire across much of Africa and Southeast Asia, and is today a permanent member of the United Nations Security Council and a founding member of what became the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Dmanisi": "Dmanisi is an archaeological site in the Kvemo Kartli region of southern Georgia, about 85 km (53 miles) south-west of Tbilisi, where hominin remains lie in volcanic and lake deposits beneath the ruins of a medieval town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fossils and simple stone tools recovered there since 1991 are dated to between about 1.85 and 1.77 million years ago, which makes them the oldest securely dated human remains outside Africa, and the site has produced five skulls along with dozens of other bones, an unusually complete sample for so early a period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The individuals had small brains and short bodies, and their classification is still contested: most researchers place them within <i>Homo erectus</i>, while others argue for a separate species or even for two kinds of hominin living side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Georgia_(country)": "Georgia is a country of 69,700 km² (26,900 sq mi) in the Caucasus on the eastern shore of the Black Sea, with roughly 3.8 million people and its capital at Tbilisi, where the Greater Caucasus range along its northern border and the Lesser Caucasus in the south enclose a corridor of valleys that has carried traffic between Europe and Asia for thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has its own language and its own alphabet, neither of them related to the tongues of its neighbours, and it adopted Christianity in the 4th century, which makes it one of the oldest Christian states. Ruled at different times from Persia, the Ottoman Empire and Russia, it was briefly independent after 1918, spent most of the 20th century as a Soviet republic, and regained its independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nomadism": "Nomadism is a way of life in which a community moves from place to place instead of settling permanently, timing its movements to the seasons, to grazing and water, or to the ripening of wild foods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herders who drive livestock between pastures, foragers who follow game and plant harvests, and travelling craft and trading groups all follow versions of it, and the routes are usually well known and repeated year after year rather than aimless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because everything must be carried, nomadic households keep their possessions few and portable, and wealth tends to be held in animals, skills and obligations between people rather than in buildings or land.",
"Pacific_Ocean": "The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest of the world's ocean basins, covering about 155 million km² (59.8 million sq mi), close to a third of the Earth's surface, and holding more than half of the free water on the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reaches from the Arctic in the north to the Southern Ocean, and from Asia and Australia across to the Americas, and its floor holds the deepest known point on the planet, the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench, measured from a submersible in 2020 at 10,935 m (35,876 feet) below sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ringed by the volcanoes and earthquake belts of the Pacific Ring of Fire and scattered with tens of thousands of islands, it was crossed and settled by seafaring peoples who reached East Polynesia by about 900 CE, centuries before Ferdinand Magellan's fleet entered it in November 1520 and gave it the name it still carries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pacific_Northwest_Coast": "The Pacific Northwest Coast is the narrow, heavily forested strip of North America's western seaboard running from southern Alaska through British Columbia to northern California, hemmed in by mountains and cut into fjords, inlets and thousands of islands. Cool, wet and extraordinarily productive, it carried dense runs of salmon along with plentiful shellfish, sea mammals and enormous conifers, and anthropologists treat it as a single culture area because the peoples along it share many practices despite speaking unrelated languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its societies were unusual among foraging peoples in living in large permanent plank houses, storing preserved food on a great scale, and maintaining ranked social orders with inherited titles, hereditary chiefs and slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tlingit": "The Tlingit are an Indigenous people of the northern Pacific Northwest Coast, living along the panhandle of southeastern Alaska and in adjoining parts of British Columbia and the Yukon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their society is organized by descent through the mother into two great halves, Raven and Eagle or Wolf, subdivided into clans that own crests, songs, names and stretches of land for hunting and fishing, and that settle obligations to the opposite half at ceremonies where goods change hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lived on salmon, halibut, sea mammals and shellfish, built large timber clan houses and raised carved poles of red cedar, and their language, Lingít, belongs to the Na-Dene family, which links it to the Athabaskan languages of the interior rather than to its coastal neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Haida": "The Haida are an Indigenous people of the northern Pacific Northwest Coast whose homeland is Haida Gwaii, an archipelago off the coast of British Columbia, with a related community in southeastern Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Haida society traces descent through the mother and is divided into two halves, Raven and Eagle, each made up of clans that hold their own crests, names and stretches of land, with names conferred and obligations to the opposite half settled at ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are especially known for monumental work in red cedar, including great longhouses and carved poles bearing Eagle and Raven crests, and for a distinctive carving tradition that carried through a collapse from introduced smallpox, measles and tuberculosis, from at least 20,000 people in the late 1700s to fewer than 600 on Haida Gwaii a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cedar": "Cedar is a name given to a number of unrelated coniferous trees valued for aromatic, straight-grained wood that resists decay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs properly to <i>Cedrus</i>, a genus of four species from the mountains of the Mediterranean and the western Himalaya, but the same name is applied to many trees of the cypress family, among them the western redcedar and yellow-cedar of North America, the incense-cedars, and the Japanese sugi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What such timbers commonly share is heartwood carrying natural fungicidal compounds, which leaves the wood light, easy to split and long-lasting even in wet ground, and has led people in many regions to use it for roofing, boats, chests, coffins and carved work, and to burn it as incense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hadza_people": "The Hadza are an Indigenous people of about 1,000 living around Lake Eyasi in the Rift Valley of northern Tanzania, some 300 of whom still take most of their food by hunting wild game and gathering tubers, berries, baobab fruit and wild honey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They live in short-term camps of roughly 25 to 75 people, recognize neither formal leaders nor a settled hierarchy and share meat widely within and between camps, with men hunting big game with bows and poisoned arrows while women and children do most of the plant gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their language, Hadzane, includes click consonants and is an isolate with no undisputed relatives, and genetic work places their ancestry among the earliest lineages to diverge in Africa, which is part of why anthropologists and geneticists have studied them so closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"San_peoples": "The San peoples are Indigenous peoples of southern Africa, counted at fewer than 110,000 across Botswana, Namibia, South Africa, Angola, Zambia and Zimbabwe in the fullest regional survey, who historically lived by hunting and gathering in the Kalahari and the drier country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not one nation but many communities with their own names, among them the Juǀʼhoansi, the ǃKung and the Naro, speaking a range of languages that make heavy use of click consonants; almost every collective label for them, San and the older Bushmen alike, was coined by outsiders, and San delegates settled on San in 1993 only as the least loaded available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Studies of their genetics have identified some of the deepest-branching lineages known among living humans, and few if any San now live chiefly by foraging, most being settled in villages on a mixed economy of hunting, wage work, craft sales and pensions, with access to ancestral land still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Arctic": "The Arctic is the northernmost region of the Earth, most often defined as the area within the Arctic Circle at about 66° 33′ north, though it is also drawn at the northern tree line, which broadly follows the limit where the average July temperature stays below 10 °C (50 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It centres on an ocean rather than a continent, the ice-covered Arctic Ocean, largely surrounded by the northern edges of North America, Europe and Asia, and its year runs to extremes of light: above the Arctic Circle the Sun does not set on the summer solstice and does not rise on the winter solstice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tundra, sea ice and permafrost dominate its landscapes, most of the world's permafrost lies here, and it supports specially adapted life from lichens and mosses to caribou, polar bears, seals and whales; much of the American Arctic has been inhabited for some 5,000 years, and the ancestors of today's Inuit and Yup'ik spread across it about a thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Levallois": "Levallois is a method of stone toolmaking in which a core is first trimmed and shaped so that a single flake of predetermined size and outline can then be struck from it in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the finished flake exists in the knapper's intention before it exists in the stone, the method is often taken as evidence of planning several steps ahead, and it yields flakes of unusually even thickness and regular outline, leaving a distinctive tortoise-shell pattern of scars on the discarded core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears in Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, with contested claims for earlier examples, and it takes its name from Levallois-Perret, a suburb of Paris where such cores turned up in 19th-century gravel quarries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mousterian": "The Mousterian is a stone tool industry of the Middle Paleolithic, current in Europe, western Asia and North Africa from roughly 300,000 to 40,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its assemblages consist of flake tools rather than large core tools, chiefly side-scrapers, points and toothed pieces struck from prepared cores, and they vary enough from site to site that archaeologists have long argued over whether the differences mark separate groups, different activities, or simply how far a tool had been reworked before it was thrown away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Europe it is the work of Neanderthals, and it goes out of use at about the time they do, while in the Levant assemblages of the same character are associated with early modern humans as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Le_Moustier": "Le Moustier is a group of three superimposed rock shelters in the valley of the Vézère in the Dordogne, southwestern France, cut into limestone cliffs in a region unusually rich in Paleolithic sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavated from the early 20th century, first by Otto Hauser and then by Denis Peyrony, its deep sequence of occupation layers supplied archaeologists with the reference collection of Middle Paleolithic flake tools, and the site's name was taken for the Mousterian industry as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two Neanderthal individuals were recovered there in the early 20th century, among them a well-known adolescent skeleton whose removal and sale to a German museum caused lasting controversy in France.",
"Prehistory": "Prehistory is the long stretch of the human past that came before written records, beginning with the first stone tools about 2.6 million years ago and closing with the appearance of writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it left no documents, it is known almost entirely from material traces such as tools, bones, hearths, dwellings, burials and art, which archaeology reconstructs with help from geology, genetics and the study of ancient climates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has no single end date: writing appeared in Mesopotamia and Egypt by about 3200 BCE but reached other regions thousands of years later, and a people first described in the records of its literate neighbours is said to belong to protohistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Denmark": "Denmark is a Nordic country in northern Europe, made up of the Jutland peninsula, which reaches north from the German border, and an archipelago of some 400 islands, of which Zealand and Funen are the largest. It is low-lying and intensively farmed, with no point much above 170 m (560 feet) and no district far from the sea, and its capital, Copenhagen, stands on the eastern side of Zealand facing Sweden across a narrow strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A constitutional monarchy with a parliamentary government, it forms together with the self-governing territories of the Faroe Islands and Greenland the wider realm known as the Kingdom of Denmark.",
"Christian_Jürgensen_Thomsen": "Christian Jürgensen Thomsen was a Danish antiquarian and museum curator who established the three-age system, the division of prehistory into a Stone Age, a Bronze Age and an Iron Age, an idea he himself called an old one and which several antiquaries had put forward before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Put in charge of Denmark's national collection of antiquities in 1816, he faced a mass of objects that carried no dates and sorted them by the material of their tools and weapons, checking the order against groups of finds recovered together in a single grave or hoard, then arranged the museum so that visitors walked through the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He set the scheme out in the guidebook <i>Ledetraad til Nordisk Oldkyndighed</i> in 1836, which appeared in German the following year and in English in 1848, and it became the framework on which prehistoric chronology was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"National_Museum_of_Denmark": "The National Museum of Denmark, in Copenhagen, is the country's largest museum of cultural history, with holdings that run from the Stone Age to the present and take in ethnographic, classical and coin collections from around the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of a royal commission for the preservation of antiquities set up in 1807, whose collection began in a church loft and was arranged by Christian Jürgensen Thomsen; the antiquities reached the 18th-century Prince's Mansion in 1855, and in 1892 the separate royal collections were gathered into a single National Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among its best-known objects are the Trundholm sun chariot, the Gundestrup cauldron and the Bronze Age burials in hollowed oak coffins, including the young woman known as the Egtved Girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Near_East": "The Near East is a name for the lands of western Asia about the eastern basin of the Mediterranean, usually taken to cover Anatolia, the Levant, Mesopotamia, the Arabian peninsula and Iran, and sometimes extended to Egypt and the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came into use in 19th-century Europe, which ranked the regions of Asia by their distance from itself into a nearer and a farther East, so the name marks a position rather than any shared frontier, culture or language, and its limits have never been firmly drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives today chiefly among archaeologists, historians and biblical scholars, for whom it names the region where farming, cities and writing first appeared, while writing on current affairs generally calls much the same area the Middle East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Europe": "Europe is a continent occupying the western part of the Eurasian landmass, bounded by the Arctic Ocean to the north, the Atlantic to the west and the Mediterranean to the south, and divided from Asia by a conventional line running along the Ural Mountains and the Ural River, the Caspian Sea, the Caucasus and the Black Sea — a boundary of custom rather than of geology, since geologists count Eurasia as one of the six continents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At about 10.2 million km² (3.94 million sq mi) it is the second-smallest of the continents, yet its deeply indented coastline, navigable rivers and mild, well-watered climate have long supported dense settlement, and it holds roughly 745 million people in about 50 countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its outline is broken by unusually large peninsulas and island groups, among them Scandinavia, Iberia, Italy, the Balkans and the British Isles, while much of its land lies in the wide plain that runs east from France into Russia.",
"Asia": "Asia is the largest and most populous continent, covering roughly 44.5 million km² (17.2 million sq mi), close to 30 percent of the world's land, and holding about 4.8 billion people, some 60 percent of humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It fills the eastern and greater part of the Eurasian landmass, which geologists count as a single continent, reaching from the Arctic coast to the equatorial islands of Indonesia and from the Mediterranean and the Urals east to the Pacific, and its terrain runs from the Himalaya and the Tibetan plateau through the Siberian forests and the deserts of Arabia and central Asia to the crowded plains of the Indus, Ganges, Mekong, Yangtze and Yellow rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds both the highest point on land, the summit of Everest, and the lowest, the shore of the Dead Sea, and its river valleys were the setting for several of the earliest farming societies, cities and writing systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Homo": "<i>Homo</i> is the genus of hominins that takes in modern people together with their closest extinct relatives, its name simply the Latin word for 'a human being'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was established by Carl Linnaeus in 1758 for living humans and later widened to fossil forms such as <i>Homo habilis</i>, <i>Homo erectus</i> and <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i>, and the oldest fossil generally assigned to it is a jaw from Ethiopia dated to about 2.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its members are set apart by larger brains relative to body size, smaller teeth and jaws and a long association with stone toolmaking, though how many species the genus holds and where its boundary should be drawn are still argued over, and only <i>Homo sapiens</i> survives today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_Revolution": "The Neolithic Revolution was the shift from hunting and gathering to farming and settled village life, a change named by the archaeologist V. Gordon Childe in 1936 and now understood as a slow transition rather than a sudden one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began around 12,000 years ago and happened independently in at least 11 regions, among them western Asia, China, Mesoamerica, the Andes, New Guinea and eastern North America, each domesticating its own crops and animals over many generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its consequences reached far: permanent settlements, stored surpluses of food, rapid population growth, private property and inherited rank, and the new diseases that came with crowding and close contact with livestock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Fertile_Crescent": "The Fertile Crescent is a curved band of comparatively well-watered land in western Asia that sweeps from the Levantine coast north through southern Anatolia and then southeast along the Tigris and Euphrates to the Gulf, named by the American Egyptologist James Henry Breasted, who put the phrase into print in 1914 and made it a proper name two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rains, rivers and stands of wild grasses and pulses gave it an unusual concentration of species suited to domestication, and wheat, barley, lentils, sheep, goats, cattle and pigs were all brought under human control there, in one of at least eleven separate centres worldwide where domestication began between about 12,000 and 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the earliest known farming villages, walled towns, cities and writing systems appeared there, and its first farmers, in the southern Levant and the Zagros, descended from the local hunter-gatherers rather than arriving from elsewhere, which is why it is often described as a cradle of civilization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sahara": "The Sahara is the largest hot desert on Earth, stretching about 4,800 km (3,000 miles) across northern Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea and covering some 9.5 million km² (3.7 million sq mi), an area close to that of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is not dune but rock and gravel: bare stone plateaus, boulder-strewn plains and dry valleys, with the great sand seas taking up roughly a quarter of the surface and the volcanic massifs of Tibesti and Ahaggar rising above 3,000 m (9,800 feet). Much of it receives under 100 mm (3.9 inches) of rain a year and parts of it go years with none at all, yet between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago a wetter climate filled it with permanent lakes, grassland and the herds of the first Saharan pastoralists, a landscape recorded in the rock paintings of Tassili n'Ajjer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australia": "Australia is a country and a continent in the southern hemisphere, made up of the Australian mainland, the island of Tasmania and many smaller islands, and covering about 7.7 million km² (3.0 million sq mi), which makes it the sixth-largest country in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the driest inhabited continent: deserts and dry scrub fill the interior, so almost all of its roughly 27 million people live along the eastern, southeastern and southwestern coasts, and its long isolation left it with plants and animals found nowhere else, among them the marsupials and the egg-laying monotremes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived there for at least 50,000 years, British colonisation began in 1788, and the separate colonies joined into a single federal nation in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Americas": "The Americas are the landmass of the western hemisphere, taking in North and South America together with Central America and the Caribbean islands, and running from the Arctic almost to Antarctica across roughly 42.5 million km² (16.4 million sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their western edge is a nearly unbroken chain of mountains, from Alaska through the Rockies and the Sierra Madre to the Andes, while great river basins — the Mississippi, the Orinoco, the Amazon and the Paraná — drain the lowlands to the east. People reached them from north-eastern Asia by way of Beringia and had already spread widely through both continents more than 14,000 years ago, going on to develop farming, cities and states independently; the name comes from the navigator Amerigo Vespucci and was first given to the southern landmass, in a book and world map printed at Saint-Dié in Lorraine in 1507.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"North_America": "North America is the continent forming the northern part of the western hemisphere, bounded by the Arctic Ocean, the Atlantic and the Pacific and linked to the southern landmass by the narrow Isthmus of Panama, with an area of about 24.7 million km² (9.54 million sq mi) that makes it the third largest continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west is dominated by the mountain belt of the Cordillera, its northeast by the worn ancient rock of the Canadian Shield, and between them lie the Great Plains, the Great Lakes and the wide basin of the Mississippi and Missouri, while its southern reach narrows into tropical Central America and the arc of the Caribbean islands. Around 600 million people live there today, and long before European ships arrived it supported societies ranging from Arctic hunters to the maize-farming cities of Mesoamerica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"South_America": "South America is the continent forming the southern part of the western hemisphere, joined to the north at the Isthmus of Panama, covering about 17.8 million km² (6.87 million sq mi) and holding roughly 433 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Andes run almost 7,000 km (4,300 miles) down its western side, the longest mountain range on any continent, and east of them lie the Amazon basin with the largest rainforest and the largest river by volume on Earth, the grasslands of the Pampas, the dry plateaus of Patagonia and the Atacama, where some weather stations have never recorded rain. It was one of the few places where farming and complex states arose independently, giving the world the potato, quinoa and the llama and societies such as the Moche, Tiwanaku and the Inca, before conquest by Spain and Portugal in the 16th century reshaped its populations and languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"India": "India is a country in South Asia occupying most of the Indian subcontinent, the seventh largest in the world at about 3.3 million km² (1.3 million sq mi) and, with roughly 1.4 billion people, the most populous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Himalaya wall it off to the north, the plains of the Ganges and the Indus carry much of its farming and its population, the Deccan plateau fills the peninsula between two coastal ranges, and its year turns on the summer monsoon, whose rains make the difference between harvest and drought. It holds the Indus valley cities of the 3rd millennium BCE and the origins of Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism, recognises 22 languages in its constitution, came under British rule during the 18th and 19th centuries, and became an independent federal republic in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Russia": "Russia is the largest country in the world by area, covering about 17.1 million km² (6.60 million sq mi), some 11 percent of all the land on Earth, spanning 11 time zones and reaching from the Baltic across eastern Europe and the whole of northern Asia to the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its roughly 145 million people live in the western fifth, on the East European Plain around Moscow and St Petersburg, while beyond the Ural Mountains lie the marshes and taiga of the West Siberian Plain, the plateaus of central Siberia and Lake Baikal, the deepest lake on Earth. It grew out of the principality of Moscow into an empire that reached the Pacific in the 17th century, formed the core of the Soviet Union between 1922 and 1991, and has since been the Russian Federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"China": "China is a country in East Asia covering about 9.6 million km² (3.7 million sq mi), among the largest in the world, and home to some 1.4 billion people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land falls in steps from west to east: the Tibetan plateau and the Himalaya, then the deserts and basins of the northwest, then the loess uplands and finally the broad alluvial plains where the Yellow River and the Yangtze reach the sea, and it is along those two rivers that farming and population have always concentrated. Its farming villages are among the oldest anywhere, its written records run continuously from about 1200 BCE, and after more than two thousand years of rule by successive dynasties it became a republic in 1912 and the People's Republic of China in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Japan": "Japan is an island country in East Asia, an archipelago of more than 14,000 islands lying off the northeastern coast of the Asian mainland, of which Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shikoku carry almost all of its land and its roughly 124 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About three-quarters of it is mountainous and forested, so cities and farmland crowd onto narrow coastal plains, and because it sits where several tectonic plates meet it has frequent earthquakes and more than a hundred active volcanoes, among them Mount Fuji. A centralised state took shape there from the 7th century, military governments ruled in the emperor's name for much of the period after 1185, contact with Europe was tightly restricted for more than two centuries until the reforms that began in 1868, and it is today a constitutional monarchy with a parliamentary government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Brazil": "Brazil is the largest country in South America and the fifth largest in the world, covering about 8.5 million km² (3.3 million sq mi) and holding more than 200 million people, and it borders every country on the continent except Chile and Ecuador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Amazon basin fills its north with the largest rainforest and river system on Earth, while the interior and the south carry the wooded savanna of the cerrado, the seasonally flooded plains of the Pantanal and a long Atlantic coast where most of its cities lie. Claimed by Portugal in 1500 and built on a plantation economy that brought in more enslaved Africans than any other colony in the Americas, it became independent in 1822 and a republic in 1889, and remains the largest Portuguese-speaking country in the world.",
"Albania": "Albania is a country in southeastern Europe on the western side of the Balkan peninsula, with coasts on the Adriatic and Ionian seas facing Italy across a strait some 72 km (45 miles) wide, and roughly 2.4 million people in an area of 28,748 km² (11,100 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mountains cover about three-quarters of it, rising steeply behind a narrow coastal plain where most of the farmland and the larger towns lie, among them the capital, Tirana. Ruled by the Ottomans for more than four centuries, it became independent in 1912, spent the years from 1944 to 1991 under one of the most isolated communist governments in Europe, and has been a parliamentary republic since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Andorra": "Andorra is a small landlocked country of 468 km² (181 sq mi) high in the eastern Pyrenees, wedged between France and Spain, with about 80,000 inhabitants and its capital at Andorra la Vella, the highest capital city in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely mountain: narrow glacial valleys and pine forest above roughly 900 m (3,000 feet), with the population gathered along the valley floors and the economy resting on winter sports, mountain tourism and cross-border trade. It is a co-principality, a form of government it has kept since a feudal settlement of 1278, in which the office of head of state is held jointly by the president of France and the bishop of Urgell in Catalonia, and its official language is Catalan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Armenia": "Armenia is a landlocked country of 29,743 km² (11,484 sq mi) in the South Caucasus, bordered by Turkey, Georgia, Azerbaijan and Iran, with roughly 3 million people and its capital at Yerevan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is high ground, a rugged volcanic plateau averaging about 1,800 m (5,900 feet) and dotted with extinct cones, with Lake Sevan, one of the largest high-altitude lakes in the world, filling a basin in its east. It has its own branch of the Indo-European family and its own alphabet, devised in the early 5th century, adopted Christianity as a state religion at the beginning of the 4th, and after centuries under Persian, Ottoman and Russian rule was a Soviet republic from 1922 until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Austria": "Austria is a landlocked country of 83,879 km² (32,386 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 9 million people, bordered by eight states and centred on its capital, Vienna, in the northeast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Alps cover about three-fifths of it, so most of the population lives in the lower east and along the Danube, which crosses the country from west to east and carries its main corridor of towns, farmland and traffic. Its ruling house, the Habsburgs, built one of Europe's great dynastic empires from Vienna, which ended with defeat in 1918; the small republic that followed was annexed by Germany in 1938, and since regaining full sovereignty in 1955 the country has been a federal republic committed to neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Azerbaijan": "Azerbaijan is a country of 86,600 km² (33,400 sq mi) in the South Caucasus with roughly 10 million people, lying on the western shore of the Caspian Sea between Russia, Georgia, Armenia, Turkey and Iran, with its capital at Baku and a separate exclave, Nakhchivan, cut off from the rest by Armenian territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greater and Lesser Caucasus ranges enclose a wide central lowland where the Kura river runs to the Caspian, and the country's climate ranges from semi-desert to humid subtropical along the southern coast. Its people speak a Turkic language and are mostly Muslim; the Baku oilfields were among the first in the world to be drilled on a large scale in the 19th century, and the country was a Soviet republic from 1922 until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belarus": "Belarus is a landlocked country of 207,600 km² (80,150 sq mi) in eastern Europe with roughly 9 million people, bordered by Russia, Ukraine, Poland, Lithuania and Latvia, and governed from its capital, Minsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely low and flat, a land of forests, some 11,000 lakes and the great Pripet marshes in the south, with no mountains and its highest ground barely above 340 m (1,100 feet). Its territory belonged in turn to the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Russian Empire, it lost a quarter of its people in the Second World War, and it was a Soviet republic until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belgium": "Belgium is a country of 30,689 km² (11,849 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 11.8 million people, facing the North Sea between the Netherlands, Germany, Luxembourg and France, with its capital at Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land rises gently from flat coastal polders and the densely built plain of the north to the wooded hills of the Ardennes in the southeast, and its position at the mouth of the Scheldt made cities such as Bruges, Ghent and Antwerp among the richest trading centres of medieval and early modern Europe. It is a federal state divided by language, Dutch-speaking in the northern region of Flanders, French-speaking in southern Wallonia and with a small German-speaking community in the east; it broke away from the Netherlands in 1830, industrialised early, and now hosts the main institutions of the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bosnia_and_Herzegovina": "Bosnia and Herzegovina is a country of 51,209 km² (19,772 sq mi) in the western Balkans with roughly 3.2 million people, almost landlocked apart from a 20 km (12 miles) strip of Adriatic coast at Neum, and governed from Sarajevo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is rugged upland: the wooded Dinaric ranges and river gorges of Bosnia in the north and centre, and the drier limestone karst of Herzegovina in the south, where sinkholes and underground rivers replace surface streams. It passed from a medieval kingdom to more than four centuries of Ottoman rule, then to Austria-Hungary in 1878, became a republic of Yugoslavia, and after a war from 1992 to 1995 was reorganised by the Dayton Agreement into two entities under a shared central government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bulgaria": "Bulgaria is a country of 110,994 km² (42,855 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 6.4 million people, holding the eastern Balkans between the Danube and Greece and Turkey, with a Black Sea coast on the east and its capital at Sofia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balkan Mountains run across it from west to east, dividing the northern plain along the Danube from the Thracian lowland and the Rhodope and Rila massifs of the south, which carry the highest summits in the peninsula. A Bulgarian state was founded in 681, its monastic schools of the 9th and 10th centuries developed the Cyrillic alphabet later used across much of the Slavic world, and after nearly five centuries of Ottoman rule the country regained independence in stages from 1878, spent 1946 to 1989 under communist government, and joined the European Union in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Croatia": "Croatia is a country of 56,594 km² (21,851 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 3.9 million people, curving from the plains of the Danube basin around the Dinaric mountains to a long Adriatic coast, with its capital at Zagreb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That coast is its defining feature: more than 1,000 islands and a shoreline of drowned limestone ridges, backed by bare karst uplands where rain vanishes underground, while the fertile Pannonian lowland in the northeast grows most of the country's grain. A medieval kingdom, it entered a union with Hungary in 1102 and later came under Habsburg rule, joined Yugoslavia after 1918, declared independence in 1991 and fought a war until 1995, and joined the European Union in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cyprus": "Cyprus is an island country of 9,251 km² (3,572 sq mi) in the eastern Mediterranean with roughly 1.3 million people, lying south of Turkey and west of Syria, with its capital at Nicosia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two mountain ranges frame it, the volcanic Troodos massif in the centre and south and the long limestone ridge of the Kyrenia range along the north coast, with a hot dry plain between them; its copper mines were worked from the Bronze Age and gave the metal its name in several languages. Greek and Turkish speaking communities have long shared the island, which passed through Byzantine, Frankish, Venetian, Ottoman and British rule before independence in 1960, and it has been divided since 1974 between the internationally recognised republic in the south and a Turkish Cypriot administration in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Czech_Republic": "The Czech Republic is a landlocked country of 78,871 km² (30,452 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 10.9 million people, bordered by Germany, Poland, Slovakia and Austria, with its capital at Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is made up of the historic lands of Bohemia, a basin ringed by wooded mountains and drained by the Vltava and the Elbe, together with Moravia and a corner of Silesia in the east, which open toward the Danube plain. The Kingdom of Bohemia was a major power of the Holy Roman Empire and its capital a seat of emperors, the lands passed to the Habsburgs in 1526, joined the new state of Czechoslovakia in 1918, and became a separate republic when that country divided peacefully at the start of 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Estonia": "Estonia is a country of 45,339 km² (17,505 sq mi) on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 1.4 million people, bordered by Latvia and Russia and separated from Finland by a gulf about 80 km (50 miles) wide, with its capital at Tallinn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is low, forested and dotted with bogs and more than 2,000 islands, and its highest hill reaches only 318 m (1,040 feet); long winters and a maritime climate shape a landscape of pine, spruce and shallow coastal water. Its language belongs to the Finnic family rather than to those of its neighbours, its towns were ruled in turn by Danish, German, Swedish and Russian powers, and after independence in 1918, Soviet annexation in 1940 and the restoration of statehood in 1991 it joined both the European Union and NATO in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Finland": "Finland is a country of 338,455 km² (130,678 sq mi) in northern Europe with roughly 5.6 million people, bordered by Sweden, Norway and Russia and facing the Baltic to the south and west, with its capital at Helsinki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers about three-quarters of it and some 188,000 lakes fill hollows left by the ice sheets, so most of the land is a low mosaic of water, bog and pine, thinning northward into the fells of Lapland where the sun stays up for weeks in midsummer. Its language belongs to the Finnic family, it formed the eastern half of the Swedish realm until 1809 and then an autonomous grand duchy under the Russian tsars, declared independence in 1917, defended it in the wars of 1939 to 1944, joined the European Union in 1995 and NATO in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Germany": "Germany is a country of 357,596 km² (138,069 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 84 million people, the most populous member of the European Union, bordered by nine states and governed from its capital, Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land rises in three broad steps from the flat, sandy plain along the North Sea and Baltic coasts, through the wooded central uplands and river valleys of the Rhine, Elbe, Weser and Main, to the Alpine foreland and the mountains of the far south. Its territory was for centuries a patchwork of principalities within the Holy Roman Empire, was united into a single state under Prussian leadership in 1871, was divided between rival states from 1949 after defeat in the Second World War, and was reunified in 1990.",
"Hungary": "Hungary is a landlocked country of 93,030 km² (35,920 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 9.6 million people, lying in the Carpathian Basin between Slovakia, Ukraine, Romania, Serbia, Croatia, Slovenia and Austria, with its capital at Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is flat and open, the Great Hungarian Plain stretching east of the Danube toward the Tisza, with low hills to the north and west and Lake Balaton, the largest lake in central Europe, in the country's western half. Its language is Uralic and unrelated to those of its neighbours, a Christian kingdom was established there in the year 1000, the land was fought over by Ottomans and Habsburgs, lost about two-thirds of its territory in the settlement that followed the First World War, and joined the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iceland": "Iceland is an island country of 103,000 km² (39,800 sq mi) in the North Atlantic just south of the Arctic Circle, with roughly 390,000 people, almost all of them around the coast and more than half in and near the capital, Reykjavík.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It straddles the boundary between two drifting plates of the Earth's crust, which makes it one of the most volcanically active places on the planet, and its interior is an uninhabited highland of lava fields, glaciers covering about a tenth of the country, hot springs and meltwater rivers. Norse settlers arrived in the late 9th century and founded an assembly, the Althing, in 930, one of the oldest surviving parliaments; the island later came under Norwegian and then Danish rule and became a fully independent republic in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ireland": "Ireland is a country of 70,273 km² (27,133 sq mi) with roughly 5.4 million people occupying about five-sixths of the island of the same name in the north Atlantic, west of Great Britain, with its capital at Dublin; the remaining northeast of the island, Northern Ireland, is part of the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring of coastal hills and mountains encloses a low, damp central plain of pasture, lakes and peat bog, and the mild wet climate carried by Atlantic winds keeps grass growing nearly year-round, which has long made livestock the mainstay of its farming. Christianised in the 5th century, it became a centre of monastic learning, came under English and later British control from the 12th century onward, lost about a quarter of its people to death and emigration in the famine of 1845 to 1852, won independence for the greater part of the island in 1922 and joined what is now the European Union in 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Italy": "Italy is a country of 301,340 km² (116,350 sq mi) in southern Europe with roughly 59 million people, made up of a long boot-shaped peninsula reaching into the Mediterranean together with Sicily, Sardinia and many smaller islands, and governed from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Alps close it off in the north above the broad plain of the river Po, the Apennines run the length of the peninsula as a rocky spine, and the country sits on an active plate boundary that has given it earthquakes and the volcanoes Vesuvius, Etna and Stromboli. From the peninsula the Roman state grew into an empire ruling the whole Mediterranean; after its fall the land was divided for centuries among city-states, kingdoms and foreign powers, produced the Renaissance in its wealthy trading cities, was united into a single kingdom in 1861 and became a republic in 1946.",
"Kosovo": "Kosovo is a landlocked territory of 10,887 km² (4,203 sq mi) in the western Balkans with roughly 1.6 million people, bordered by Serbia, North Macedonia, Albania and Montenegro, with its capital at Pristina. It is a high basin, mostly between 400 and 700 m (1,300 to 2,300 feet), ringed by mountains that rise above 2,500 m (8,200 feet) along its southern and western rims, and its population is largely Albanian-speaking with a Serb minority concentrated in the north and in scattered enclaves. Held by medieval Serbian rulers and then for centuries by the Ottomans, it was governed as an autonomous province of Serbia within Yugoslavia, was the scene of an armed conflict and a NATO intervention in 1998 and 1999, and declared independence in 2008, a step recognised by many states but not by Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Latvia": "Latvia is a country of 64,589 km² (24,938 sq mi) on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 1.9 million people, lying between Estonia, Russia, Belarus and Lithuania, with its capital and largest port at Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is low and heavily wooded, with forest over about half its area, long sandy beaches along the Gulf of Riga, and the Daugava river running across it from the east to reach the sea at the capital. Its language is one of only two surviving Baltic tongues, its towns were founded and long dominated by German crusading orders and merchants, and after periods under Polish, Swedish and Russian rule it was independent from 1918 to 1940, annexed by the Soviet Union, restored to statehood in 1991 and joined the European Union and NATO in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Liechtenstein": "Liechtenstein is a landlocked microstate of 160 km² (62 sq mi) in the Alps between Switzerland and Austria, with about 40,000 inhabitants and its capital at Vaduz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It occupies the eastern side of the upper Rhine valley, a narrow strip of flat riverside land backed by steep mountain slopes that rise to more than 2,500 m (8,200 feet), and its small population is dense in the valley villages while the heights are given over to pasture and forest. It is a constitutional monarchy ruled by a prince, took its name and its independence from a princely family that bought two small lordships in the early 18th century, and is closely tied to Switzerland, with which it shares a currency, a customs union and its diplomatic representation abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lithuania": "Lithuania is a country of 65,300 km² (25,200 sq mi) on the southeastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 2.9 million people, bordered by Latvia, Belarus, Poland and the Russian region of Kaliningrad, with its capital at Vilnius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is gently rolling lowland scattered with some 3,000 lakes and drained by the Nemunas river, and its short coast is closed off by the Curonian Spit, a narrow dune barrier nearly 100 km (62 miles) long. Its language is the most conservative surviving Indo-European tongue, valued by linguists for its archaic forms; the Grand Duchy of Lithuania was one of the largest states in 14th-century Europe and later joined Poland in a commonwealth, the country was partitioned away in 1795, independent between the world wars, annexed by the Soviet Union in 1940 and the first Soviet republic to declare the restoration of its independence, in 1990.",
"Luxembourg": "Luxembourg is a landlocked country of 2,586 km² (998 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 670,000 people, wedged between Belgium, Germany and France, with its capital of the same name built above deep river gorges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wooded hills and narrow valleys of the Ardennes fill its northern third, while the lower, more fertile Gutland in the south carries most of the population, the farmland and the vineyards along the Moselle. It is a grand duchy, the only one left in the world, its people commonly use Luxembourgish, French and German side by side, and it was one of the six founding members of the communities that grew into the European Union, several of whose institutions it hosts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Malta": "Malta is an island country of 316 km² (122 sq mi) in the central Mediterranean about 80 km (50 miles) south of Sicily, with roughly 550,000 people, which makes it one of the most densely populated states in the world; its capital is Valletta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archipelago is a low limestone platform with almost no rivers or lakes, tilted so that sheer cliffs face the southwest and gentle terraced slopes and deep natural harbours the northeast, and it depends on desalination for most of its fresh water. Its language is Semitic in origin but written in the Latin alphabet and full of Italian and English borrowings; its position on the sea routes between Europe and Africa brought it Phoenician, Roman, Arab, Norman, Habsburg, French and British rulers, along with the Knights of St John from 1530, and it became independent in 1964 and joined the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Moldova": "Moldova is a landlocked country of 33,846 km² (13,068 sq mi) in eastern Europe with roughly 2.4 million people, lying between Romania and Ukraine, with its capital at Chișinău.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a land of low rolling hills and deep black soil between the Prut and Dniester rivers, among the most fertile ground in Europe, and its farms and hillside vineyards make wine and fruit the mainstay of its exports. Known historically as Bessarabia, it passed from the principality of Moldavia to the Russian Empire in 1812, to Romania after 1918 and to the Soviet Union in 1940, became independent in 1991, and a strip of land east of the Dniester has been outside the government's control since a brief conflict in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Monaco": "Monaco is a sovereign city-state of about 2 km² (1 sq mi) on the Mediterranean coast of southern Europe, surrounded on land by France and lying a few kilometres from the Italian border, and it is the second-smallest country in the world after the Vatican.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly 39,000 people live there, packed onto a strip of steep coast between the sea and the maritime Alps, which makes it the most densely populated state on Earth; land reclaimed from the sea has added to its area over the past century. It has been ruled by the Grimaldi family since the end of the 13th century, is a constitutional monarchy under a prince, and lives on banking, tourism and property, drawing residents with its low personal taxation and international attention with its casino and its street motor race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Montenegro": "Montenegro is a country of 13,812 km² (5,333 sq mi) in the western Balkans with about 620,000 people, holding a stretch of Adriatic coast between Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Serbia, Kosovo and Albania, with its capital at Podgorica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a narrow shore of bays and beaches, including the deeply indented Bay of Kotor, the land climbs abruptly into bare limestone mountains cut by the Tara canyon and holding Durmitor's glacial peaks, while Lake Skadar on the Albanian border is the largest lake in southern Europe. Long defended as a mountain principality under its prince-bishops, it became a kingdom in 1910, joined Yugoslavia after the First World War, remained in a union with Serbia after that state broke up, and voted for independence in a referendum in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Netherlands": "The Netherlands is a country of 41,850 km² (16,160 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 18 million people, facing the North Sea between Belgium and Germany, with Amsterdam as its capital and the government seated at The Hague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is exceptionally flat and low: about a quarter of the land lies below sea level and would flood without an old and constantly maintained system of dunes, dikes, pumps and drained polders, and the country sits on the delta where the Rhine, Meuse and Scheldt reach the sea. Its merchant republic of the 17th century built a trading empire reaching Asia, the Americas and southern Africa and supported a celebrated age of painting and science; the modern kingdom dates from 1815, and the country was one of the six founding members of what became the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"North_Macedonia": "North Macedonia is a landlocked country of 25,713 km² (9,928 sq mi) in the southern Balkans with roughly 1.8 million people, bordered by Serbia, Kosovo, Albania, Greece and Bulgaria, with its capital at Skopje.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains cover most of it and the Vardar river cuts a broad valley through the middle from northwest to southeast, carrying the main road and rail route between central Europe and the Aegean, while Lake Ohrid on the Albanian border is among the oldest and deepest lakes in Europe. Its Slavic-speaking majority lives alongside a large Albanian minority; the territory was ruled by the Ottomans for five centuries, became a republic of Yugoslavia, gained independence in 1991, and adopted its present name in 2019 under an agreement with Greece that opened the way to NATO membership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Norway": "Norway is a country of about 324,000 km² (125,000 sq mi) on the mainland of northern Europe with roughly 5.5 million people, occupying the western side of the Scandinavian peninsula from the North Sea to the Arctic, with its capital at Oslo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains and high plateau cover most of it and glaciers of the last ice age carved the deep flooded valleys of its coast, giving it a shoreline that measures tens of thousands of kilometres once its fjords and offshore islands are counted, while the warm Atlantic current keeps its harbours ice-free far into the north. Seafarers from its fjords ranged across the north Atlantic in the Viking age, the country was joined to Denmark and then to Sweden before regaining full independence in 1905, and since oil and gas were found offshore in 1969 it has placed much of the revenue in one of the world's largest public investment funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Poland": "Poland is a country of 312,696 km² (120,733 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 37 million people, stretching from a Baltic coast of dunes and lagoons in the north to the Carpathian and Sudeten mountains along its southern border, with its capital at Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is open lowland plain crossed from south to north by the Vistula and the Oder, ground that has made the country rich in farmland and coal but has also left it without natural defences on its eastern and western approaches. A Christian kingdom was founded there in 966, it joined Lithuania in a commonwealth that was the largest state in 16th-century Europe, was partitioned out of existence by its neighbours in 1795, returned to the map in 1918, lost about a fifth of its people in the Second World War and had its borders moved west afterwards, and joined the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Portugal": "Portugal is a country of 92,230 km² (35,610 sq mi) in southwestern Europe with roughly 10.5 million people, occupying the western edge of the Iberian peninsula with a long Atlantic coast, bordered on land only by Spain, and including the Atlantic island groups of the Azores and Madeira; its capital is Lisbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The river Tagus divides it in two: a green, hilly and rainier north of small farms and terraced vineyards, and a hotter, flatter south of wide plains, cork oak and olive groves running down to the beaches of the Algarve. An independent kingdom from 1143, with borders little changed since the 13th century, it sent ships down the African coast and across the Indian and Atlantic oceans in the 15th and 16th centuries and built an empire that lasted into the 1970s; it became a republic in 1910, returned to democratic government in 1974 and joined the European Union in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Romania": "Romania is a country of 238,398 km² (92,046 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 19 million people, lying where the lower Danube meets the Black Sea between Ukraine, Moldova, Bulgaria, Serbia and Hungary, with its capital at Bucharest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Carpathian mountains sweep through it in a great arc, enclosing the raised plateau of Transylvania and separating it from the plains of Wallachia and Moldavia, and the Danube ends its course here in a broad delta of reed marsh and channels that is one of the largest wetlands in Europe. Its language is Romance, descended from the Latin of the Roman province of Dacia and unusual among its Slavic and Hungarian-speaking neighbours; the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia united in 1859, the kingdom that followed doubled in size after the First World War, and after a communist government fell in the revolution of 1989 the country joined the European Union in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"San_Marino": "San Marino is a landlocked microstate of 61 km² (24 sq mi) enclosed entirely by Italy, near the Adriatic coast in the country's northeast, with about 34,000 inhabitants and its capital in the town of the same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its territory is a cluster of hills dominated by Monte Titano, a limestone ridge rising to 739 m (2,420 feet) whose three fortified towers stand above the old town and appear on the state's flag, with the rest of the land falling away in vineyards and small settlements. It presents itself as the oldest surviving republic in the world, tracing its foundation by tradition to the year 301, keeps a constitution based on statutes of 1600, and is governed by two captains regent elected together for terms of six months.",
"Serbia": "Serbia is a landlocked country in the central Balkans with roughly 6.6 million people, covering 77,589 km² (29,957 sq mi), or 88,499 km² (34,170 sq mi) counting Kosovo, whose separate independence it does not accept, with its capital at Belgrade where the Sava joins the Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The flat and fertile Pannonian plain of Vojvodina fills the north, while the land south of the two rivers rises into hills and wooded mountains cut by the valleys of the Morava, the country's main north-south corridor since antiquity. A medieval kingdom that grew into an empire in the 14th century, it fell under Ottoman rule for some four hundred years, won autonomy from 1815 and full independence in 1878, formed the core of the Yugoslav state created in 1918, and became a separate republic again when its union with Montenegro ended in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Slovakia": "Slovakia is a landlocked country of 49,035 km² (18,933 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 5.4 million people, bordered by Poland, Ukraine, Hungary, Austria and the Czech Republic, with its capital at Bratislava on the Danube at the western edge of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Carpathian mountains fill its north and centre, rising in the High Tatras to peaks above 2,600 m (8,500 feet) and cut by wooded valleys and caves, while the south opens into the lowland plain along the Danube where most of the farmland and the larger towns are found. Its lands belonged to the Kingdom of Hungary for about a thousand years, joined the Czechs in the new state of Czechoslovakia in 1918, became a separate republic when that country divided at the start of 1993, and joined the European Union in 2004 and the euro in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Slovenia": "Slovenia is a country of 20,271 km² (7,827 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 2.1 million people, meeting the Alps in the north, the Pannonian plain in the east, the Dinaric uplands in the south and a 47 km (29 miles) strip of Adriatic coast in the southwest, with its capital at Ljubljana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers about three-fifths of it, more than almost anywhere else in Europe, and its limestone southwest is the region that gave the word karst to geography, a landscape of sinkholes, disappearing rivers and long cave systems. Its Slavic-speaking population lived for centuries under Habsburg rule, joined Yugoslavia after 1918, declared independence in 1991 after a conflict of only ten days, and joined the European Union in 2004 and the euro in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spain": "Spain is a country of 505,990 km² (195,360 sq mi) in southwestern Europe with roughly 48 million people, filling most of the Iberian peninsula together with the Balearic Islands in the Mediterranean, the Canary Islands off northwest Africa and two cities on the African coast, with its capital at Madrid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of it is high: a broad central plateau, the Meseta, sits above 600 m (2,000 feet) and is ringed and crossed by mountain ranges, with the Pyrenees closing the French border in the north and the Sierra Nevada rising in the far south, which makes it one of the driest and most mountainous countries in Europe. Roman and then Visigothic rule was followed by nearly eight centuries in which Muslim and Christian states shared the peninsula, the crowns of Castile and Aragon were joined at the end of the 15th century, Spain built a global empire in the Americas and the Pacific, and after a civil war and a long dictatorship it returned to democracy in the late 1970s and joined the European Union in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sweden": "Sweden is a country of 450,295 km² (173,860 sq mi) in northern Europe with roughly 10.6 million people, occupying the eastern side of the Scandinavian peninsula along the Baltic Sea, bordered by Norway and Finland, with its capital at Stockholm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers about two-thirds of it and roughly 100,000 lakes fill the hollows the ice sheets left behind, and the country stretches so far from south to north that its farmland and cities sit in the milder south while the far north lies inside the Arctic Circle. It was a great military power around the Baltic in the 17th century, lost that position by 1721, stayed out of both world wars while building an extensive welfare state in the 20th century, joined the European Union in 1995 and, after two centuries without formal alliances, joined NATO in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Switzerland": "Switzerland is a landlocked country of 41,291 km² (15,943 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 8.9 million people, bordered by Germany, France, Italy, Austria and Liechtenstein, with its federal seat at Bern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Alps cover some three-fifths of it and the Jura ranges another tenth, so nearly everyone lives on the plateau between them, and the high country holds the sources of the Rhine and the Rhône along with glaciers that feed much of western Europe's water. It has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh, traces its confederation to alliances among mountain communities in the late Middle Ages, has kept a policy of armed neutrality since 1815, decides many questions by direct popular vote, and has stayed outside the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Turkey": "Turkey is a country of 783,562 km² (302,535 sq mi) with roughly 85 million people, spread across two continents: the great peninsula of Anatolia in western Asia and a smaller corner of southeastern Europe, divided by the Bosporus and Dardanelles straits, with its capital at Ankara and its largest city, Istanbul, on the straits themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is a high interior plateau ringed by mountains that rise above 5,000 m (16,000 feet) in the east, it lies on major fault lines and suffers severe earthquakes, and its rivers include the headwaters of both the Tigris and the Euphrates. Its land carries some of the earliest farming villages and towns known, was ruled in turn by Hittites, Greeks, Romans and the Byzantine Empire, became the centre of the Ottoman Empire from about 1300 until 1922, and was reorganised as a secular republic in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ukraine": "Ukraine is a country of 603,550 km² (233,030 sq mi) in eastern Europe, the largest that lies wholly within the continent, with a population recorded at about 41 million before 2022 and much reduced since by war and displacement, and its capital at Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mostly flat: open steppe and rolling farmland on some of the deepest black soil in the world, drained by the Dnieper flowing south to the Black Sea, with the Carpathian mountains in the far west and the Crimean peninsula in the south. The medieval state of Kyivan Rus grew up along its rivers, its lands were later divided between Poland, the Russian Empire and Habsburg rule, it lost millions to a man-made famine in 1932 and 1933 as a Soviet republic, became independent in 1991, and has been at war since Russia annexed Crimea in 2014 and invaded on a large scale in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_Kingdom": "The United Kingdom is an island country of 243,610 km² (94,058 sq mi) off the northwestern coast of mainland Europe with roughly 68 million people, made up of the island of Great Britain, the northeastern part of the island of Ireland and many smaller islands, with its capital at London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a union of four constituent countries, England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, whose landscapes run from the low, farmed southeast through the uplands of Wales and the Pennines to the mountains and sea lochs of the Scottish Highlands, all under a mild, wet climate carried in from the Atlantic. The crowns of England and Scotland were joined in 1603 and their parliaments in 1707, Ireland was added in 1801 and mostly left in 1922; the country was the first to industrialise, ruled the largest empire in history at its height, and left the European Union in 2020.",
"Vatican_City": "Vatican City is the smallest sovereign state in the world, a walled enclave of about 0.44 km² (0 sq mi) inside the city of Rome with a population of only a few hundred, and it serves as the seat of the government of the Roman Catholic Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its grounds hold St Peter's Basilica and its square, the Apostolic Palace, the Sistine Chapel with Michelangelo's ceiling, formal gardens covering more than half the territory, and museums and an archive holding one of the richest collections of art and documents anywhere. Popes ruled a much larger block of territory across central Italy for more than a thousand years until it was absorbed into the new Italian kingdom in 1870; the present state was created by a treaty with Italy in 1929 and is governed by the pope, who holds legislative, executive and judicial authority within its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Afghanistan": "Afghanistan is a landlocked country of 652,867 km² (252,073 sq mi) in south-central Asia with roughly 44 million people, bordered by Pakistan, Iran, Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and China, with its capital at Kabul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hindu Kush runs through it from northeast to southwest, so about three-quarters of the land is mountain or high plateau, with irrigated valleys and the fertile plains of the north and the Helmand basin holding most of the farming and the population. Its passes have carried armies and caravans between Central Asia, Iran and the Indian subcontinent for thousands of years; a unified Afghan state dates from 1747, it resisted British campaigns in the 19th and early 20th centuries, and it has seen almost continuous conflict since the Soviet invasion of 1979, with the Taliban returning to power in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bahrain": "Bahrain is an island country of 786 km² (303 sq mi) in the Persian Gulf with roughly 1.6 million people, lying off the eastern coast of the Arabian peninsula and joined to Saudi Arabia since 1986 by a causeway 25 km (16 miles) long, with its capital at Manama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is an archipelago of some 50 low, mostly barren islands built of limestone, with almost no rainfall and no permanent rivers, though freshwater springs rising through the seabed made it habitable and once supported palm groves along its northern shore. Its position on the sea route between Mesopotamia and the Indus made it the centre of the ancient trading civilisation of Dilmun, and it lived on pearl diving until oil was struck in 1932, the first commercial find on the Arabian side of the Gulf; it was under British protection until independence in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bangladesh": "Bangladesh is a country of 148,460 km² (57,321 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 171 million people, almost surrounded by India with a short border with Myanmar and a coast on the Bay of Bengal, and governed from Dhaka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly all of it lies on the delta where the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers meet the sea, the largest river delta in the world, which gives it exceptionally fertile silt and three rice harvests a year but leaves much of the country only a few metres above sea level and exposed to floods and cyclones. The region of Bengal was a wealthy centre of textile production under the Mughals and then under British rule, was partitioned in 1947 so that its eastern half became East Pakistan, and became independent as Bangladesh after a war in 1971.",
"Bhutan": "Bhutan is a landlocked country of 38,394 km² (14,824 sq mi) in the eastern Himalaya with about 790,000 people, wedged between China and India, with its capital at Thimphu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land climbs within a short distance from subtropical plains along the southern border, through temperate valleys where most of the towns and farms lie, to glaciated peaks above 7,000 m (23,000 feet) on the northern frontier, and forest still covers more than two-thirds of it, a proportion the constitution requires never to fall below three-fifths. Vajrayana Buddhism shapes its law, architecture and calendar, a monarchy was established in 1907 and became a constitutional one in 2008, and the government has long measured its progress by a index it calls gross national happiness rather than by output alone.",
"Brunei": "Brunei is a small country of 5,765 km² (2,226 sq mi) on the northern coast of the island of Borneo in Southeast Asia, with about 460,000 people and its capital at Bandar Seri Begawan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is surrounded on land by the Malaysian state of Sarawak, which also splits it into two separate parts, and tropical rainforest still covers roughly two-thirds of its territory, one of the highest proportions left anywhere in the region. Its sultanate once controlled much of Borneo and the southern Philippines, shrank to its present size during the 19th century and became a British protectorate in 1888, regained full independence in 1984, and lives largely on the oil and natural gas found off its coast since 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cambodia": "Cambodia is a country of 181,035 km² (69,898 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 18 million people, bordered by Thailand, Laos and Vietnam with a coast on the Gulf of Thailand, and governed from Phnom Penh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is a low basin around the Tonlé Sap, a lake whose outflowing river reverses direction each monsoon so that the lake swells several times over, flooding the surrounding forest and producing one of the richest inland fisheries in the world. The Khmer Empire ruled much of the region from the 9th to the 15th century and left the temple city of Angkor, the country was a French protectorate from 1863 and independent from 1953, and it lost perhaps a quarter of its population under the Khmer Rouge government of 1975 to 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Indonesia": "Indonesia is a country of 1,910,931 km² (737,814 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 280 million people, the fourth most populous in the world and the largest archipelago, made up of some 17,000 islands strung along the equator between the Indian and Pacific oceans, with its capital at Jakarta on Java.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits where several tectonic plates grind together, giving it about 130 active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes and tsunamis, while its rainforests on Sumatra, Borneo and New Guinea hold some of the greatest concentrations of plant and animal species on Earth. Its islands were the source of the spices that drew traders from India, China, Arabia and later Europe, most of the archipelago was ruled by the Netherlands as the Dutch East Indies, and independence was proclaimed in 1945 and recognised in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iran": "Iran is a country of 1,648,195 km² (636,371 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 92 million people, bordered by Iraq, Turkey, the Caucasus states, Turkmenistan, Afghanistan and Pakistan, with coasts on the Caspian Sea and the Persian Gulf and its capital at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its heart is a high, dry interior plateau ringed by mountains, the Zagros along the west and the Alborz across the north, which trap most of the rain and leave two great salt deserts in the middle, so settlement has always clustered in oasis cities and mountain valleys fed by underground channels. Persian empires ruled from here from the 6th century BCE onward, Islam arrived in the 7th century CE, the Safavids made the country a Shia state from 1501, and a revolution in 1979 replaced the monarchy with an Islamic republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iraq": "Iraq is a country of 435,052 km² (167,974 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 46 million people, bordered by Turkey, Iran, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Jordan and Syria with a narrow outlet to the Persian Gulf, and governed from Baghdad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tigris and Euphrates run its whole length and join near the head of the Gulf, watering a broad alluvial plain that has been farmed by irrigation for thousands of years, while mountains rise in the Kurdish north and desert stretches across the west and southwest. This is the land of ancient Mesopotamia, where some of the earliest cities, writing systems and law codes appeared; Baghdad was founded in 762 and became the capital of the Abbasid caliphate, the country was under Ottoman and then British control before becoming a kingdom in 1932 and a republic in 1958, and it has been shaped since by the wars of 1980, 1991 and 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Israel": "Israel is a country of about 22,000 km² (8,500 sq mi) at the eastern end of the Mediterranean with roughly 10 million people, bordered by Lebanon, Syria, Jordan and Egypt and by the Palestinian territories, with a small outlet to the Red Sea at Eilat; its government sits in Jerusalem, a status most other states do not recognise, and Tel Aviv is its main commercial centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a short distance the land runs from a fertile coastal plain through central highlands to the Jordan Rift Valley, which holds the Sea of Galilee and the Dead Sea, the lowest exposed land surface on Earth, while the arid Negev fills the southern half of the country. It was established in 1948 in the aftermath of the British Mandate over Palestine and the Nazi genocide of European Jews, has fought several wars with neighbouring states, and has occupied the West Bank and other territory since 1967, a situation that remains the central issue of the wider conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jordan": "Jordan is a country of 89,318 km² (34,486 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 12 million people, bordered by Syria, Iraq, Saudi Arabia, Israel and the Palestinian territories, with a single short outlet to the sea at Aqaba on the Red Sea, and governed from Amman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is arid plateau and desert, but along its western edge the ground drops into the Jordan Rift Valley, where the river Jordan runs down to the Dead Sea, and rainfall on the highlands above supports the farmland and nearly all of the population. It holds the rock-cut city of Petra, capital of the Nabataean kingdom, was part of the Ottoman Empire until 1918 and then the British-administered Emirate of Transjordan, became fully independent in 1946, and has since taken in large numbers of refugees from neighbouring conflicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kazakhstan": "Kazakhstan is a country of 2,724,900 km² (1,052,100 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 21 million people, the largest landlocked state in the world and the ninth largest of any kind, bordered by Russia, China, Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan, with a long shore on the Caspian Sea and its capital at Astana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is open steppe and semi-desert, thinly settled, with mountains only along the southeastern rim; diversion of its rivers for irrigation in the Soviet period shrank the Aral Sea to a fraction of its former size, one of the severest environmental losses of the 20th century. Its Turkic-speaking population was largely nomadic and pastoral until the 20th century, the region was absorbed by the Russian Empire in the 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and it gained independence in 1991; the Baikonur spaceport on its steppe has launched crewed missions since 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kuwait": "Kuwait is a country of 17,818 km² (6,880 sq mi) at the head of the Persian Gulf with roughly 4.9 million people, bordered by Iraq and Saudi Arabia, and governed from Kuwait City on a deep natural harbour that gave the state its start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely flat desert with no rivers or lakes and very little rainfall, so nearly all its drinking water is distilled from the sea, and summer temperatures on its gravel plains are among the highest recorded anywhere. A trading and pearling port under the ruling Al Sabah family from the 18th century, it came under British protection in 1899, struck oil in 1938 and became one of the wealthiest states per head in the world, gained independence in 1961, and was invaded and occupied by Iraq in 1990 before being freed by an international coalition the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kyrgyzstan": "Kyrgyzstan is a landlocked country of 199,951 km² (77,201 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 7 million people, bordered by Kazakhstan, China, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan, with its capital at Bishkek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tien Shan ranges cover almost all of it and more than nine-tenths of the land lies above 1,500 m (4,900 feet), so life concentrates in a few valleys and around Issyk-Kul, a large mountain lake that never freezes because of its depth and slight saltiness. Its people were traditionally nomadic herders whose summers were spent on high pasture, the region passed to the Russian Empire in the 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and the country has been independent since 1991, with a mountain economy resting on livestock, gold mining and hydroelectric power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Laos": "Laos is a landlocked country of 236,800 km² (91,430 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 7.7 million people, bordered by China, Vietnam, Cambodia, Thailand and Myanmar, with its capital at Vientiane on the Mekong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains and forested plateaus cover most of it, and the Mekong runs the length of its western border, providing the lowland strips where most farming and settlement are found and, through a chain of dams, much of the electricity it sells to its neighbours. The kingdom of Lan Xang ruled here from 1353, the country became part of French Indochina and gained independence in 1953, it was bombed heavily during the Vietnam War, and a communist government has held power since 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lebanon": "Lebanon is a country of 10,452 km² (4,036 sq mi) on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean with roughly 5.5 million people, bordered by Syria and Israel, with its capital at Beirut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two mountain ranges run the length of the country parallel to the coast, enclosing the fertile Bekaa valley between them, and the snow that falls on the higher ridges gives it more water than most of its neighbours and once fed the cedar forests that made it famous in antiquity. Its coastal cities were the harbours of the Phoenicians, who carried an alphabet across the Mediterranean; the country was part of the Ottoman Empire, came under French mandate after 1918, became independent in 1943 with a system that divides state offices among religious communities, and endured a civil war from 1975 to 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Malaysia": "Malaysia is a country of 330,803 km² (127,724 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 34 million people, made up of two parts about 640 km (400 miles) apart across the South China Sea: the southern half of the Malay peninsula, and the northern edge of the island of Borneo, with its capital at Kuala Lumpur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both parts are hot and wet the year round, with mountain spines running down their centres and rainforest that is among the oldest on Earth, though much of the lowland has been cleared for rubber and oil palm. The port of Melaka made the peninsula a hinge of the trade between the Indian Ocean and China, Portuguese, Dutch and British powers ruled in turn from 1511, the peninsula became independent in 1957, the wider federation was formed in 1963, and Singapore left it in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Maldives": "The Maldives is an island country of about 300 km² (100 sq mi) of land in the Indian Ocean southwest of India, with roughly 520,000 people and its capital at Malé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It consists of some 1,190 coral islands grouped into a double chain of atolls spread over 90,000 km² (30,000 sq mi) of sea, and it is the lowest-lying country in the world, with an average elevation of about 1.5 m (4 ft 11 in), which makes rising sea levels a direct threat to its existence. Buddhist for many centuries before converting to Islam in the 12th century, it was a sultanate that came under Portuguese, Dutch and finally British influence, became independent in 1965, and now depends heavily on tourism and fishing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mongolia": "Mongolia is a landlocked country of 1,564,116 km² (603,908 sq mi) in East Asia with roughly 3.5 million people, wedged between Russia and China, which makes it the most thinly peopled sovereign state in the world, with its capital at Ulaanbaatar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is high, treeless steppe at over 1,000 m (3,300 feet), giving way to the Gobi desert in the south and to forested mountains in the north and west, and its sharply continental climate swings from summer heat to winters that can fall below minus 40 degrees. Herding on horseback has shaped its life for millennia; Genghis Khan united its tribes in 1206 and their descendants built the largest contiguous land empire in history, the country was later ruled from Qing China, became fully independent in the early 20th century, and left one-party communist rule in 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Myanmar": "Myanmar is a country of 676,578 km² (261,228 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 54 million people, bordered by Bangladesh, India, China, Laos and Thailand with a long coast on the Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea, and its capital at Naypyidaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A horseshoe of mountains encloses it on three sides and the Irrawaddy runs down the middle to a wide delta, giving the central dry zone and the coastal rice lands where most of the population lives; teak forests, jade and gemstones have long been among its exports. Buddhist kingdoms centred on Pagan and later Mandalay ruled here from the 11th century, Britain annexed the country in stages during the 19th century and governed it as part of its Indian empire, independence came in 1948, and the military has held power for most of the period since 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nepal": "Nepal is a landlocked country of 147,181 km² (56,827 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 30 million people, lying between China and India along the southern flank of the Himalaya, with its capital at Kathmandu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within about 200 km (120 miles) the land rises from the subtropical plains of the Tarai, barely 60 m (200 feet) above sea level, through the middle hills where most towns and farms lie, to eight of the world's ten highest peaks, among them Everest at 8,849 m (29,030 feet) on the Chinese border. A kingdom unified in the 18th century and never formally colonised, it opened to outsiders only in the mid-20th century, went through a Maoist insurgency from 1996 to 2006, and abolished the monarchy in 2008 to become a federal republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"North_Korea": "North Korea is a country of 120,540 km² (46,541 sq mi) on the northern half of the Korean peninsula in East Asia, with roughly 26 million people, bordered by China, Russia and, across a heavily fortified line, South Korea, and governed from Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four-fifths of it is mountain and upland, with the highest ground in the northeast and the little arable land concentrated on western coastal plains, and its mineral deposits of coal, iron and rare metals are extensive but hard to work. The peninsula was ruled by Japan from 1910 to 1945 and then divided along the 38th parallel; the northern state was founded in 1948, fought the Korean War from 1950 to 1953, which ended in an armistice rather than a peace treaty, and has since been a one-party state under three generations of the same family, isolated from most of the world economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Oman": "Oman is a country of 309,500 km² (119,500 sq mi) on the southeastern corner of the Arabian peninsula with roughly 5.5 million people, bordered by the United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia and Yemen, with long coasts on the Arabian Sea and the Gulf of Oman and its capital at Muscat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a narrow coastal plain the Hajar mountains rise above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) and catch enough rain to feed the irrigation channels that have watered its date gardens for two thousand years, while the interior fades into the sands of the Rub' al Khali and the far south catches a summer monsoon that turns the hills of Dhofar green. Its frankincense was traded to Egypt and Rome, its sailors ranged across the Indian Ocean, and from the 17th century its rulers held an overseas empire that reached Zanzibar and the East African coast before the modern sultanate took its present form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pakistan": "Pakistan is a country of about 881,900 km² (340,500 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 245 million people, bordered by India, China, Afghanistan and Iran with a coast on the Arabian Sea, and its capital at Islamabad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Indus and its tributaries run the length of the country and support one of the largest irrigation networks in the world, with the Karakoram and Himalaya rising in the north to K2 at 8,611 m (28,250 feet), the Thar desert in the southeast and the dry Balochistan plateau in the west. The Indus valley held one of the earliest urban civilisations, from about 2600 BCE; the modern state was created in 1947 when British India was partitioned, its eastern wing separated as Bangladesh in 1971, and it has alternated between elected and military governments ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"State_of_Palestine": "The State of Palestine is a state recognised by a large majority of United Nations members and holding non-member observer status at the United Nations, claiming the West Bank and the Gaza Strip, together about 6,000 km² (2,000 sq mi), with a population of roughly 5.5 million; its administrative seat is Ramallah and it claims Jerusalem as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two territories are separated by Israeli land: the West Bank is a hilly upland west of the Jordan valley, with olive groves and the cities of Hebron, Nablus and Bethlehem, while Gaza is a small, densely built coastal strip on the Mediterranean. The territories were part of the British Mandate over Palestine until 1948, came under Jordanian and Egyptian administration, and have been under Israeli occupation since 1967; statehood was declared in 1988, limited self-government followed accords signed in the 1990s, and the borders and final status remain unresolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Philippines": "The Philippines is an island country of about 300,000 km² (100,000 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 114 million people, made up of some 7,600 islands in the western Pacific, of which Luzon and Mindanao are by far the largest, with its capital at Manila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire and in the path of the Pacific typhoon belt, so earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and around 20 tropical storms a year are part of ordinary life, while its seas hold some of the richest coral reefs and marine biodiversity on Earth. Its islands traded with China and the Malay world long before Spain claimed them in 1565 and ruled for over three centuries, leaving Catholicism as the majority religion; the United States took control in 1898, and the country became fully independent in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Qatar": "Qatar is a country of 11,586 km² (4,473 sq mi) on a peninsula reaching north into the Persian Gulf from the Arabian mainland, with roughly 3 million people, bordered on land only by Saudi Arabia, and governed from Doha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is flat, low desert of gravel and sand with no permanent rivers and very little rain, so drinking water comes almost entirely from desalination and almost all of its farmland is irrigated; most of the population is concentrated in and around the capital. Long a small pearling and fishing society under the ruling Al Thani family, it was under British protection from 1916 until independence in 1971, and the discovery of a vast offshore natural gas field has since made it one of the highest-income countries in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saudi_Arabia": "Saudi Arabia is a country of about 2,150,000 km² (830,000 sq mi) occupying most of the Arabian peninsula, with roughly 34 million people, bordered by Jordan, Iraq, Kuwait, Qatar, the United Arab Emirates, Oman and Yemen, with coasts on the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf and its capital at Riyadh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has no permanent rivers at all, and much of its interior is desert, including the Rub' al Khali, the largest continuous body of sand in the world, with mountains along the Red Sea coast catching what little rain falls and oases and deep wells supporting settlement elsewhere. Mecca and Medina, the two holiest cities of Islam, lie in its west and draw millions of pilgrims each year; the modern kingdom was founded in 1932 by Abdulaziz Ibn Saud, and oil discovered in 1938 transformed it into one of the world's largest exporters of crude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Singapore": "Singapore is an island city-state of about 735 km² (284 sq mi) at the southern tip of the Malay peninsula in Southeast Asia, with roughly 6 million people, which makes it one of the most densely populated countries in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It consists of one main island and some 60 smaller ones, is low and flat with its highest point at 164 m (538 feet), and has steadily enlarged itself with land reclaimed from the sea; it sits beside the Strait of Malacca, through which a large share of the world's seaborne trade passes. Founded as a British trading post in 1819 on the site of an older port, it grew into one of the busiest harbours in the world, was occupied by Japan during the Second World War, joined Malaysia in 1963 and separated from it in 1965, and has since become a major financial and shipping centre with four official languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"South_Korea": "South Korea is a country of 100,363 km² (38,750 sq mi) on the southern half of the Korean peninsula in East Asia, with roughly 52 million people, bordered only by North Korea across a fortified line and otherwise surrounded by sea, with its capital at Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About seven-tenths of it is hill and mountain, mostly along the east, so the population and industry crowd onto the western and southern plains and river valleys, and around half of everyone in the country lives in the wider Seoul region. The peninsula was ruled by Japan from 1910 to 1945 and then divided; the southern state was founded in 1948, fought the Korean War from 1950 to 1953, industrialised at extraordinary speed from the 1960s onward, and moved from military rule to competitive elections in 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sri_Lanka": "Sri Lanka is an island country of 65,610 km² (25,330 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean off the southeastern tip of India, with roughly 22 million people, its commercial centre at Colombo and its legislature at nearby Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring of coastal plain surrounds central highlands rising above 2,500 m (8,200 feet), which catch two separate monsoons and divide the island into a wet southwest and a drier north and east, and those hills carry the tea plantations that have been its best-known export since the 19th century. Its ancient capital Anuradhapura and its Theravada Buddhist tradition go back more than two thousand years, Portuguese, Dutch and finally British powers ruled the coasts and then the whole island, independence came in 1948, and a civil war between the government and Tamil separatists lasted from 1983 to 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Syria": "Syria is a country of 185,180 km² (71,498 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 26 million people, bordered by Turkey, Iraq, Jordan, Israel and Lebanon with a short Mediterranean coast, and governed from Damascus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A narrow fertile coastal strip gives way to mountains and then to a wide inland plateau that dries into desert toward the southeast, with the Euphrates crossing the northeast and providing most of the country's irrigation water. Damascus and Aleppo are among the longest continuously inhabited cities in the world, the region held some of the earliest farming settlements and later the capital of the Umayyad caliphate, it came under Ottoman and then French rule before independence in 1946, and after decades of rule by the Assad family and a civil war that began in 2011 the government was overthrown at the end of 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Taiwan": "Taiwan is an island of 36,197 km² (13,976 sq mi) in East Asia, separated from the coast of mainland China by a strait about 180 km (110 miles) wide, with roughly 23 million people and its seat of government at Taipei. Mountains cover about two-thirds of it, running down the eastern side in a steep spine that rises to 3,952 m (12,970 feet) and drops almost directly into the Pacific, while the population, farmland and industry crowd onto the western coastal plain; it lies on an active plate boundary and in the path of Pacific typhoons. Settled first by Austronesian peoples, it saw Dutch and Spanish outposts in the 17th century, was ruled by the Qing from 1683, ceded to Japan in 1895 and returned in 1945; the government of the Republic of China moved there in 1949 after losing the Chinese civil war, and its political status remains disputed with the People's Republic of China, which claims the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tajikistan": "Tajikistan is a landlocked country of 141,400 km² (54,590 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 11 million people, bordered by Uzbekistan, Kyrgyzstan, China and Afghanistan, with its capital at Dushanbe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains cover about 93 percent of it, rising in the east to the Pamirs, a range so high and remote that it has been called the roof of the world, and its glaciers and rivers hold much of the water on which its downstream neighbours depend for irrigation. Its language is a form of Persian, unlike the Turkic tongues of most of the region; the area was part of successive Persian and Islamic states, was absorbed by the Russian Empire and became a Soviet republic, gained independence in 1991, and endured a civil war from 1992 to 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Thailand": "Thailand is a country of 513,120 km² (198,120 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 72 million people, bordered by Myanmar, Laos, Cambodia and Malaysia with coasts on both the Gulf of Thailand and the Andaman Sea, and its capital at Bangkok.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is the broad plain of the Chao Phraya, one of the great rice-growing regions of the world, with forested mountains in the north, the dry Khorat plateau in the northeast, and a long narrow isthmus running south toward the Malay peninsula. Kingdoms centred on Sukhothai and then Ayutthaya ruled from the 13th century, the country was the only state in the region never colonised by a European power, changed its name from Siam in 1939, and has been a constitutional monarchy since 1932, though with frequent military interventions in government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"East_Timor": "East Timor is a country of 14,954 km² (5,774 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 1.4 million people, occupying the eastern half of the island of Timor together with a small enclave on the island's northwest coast, with its capital at Dili.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rugged spine of mountains rises to almost 3,000 m (9,800 feet) close behind the northern shore, leaving little flat land, and a pronounced dry season on the northern side contrasts with wetter conditions in the south, so most households depend on maize, rice, coffee and livestock. A Portuguese colony for some four centuries, it declared independence in 1975 and was invaded and annexed by Indonesia days later; after a vote for independence in 1999 and a period of United Nations administration, it became fully sovereign in 2002, and it now relies heavily on oil and gas revenue from the Timor Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Turkmenistan": "Turkmenistan is a country of 491,210 km² (189,660 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 7.6 million people, bordered by Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Afghanistan and Iran with a long shore on the Caspian Sea, and its capital at Ashgabat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four-fifths of it is taken up by the Karakum, a sand and gravel desert with almost no rainfall, so settlement clings to the southern mountain foot and to the Amu Darya and the long Karakum canal drawn from it, while its natural gas reserves are among the largest in the world. Caravan routes across it carried the trade of the Silk Road through cities such as Merv, the region was absorbed by the Russian Empire in the late 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and since independence in 1991 it has followed a declared policy of permanent neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_Arab_Emirates": "The United Arab Emirates is a country of 83,600 km² (32,300 sq mi) on the southeastern side of the Arabian peninsula with roughly 11 million people, bordered by Saudi Arabia and Oman with a long coast on the Persian Gulf, and its capital at Abu Dhabi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mostly flat desert of sand and salt flats, rising to the Hajar mountains in the east, with no permanent rivers and summer heat and humidity that make air conditioning general; the great majority of its residents are foreign nationals who have come to work. It is a federation of seven emirates formed in 1971 when British protection ended, each keeping its own ruler while sharing a federal government; oil found in the late 1950s funded rapid growth, and Dubai in particular has built itself into a hub for trade, aviation and tourism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uzbekistan": "Uzbekistan is a country of 448,978 km² (173,351 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 36 million people, bordered by Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Afghanistan and Turkmenistan, and one of only two states in the world that are doubly landlocked, meaning every neighbour is landlocked too; its capital is Tashkent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it is the Kyzylkum desert and dry steppe, with mountains in the east and life concentrated along the Amu Darya and Syr Darya and in the fertile Fergana valley, while heavy diversion of those rivers for cotton has left the Aral Sea a fraction of its former size. Samarkand, Bukhara and Khiva were among the great cities of the Silk Road and later of the Timurid Empire, the region was conquered by the Russian Empire in the 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and the country has been independent since 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vietnam": "Vietnam is a country of 331,344 km² (127,933 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 101 million people, running in a long narrow S-shape down the eastern edge of the Indochinese peninsula, bordered by China, Laos and Cambodia with a coast of more than 3,000 km (1,900 miles), and its capital at Hanoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two crowded river deltas hold most of its people and rice, the Red River in the north and the Mekong in the south, joined by a coastal strip that narrows to under 50 km (31 miles) where the forested Annamite range presses close to the sea. Its territory was ruled from China for about a thousand years until 938, then by a succession of Vietnamese dynasties, became part of French Indochina in the 19th century, was divided in 1954 and fought a long war before being reunified in 1976, and opened its economy to markets from 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Yemen": "Yemen is a country of 527,968 km² (203,850 sq mi) at the southwestern corner of the Arabian peninsula with roughly 42 million people, bordered by Saudi Arabia and Oman with coasts on the Red Sea and the Arabian Sea, and its recognised capital at Sana'a.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a hot coastal plain the land climbs into highlands above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) that catch a summer monsoon, making it the best-watered part of the peninsula and allowing the terraced farming and coffee growing for which it has long been known, while the interior falls away into the sands of the Rub' al Khali and the island of Socotra lies far offshore with plants found nowhere else. Ancient kingdoms here grew rich on the incense trade, the north and south of the country were separate states until they merged in 1990, and a war that began in 2014 has divided control and caused a severe humanitarian crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Algeria": "Algeria is a country of 2,381,741 km² (919,595 sq mi) in North Africa, the largest on the continent, with roughly 47 million people, a Mediterranean coast and borders with Tunisia, Libya, Niger, Mali, Mauritania, Western Sahara and Morocco, and its capital at Algiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than four-fifths of it is Sahara, so almost the whole population lives on the narrow coastal plain and in the valleys of the Atlas ranges behind it, while the desert holds the oil and natural gas fields that dominate its exports. Its coast was Numidian, then Roman and Byzantine before the Arab conquests of the 7th century, it was an Ottoman regency for three hundred years, France invaded in 1830 and settled it as a colony, and independence came in 1962 after a war of nearly eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Angola": "Angola is a country of 1,246,700 km² (481,350 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 39 million people, facing the Atlantic between the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Zambia and Namibia, with a separate northern province, Cabinda, cut off from the rest, and its capital at Luanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A narrow coastal plain rises sharply to a broad interior plateau above 1,000 m (3,300 feet), cooler and better watered than the coast, which carries most of the farming, while the far south fades into desert and the north into tropical forest. Portugal held trading posts here from the 16th century and shipped enormous numbers of enslaved people to Brazil, colonial rule was formalised in the 19th century, independence came in 1975, and a civil war then continued until 2002; oil and diamonds dominate the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Benin": "Benin is a country of 114,763 km² (44,310 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 15 million people, a narrow strip running north from a short Atlantic coast between Togo, Burkina Faso, Niger and Nigeria, with Porto-Novo as its official capital and the government seated at Cotonou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind lagoons and sandbars on the coast the land rises through a humid plateau of oil palm and farmland to drier savanna and the Atakora hills in the northwest, where the country's few uplands lie. The kingdom of Dahomey ruled much of the region from the 17th century and grew powerful on the Atlantic slave trade through the port of Ouidah, France made it a colony in the 1890s, independence came in 1960, and a Marxist government held power from 1972 until multiparty elections in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Botswana": "Botswana is a landlocked country of 581,730 km² (224,610 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 2.7 million people, bordered by South Africa, Namibia, Zimbabwe and Zambia, with its capital at Gaborone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around seven-tenths of it is covered by the sands of the Kalahari, so surface water is scarce and settlement clusters in the east, but in the northwest the Okavango river spreads out and dies in the desert, creating a vast seasonal wetland that draws some of the greatest concentrations of wildlife in Africa. It was the British protectorate of Bechuanaland from 1885, became independent in 1966 as one of the poorest countries in the world, and the diamonds found soon afterwards funded decades of steady growth and a comparatively stable multiparty politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Burkina_Faso": "Burkina Faso is a landlocked country of 274,200 km² (105,900 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 24 million people, bordered by Mali, Niger, Benin, Togo, Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire, with its capital at Ouagadougou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits mostly on a low savanna plateau in the Sahel, with a single rainy season of three or four months, soils that are thin and easily exhausted, and the three branches of the Volta river providing much of its water; cotton and gold are its main exports. The Mossi kingdoms held the central plateau from about the 15th century and resisted outside control for a long time, France took the territory in the 1890s and ruled it as Upper Volta, independence came in 1960, and the country took its present name, meaning 'land of upright people', in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Burundi": "Burundi is a landlocked country of 27,834 km² (10,747 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 14 million people, bordered by Rwanda, Tanzania and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with Gitega as its political capital and Bujumbura, on Lake Tanganyika, as its largest city and economic centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is hilly plateau between about 1,400 and 2,000 m (4,600 to 6,600 feet), cool for the tropics and densely farmed on small plots, with the Nile and Congo watersheds meeting along its highest ridge; coffee and tea are its main exports. A kingdom ruled here for centuries before German and then Belgian administration, independence came in 1962, and repeated violence between Hutu and Tutsi communities culminated in a civil war that lasted from 1993 until the mid-2000s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cape_Verde": "Cape Verde is an island country of 4,033 km² (1,557 sq mi) in the Atlantic Ocean about 570 km (350 miles) west of Senegal, with roughly 525,000 people and its capital at Praia on the island of Santiago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is an archipelago of ten volcanic islands and several islets, dry and swept by the trade winds, with steep peaks rising to an active volcano of 2,829 m (9,281 feet) on Fogo and long droughts that have repeatedly driven its people to emigrate, so more Cape Verdeans now live abroad than at home. The islands were uninhabited when Portuguese ships reached them in 1456, were settled from 1462 and became an early base of the Atlantic slave trade, gained independence in 1975, and have since been noted for stable multiparty government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cameroon": "Cameroon is a country of 475,442 km² (183,569 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 29 million people, bordered by Nigeria, Chad, the Central African Republic, the Republic of the Congo, Gabon and Equatorial Guinea with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea, and its capital at Yaoundé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is sometimes called Africa in miniature because almost every landscape of the continent appears within it, from mangrove and rainforest in the south through central plateaus to savanna and the dry edge of the Sahel in the far north, with Mount Cameroon, an active volcano of 4,040 m (13,300 feet), rising near the coast. Germany claimed the territory in 1884, France and Britain divided it as mandates after the First World War, and the French and British portions became independent in 1960 and 1961 and joined, leaving the country with both French and English as official languages.",
"Central_African_Republic": "The Central African Republic is a landlocked country of 622,984 km² (240,535 sq mi) near the middle of the continent with roughly 5.5 million people, bordered by Chad, Sudan, South Sudan, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, the Republic of the Congo and Cameroon, with its capital at Bangui on the Ubangi river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is a rolling savanna plateau between about 600 and 700 m (2,000 to 2,300 feet) that forms the watershed between the Chad and Congo basins, with dense rainforest in the southwest and drier country toward the Sahel in the north. France ruled the territory as Ubangi-Shari, where concession companies imposed forced labour on a brutal scale, independence came in 1960, and repeated coups and armed conflicts since have left it among the poorest countries in the world despite deposits of diamonds, gold and uranium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Chad": "Chad is a landlocked country of 1,284,000 km² (495,800 sq mi) in north-central Africa with roughly 21 million people, bordered by Libya, Sudan, the Central African Republic, Cameroon, Nigeria and Niger, with its capital at N'Djamena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into three bands: Sahara desert across the north, rising to the volcanic Tibesti massif above 3,400 m (11,000 feet), Sahelian scrub through the middle, and wetter savanna in the south where most of the farming and population are found. Lake Chad on its western border, once one of the largest lakes in Africa, has shrunk drastically since the 1960s through drought and irrigation; the region was dominated by the Kanem-Bornu empire for close to a thousand years, France conquered it around 1900, independence came in 1960, and civil war and border conflicts have recurred ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Comoros": "The Comoros is an island country of 1,861 km² (719 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean, at the northern end of the Mozambique Channel between Mozambique and Madagascar, with roughly 880,000 people and its capital at Moroni on Grande Comore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It comprises three volcanic islands and their islets, steep and densely farmed, with an active volcano of 2,361 m (7,746 feet) on the largest; a fourth island of the archipelago, Mayotte, voted to stay under French administration and is claimed by the Comoros. The islands were settled by seafarers from East Africa, Arabia and the Malay world and became a Muslim trading society, France took them in the 19th century, independence came in 1975, and the country has since seen a long series of coups, though it remains a leading grower of ylang-ylang and vanilla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Republic_of_the_Congo": "The Republic of the Congo is a country of 342,000 km² (132,000 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 6.5 million people, straddling the equator between Gabon, Cameroon, the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Angola's Cabinda province, with a short Atlantic coast and its capital at Brazzaville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dense tropical rainforest covers about two-thirds of the country, drained by the Congo river and its tributary the Ubangi, which form its long eastern border, while a coastal plain and a series of plateaus lie to the west and centre. The area was part of the Kingdom of Kongo's wider sphere before France established control in the 1880s and made Brazzaville the capital of French Equatorial Africa, independence came in 1960, a Marxist government ruled from 1969 to 1992, and the economy now rests largely on offshore oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo": "The Democratic Republic of the Congo is a country of 2,344,858 km² (905,354 sq mi) in Central Africa, the second largest on the continent, with roughly 113 million people, bordered by nine states and holding only a 40 km (25 miles) outlet to the Atlantic, with its capital at Kinshasa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it lies in the basin of the Congo river, which curves across the equator and carries more water than any river except the Amazon, feeding the second largest rainforest in the world, with highlands and volcanic lakes along the eastern rift. Its territory was held from 1885 as the personal possession of King Leopold II of Belgium, whose rubber regime killed people on a vast scale, passed to the Belgian state in 1908 and became independent in 1960; wars from 1996 to 2003 drew in several neighbouring armies, and fighting continues in the east over land rich in cobalt, copper and coltan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ivory_Coast": "Ivory Coast is a country of 322,463 km² (124,504 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 33 million people, bordered by Liberia, Guinea, Mali, Burkina Faso and Ghana with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea, with Yamoussoukro as its official capital and the government and business seated at Abidjan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A flat coastal belt of lagoons gives way to rainforest across the south and to savanna in the north, with the country's few mountains in the far west near the Guinean border. Its plantations make it the world's largest producer of cocoa by a wide margin, France ruled it as a colony from 1893, independence came in 1960 under Félix Houphouët-Boigny, and after decades of prosperity the country went through political crises and two civil conflicts between 2002 and 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Djibouti": "Djibouti is a country of 23,200 km² (8,960 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 1.2 million people, bordered by Eritrea, Ethiopia and Somalia and facing Yemen across the Bab el-Mandeb strait, with its capital, also called Djibouti, on the Gulf of Tadjoura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the hottest and driest countries on Earth, a landscape of volcanic desert, lava fields and salt pans that includes Lake Assal, at 155 m (509 feet) below sea level the lowest point in Africa and among the saltiest bodies of water anywhere. Its position at the mouth of the Red Sea, on the shipping lane between Europe and Asia, is its chief asset: the port handles most of landlocked Ethiopia's trade, and several countries maintain military bases there; it was ruled by France, latterly as the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas, until independence in 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Egypt": "Egypt is a country of 1,001,450 km² (386,662 sq mi) in the northeastern corner of Africa with roughly 118 million people, bordered by Libya, Sudan and Israel, with coasts on the Mediterranean and the Red Sea and its capital at Cairo; the Sinai peninsula east of the Suez Canal lies in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is desert and almost all of its people live along the Nile and in its delta, a strip of irrigated land that makes up only a few per cent of the territory yet is among the most densely settled ground anywhere. Farming villages appeared along the river before 5000 BCE and a unified kingdom by about 3100 BCE, leaving pyramids, temples and hieroglyphic records; the country was later Persian, Greek, Roman, Arab and Ottoman, was occupied by Britain from 1882, and became a republic in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Equatorial_Guinea": "Equatorial Guinea is a country of 28,051 km² (10,831 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 1.9 million people, made up of a mainland region between Cameroon and Gabon and several islands in the Gulf of Guinea, with its capital at Malabo on the volcanic island of Bioko, well to the north of the rest of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mainland is mostly coastal plain rising to interior hills under dense rainforest, while Bioko is a steep volcanic island of rich soils; the climate is equatorial and wet through most of the year. Portugal ceded the territory to Spain in 1778, making it the only sovereign African state with Spanish as an official language, independence came in 1968, and the discovery of large offshore oil reserves in the 1990s transformed its economy while leaving much of the population poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Eritrea": "Eritrea is a country of 117,600 km² (45,410 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 3.7 million people, bordered by Sudan, Ethiopia and Djibouti with a long Red Sea coast that includes the Dahlak archipelago, and its capital at Asmara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hot, dry coastal strip climbs steeply to a central highland spine above 2,000 m (6,600 feet), cooler and better watered, where most of the population lives, while the western lowlands slope toward the Sudanese plains. Italy made it a colony in 1890 and built the modernist architecture of Asmara, Britain administered it after 1941, it was federated with and then annexed by Ethiopia, and after a war of independence lasting thirty years it became a sovereign state in 1993; a border war with Ethiopia followed from 1998 to 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Eswatini": "Eswatini is a landlocked country of 17,364 km² (6,704 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 1.2 million people, almost entirely surrounded by South Africa with a short eastern border with Mozambique, and with Mbabane as its administrative capital and Lobamba as the seat of its parliament and royal court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Despite its small size it falls into four clear belts running north to south: a cool mountainous highveld in the west, a middleveld of hills, a hot lowveld of savanna, and the Lubombo escarpment along the eastern edge. A Swazi kingdom took shape in the 18th and 19th centuries, the territory became a British protectorate in 1903, independence came in 1968, and it remains one of the last absolute monarchies in the world, renamed from Swaziland in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ethiopia": "Ethiopia is a landlocked country of 1,104,300 km² (426,370 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 135 million people, the second most populous on the continent, bordered by Eritrea, Djibouti, Somalia, Kenya, South Sudan and Sudan, with its capital at Addis Ababa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core is a broad highland massif rising above 2,000 m (6,600 feet) and split by the Great Rift Valley, cool and well watered, where most of the population farms and where Lake Tana feeds the Blue Nile, which supplies most of the water reaching Egypt; the surrounding lowlands are hot and dry. The kingdom of Aksum traded across the Red Sea and adopted Christianity in the 4th century, the country kept its own script, calendar and church, was the only African state to defeat a European invasion and remain independent apart from Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941, and a monarchy claiming descent from Solomon lasted until 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gabon": "Gabon is a country of 267,668 km² (103,347 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 2.4 million people, straddling the equator between Equatorial Guinea, Cameroon and the Republic of the Congo with an Atlantic coast, and its capital at Libreville. Tropical rainforest covers about 85 per cent of it, one of the highest proportions of any country, and its low population and extensive national parks leave large tracts of forest and coastline largely undisturbed, home to forest elephants, gorillas and nesting sea turtles. France established control in the 19th century and ruled it within French Equatorial Africa, independence came in 1960, and offshore oil discovered soon afterwards has given it one of the higher average incomes in sub-Saharan Africa while leaving the economy narrowly based.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"The_Gambia": "The Gambia is a country of 11,300 km² (4,360 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 2.7 million people, the smallest state on the African mainland, consisting of a narrow strip of land along both banks of the Gambia river that is surrounded by Senegal except at its short Atlantic coast, with its capital at Banjul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rarely more than 50 km (31 miles) wide, the country is flat and low, and life turns on the river, which is navigable far inland and floods a belt of rice fields and mangrove each rainy season, while groundnuts and tourism supply most of its earnings. British traders established posts on the river in the 17th century and it became a colony in the 19th, a period in which the estuary was a route for the Atlantic slave trade; independence came in 1965, and a leader who took power in a 1994 coup ruled until losing an election in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ghana": "Ghana is a country of 238,535 km² (92,099 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 34 million people, bordered by Ivory Coast, Burkina Faso and Togo with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea, and its capital at Accra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is low plains and plateau, with rainforest and cocoa farms in the southwest, savanna across the north, and the Volta river dammed at Akosombo to form Lake Volta, which by surface area is the largest reservoir in the world. Gold worked here for centuries drew European traders to build forts along the coast, which also became a centre of the Atlantic slave trade, the Ashanti Empire dominated the interior before British conquest, and in 1957 Ghana became the first colony in sub-Saharan Africa to gain independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guinea": "Guinea is a country of 245,857 km² (94,926 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 15 million people, bordered by Guinea-Bissau, Senegal, Mali, Ivory Coast, Liberia and Sierra Leone with an Atlantic coast, and its capital at Conakry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The highlands of the Fouta Djallon in its interior are sometimes called the water tower of West Africa, since the Niger, the Senegal and the Gambia rivers all rise there, and the country holds the largest known reserves of bauxite, the ore from which aluminium is made. It was ruled by France from the late 19th century and in 1958 was the only territory to reject membership of the French Community, taking immediate independence at the cost of an abrupt French withdrawal; it was governed by a single party until 1984 and has seen repeated coups since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Guinea-Bissau": "Guinea-Bissau is a country of 36,125 km² (13,948 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 2.3 million people, bordered by Senegal and Guinea with an Atlantic coast that includes the Bijagós archipelago of some 88 islands, and its capital at Bissau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is low and flat, cut by wide tidal estuaries and fringed with mangrove and swamp rice fields, with savanna and forest inland; cashew nuts account for most of its exports and much of its farming. Portugal claimed the coast in the 15th century and used it as a slaving station, colonial rule inland was only imposed in the early 20th century, an armed independence movement fought from 1963 and won recognition in 1974, and the country has since experienced repeated coups and political instability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lesotho": "Lesotho is a landlocked country of 30,355 km² (11,720 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 2.3 million people, entirely surrounded by South Africa, with its capital at Maseru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the only country in the world whose whole territory lies above 1,400 m (4,600 feet), and the Maloti and Drakensberg ranges rise above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) in the east, giving it snow in winter and abundant rainfall that it sells as water to South African cities through a large system of dams and tunnels. The Basotho nation was formed under Moshoeshoe I in the 1820s and 1830s as refugees gathered on its mountain strongholds, it became the British protectorate of Basutoland in 1868, which spared it absorption into South Africa, and it became an independent kingdom in 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Liberia": "Liberia is a country of 111,369 km² (43,000 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 5.7 million people, bordered by Sierra Leone, Guinea and Ivory Coast with an Atlantic coast, and its capital at Monrovia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a sandy coast of lagoons and mangrove the land rises through rolling forested hills to low mountains on the Guinean border, and it holds some of the largest remaining blocks of West Africa's rainforest along with iron ore deposits and rubber plantations. It was founded from 1822 as a settlement for free and formerly enslaved African Americans organised by the American Colonization Society, declared itself a republic in 1847 and is the oldest in Africa, was long dominated by the settler minority over the indigenous majority, and endured civil wars from 1989 to 2003 followed by an Ebola epidemic in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Libya": "Libya is a country of 1,759,540 km² (679,362 sq mi) in North Africa with roughly 7.5 million people, bordered by Egypt, Sudan, Chad, Niger, Algeria and Tunisia with a Mediterranean coast of more than 1,700 km (1,100 miles), and its capital at Tripoli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over nine-tenths of it is Sahara, and almost the entire population lives on the coastal strip and in a few oases, drawing much of its fresh water from a vast fossil aquifer pumped north through a system of pipelines. Its coast held Phoenician and Greek cities and later Roman ones such as Leptis Magna, it was Ottoman and then an Italian colony from 1911, became independent as a kingdom in 1951, was ruled by Muammar Gaddafi from 1969 until his overthrow in 2011, and has since been divided between rival governments; oil dominates its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Madagascar": "Madagascar is an island country of 587,041 km² (226,658 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean, about 400 km (250 miles) off the southeast coast of Africa across the Mozambique Channel, with roughly 33 million people and its capital at Antananarivo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the fourth largest island in the world and has been separated from other landmasses for tens of millions of years, so most of its plants and animals live nowhere else, among them the lemurs and the baobabs, though clearing for farming has destroyed a large share of its original forest. Its people are descended from Austronesian seafarers who arrived from the region of Borneo along with later Bantu-speaking settlers from Africa, the Merina kingdom unified most of the island in the 19th century, France conquered it in the 1890s, and independence came in 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Malawi": "Malawi is a landlocked country of 118,484 km² (45,747 sq mi) in southeastern Africa with roughly 21 million people, a long narrow state bordered by Zambia, Tanzania and Mozambique, with its capital at Lilongwe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lake Malawi fills the eastern side of the country and covers about a fifth of its area, a deep rift lake holding more species of fish than any other lake in the world, while highland plateaus rise on either side of the valley and most of the population farms small plots of maize and tobacco. Missionaries and traders arrived in the 19th century in the wake of David Livingstone's journeys, Britain declared the protectorate of Nyasaland in 1891, independence came in 1964, and one-party rule under Hastings Banda lasted until multiparty elections in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mali": "Mali is a landlocked country of 1,240,192 km² (478,841 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 25 million people, bordered by Algeria, Niger, Burkina Faso, Ivory Coast, Guinea, Senegal and Mauritania, with its capital at Bamako.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern half is Sahara and the centre Sahel, while the Niger river arcs through the country and spreads into a vast inland delta whose seasonal flood supports rice, fishing and herding and carries most of the population. The medieval empires of Ghana, Mali and Songhai grew rich on trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt and made Timbuktu and Djenné centres of learning; France conquered the region in the late 19th century, independence came in 1960, and since 2012 the north and centre have been contested by armed groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mauritania": "Mauritania is a country of 1,030,700 km² (397,960 sq mi) in northwest Africa with roughly 5.3 million people, bordered by Western Sahara, Algeria, Mali and Senegal with a long Atlantic coast, and its capital at Nouakchott.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around three-quarters of it is Sahara, and the desert has been advancing for decades, pushing herders into towns so that a mostly nomadic society has become a largely urban one within a single lifetime; only the far south along the Senegal river is reliably cultivable. Its coastal waters are among the richest fishing grounds in the world and it exports large quantities of iron ore from mines at Zouérat; France completed its conquest in the early 20th century, independence came in 1960, and Mauritania was the last country in the world to abolish slavery in law, in 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mauritius": "Mauritius is an island country of 2,040 km² (788 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean about 2,000 km (1,200 miles) off the southeast coast of Africa, with roughly 1.3 million people and its capital at Port Louis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island is volcanic, ringed by one of the world's longest continuous coral reefs and rising to a broken central plateau, and it was once covered in forest that has largely been cleared for sugar cane, a change that helped drive the dodo to extinction within a century of European arrival. Uninhabited until the Dutch settled it in 1638, it passed to France and then to Britain, its plantations worked first by enslaved Africans and later by indentured labourers from India, whose descendants form the largest part of the population; independence came in 1968, and the country has since built a diversified economy around textiles, tourism and financial services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Morocco": "Morocco is a country of 446,550 km² (172,410 sq mi) in northwest Africa with roughly 38 million people, facing both the Atlantic and the Mediterranean and separated from Spain by the Strait of Gibraltar, with its capital at Rabat; it also administers most of the disputed territory of Western Sahara to its south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three ranges of the Atlas mountains cross the country from southwest to northeast, rising above 4,000 m (13,000 feet) and catching the rain that feeds its farmland, while the land beyond them falls away into the Sahara. Berber dynasties including the Almoravids and Almohads ruled from here over an empire reaching into Spain and West Africa, the country was never part of the Ottoman Empire, became a French and Spanish protectorate in 1912, regained independence in 1956, and remains a monarchy under a dynasty in place since the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mozambique": "Mozambique is a country of 801,590 km² (309,500 sq mi) in southeastern Africa with roughly 34 million people, with a coast of about 2,500 km (1,600 miles) on the Indian Ocean and borders with Tanzania, Malawi, Zambia, Zimbabwe, Eswatini and South Africa, and its capital at Maputo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Coastal lowlands make up nearly half the country and are crossed by the Zambezi, which is dammed at Cahora Bassa for hydroelectricity, while plateaus and highlands rise inland; cyclones and floods strike the coast regularly. Swahili trading towns lined the shore for centuries before Portugal took control of the coast from 1498 and eventually the interior, independence came in 1975 after a ten-year war, a civil war followed until 1992, and large offshore gas fields found since 2010 now dominate its investment.",
"Namibia": "Namibia is a country of 825,615 km² (318,772 sq mi) in southwestern Africa with roughly 3 million people, which makes it one of the most thinly populated countries in the world, bordered by Angola, Zambia, Botswana and South Africa with a long Atlantic coast, and its capital at Windhoek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Namib desert runs the length of that coast and is thought to be the oldest desert on Earth, its dunes among the highest anywhere, while the Kalahari lies to the east and a central plateau in between holds most of the towns and farms. Germany colonised the territory in 1884 and put down the Herero and Nama uprisings of 1904 to 1908 with a campaign now widely recognised as genocide, South Africa ruled it after 1915 and extended apartheid to it, and independence came only in 1990 after a long guerrilla war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Niger": "Niger is a landlocked country of 1,267,000 km² (489,200 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 28 million people, bordered by Libya, Chad, Nigeria, Benin, Burkina Faso, Mali and Algeria, with its capital at Niamey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four-fifths of it is Sahara or the semi-arid Sahel, with the volcanic Aïr massif rising in the centre and the country's only permanent river, the Niger, crossing the far southwest, where nearly all the farmland and population are found. Trans-Saharan caravan routes ran through it for centuries and the sultanate of Agadez controlled the trade, France conquered the region around 1900, independence came in 1960, and its uranium mines supply a significant share of world output while drought and armed conflict in the Sahel continue to press on daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Nigeria": "Nigeria is a country of 923,769 km² (356,669 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 230 million people, by far the most populous in Africa, bordered by Benin, Niger, Chad and Cameroon with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea, and its capital at Abuja, while Lagos is its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Niger and Benue rivers meet in the middle and divide the country into three parts: mangrove and rainforest in the south, savanna plateaus in the centre, and drier country toward the Sahel in the north, with oil fields in the Niger delta supplying most export earnings. More than 250 ethnic groups live within its borders, the region held the Sokoto Caliphate, the kingdom of Benin and the Oyo Empire before Britain amalgamated it as a single colony in 1914, independence came in 1960, and a war over the secession of Biafra was fought from 1967 to 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rwanda": "Rwanda is a landlocked country of 26,338 km² (10,169 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 14 million people, bordered by Uganda, Tanzania, Burundi and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with its capital at Kigali.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Known as the land of a thousand hills, it is almost entirely upland between about 1,500 and 2,500 m (4,900 to 8,200 feet), with volcanoes along its northwestern border and terraced smallholdings covering the slopes; it is the most densely populated country on the African mainland, and coffee, tea and tourism to see mountain gorillas carry much of its foreign earnings. A kingdom ruled here before German and then Belgian administration hardened the distinction between Hutu and Tutsi, independence came in 1962, and in 1994 a genocide killed some 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi, in about a hundred days.",
"São_Tomé_and_Príncipe": "São Tomé and Príncipe is an island country of 964 km² (372 sq mi) in the Gulf of Guinea about 250 km (160 miles) off the coast of Central Africa, with roughly 240,000 people and its capital at São Tomé; it is the second smallest state in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two main islands and several islets are the peaks of extinct volcanoes rising steeply from deep water, wet and heavily forested, with dramatic rock towers left where softer material has eroded away and a large share of the land now under protection. The islands were uninhabited when Portuguese navigators reached them in the 1470s and became the first plantation colony in the tropics, worked by enslaved Africans and producing first sugar and later cocoa; independence came in 1975, and cocoa and tourism remain the mainstays of the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Senegal": "Senegal is a country of 196,712 km² (75,951 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 19 million people, bordered by Mauritania, Mali, Guinea and Guinea-Bissau and wrapped around The Gambia, with an Atlantic coast and its capital at Dakar on the Cap-Vert peninsula, the westernmost point of the African mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mostly low and flat, sandy in the drier north and greener toward the south, with the Senegal river marking its northern border and groundnuts, fishing and phosphates supporting much of the economy. The island of Gorée off Dakar was a European trading post from the 15th century and a site associated with the Atlantic slave trade, France ruled the territory and made Dakar the capital of French West Africa, independence came in 1960, and Senegal has since had one of the steadier records of civilian government in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Seychelles": "Seychelles is an island country of 459 km² (177 sq mi) in the western Indian Ocean, about 1,500 km (930 miles) east of the African mainland, with roughly 120,000 people and its capital at Victoria on Mahé; it has the smallest population of any African state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It comprises 115 islands of two very different kinds: an inner group of granite islands, unusual for mid-ocean and thought to be fragments of an ancient continent, and outer coral atolls including Aldabra, home to the world's largest population of giant tortoises. Uninhabited until the French began settling it in 1770, it passed to Britain in 1814, was worked by enslaved Africans on coconut and spice plantations, became independent in 1976, and now depends on tourism and tuna fishing, giving it the highest income per head in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sierra_Leone": "Sierra Leone is a country of 71,740 km² (27,700 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 8.6 million people, bordered by Guinea and Liberia with an Atlantic coast, and its capital at Freetown, built around one of the largest natural harbours in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mangrove swamps line the shore, rainforest and rolling plateau fill the interior, and the country lies in one of the wettest belts in Africa, with several metres of rain falling in a season; diamonds, rutile and bauxite are its chief mineral exports. Freetown was founded in 1787 as a settlement for freed and rescued slaves, the interior became a British protectorate in 1896, independence came in 1961, and a civil war fuelled partly by diamonds ran from 1991 to 2002 and was followed by a severe Ebola epidemic in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Somalia": "Somalia is a country of 637,657 km² (246,201 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 18 million people, bordered by Djibouti, Ethiopia and Kenya and holding the longest mainland coastline in Africa, about 3,300 km (2,100 miles) along the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean, with its capital at Mogadishu. It is mostly dry plateau and semi-desert with mountains in the north, and only the two rivers of the south, the Jubba and the Shabelle, allow settled farming, so herding camels, goats and cattle has long been the way of life for much of the population. Its coastal towns traded across the Indian Ocean for centuries, the territory was divided between Britain and Italy and reunited at independence in 1960, and the collapse of central government in 1991 was followed by decades of conflict, with the northwest functioning separately as Somaliland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"South_Africa": "South Africa is a country of 1,221,037 km² (471,445 sq mi) at the southern end of the continent with roughly 63 million people, bordered by Namibia, Botswana, Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Eswatini, wrapped around Lesotho, and with coasts on both the Atlantic and the Indian Ocean; it has three capitals, Pretoria for the executive, Cape Town for parliament and Bloemfontein for the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A high interior plateau is separated from a narrow coastal belt by the Great Escarpment, including the Drakensberg, and the climate ranges from a Mediterranean southwest to subtropical east and the arid Karoo and Kalahari in between; its mines have long led the world in platinum and gold. Dutch settlement began at the Cape in 1652 and British rule followed, the Union was formed in 1910, from 1948 the apartheid system enforced racial segregation and disenfranchised the Black majority, and the first fully democratic elections in 1994 brought Nelson Mandela to the presidency.",
"South_Sudan": "South Sudan is a landlocked country of 644,329 km² (248,777 sq mi) in East-Central Africa with roughly 12 million people, bordered by Sudan, Ethiopia, Kenya, Uganda, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and the Central African Republic, with its capital at Juba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The White Nile runs the length of the country and spreads in the middle into the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world, which floods over an area the size of a small country each rainy season and long made travel through the region extremely difficult; savanna and tropical forest lie beyond it. Two long wars against governments in Khartoum, from 1955 to 1972 and from 1983 to 2005, ended in a referendum that made it the world's newest widely recognised state in 2011; a civil war followed from 2013 to 2018, and oil provides almost all government revenue.",
"Sudan": "Sudan is a country of 1,861,484 km² (718,723 sq mi) in northeastern Africa, the third largest on the continent, with roughly 52 million people, bordered by Egypt, Eritrea, Ethiopia, South Sudan, the Central African Republic, Chad and Libya with a Red Sea coast, and its capital at Khartoum, where the Blue and White Niles meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Desert covers the north, giving way to Sahel scrub and then savanna in the south, and almost all settlement follows the Nile and its tributaries, while the Darfur region in the west rises to the volcanic Marra mountains. The Nubian kingdoms of Kush and Meroë ruled here and at one point conquered Egypt, the country was governed jointly by Britain and Egypt until independence in 1956, it lost its southern third when South Sudan seceded in 2011, and a war between rival armed forces broke out in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Togo": "Togo is a country of 56,785 km² (21,925 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 8.6 million people, a narrow strip running about 550 km (340 miles) inland from a coast of only 56 km (35 miles) on the Gulf of Guinea, bordered by Ghana, Burkina Faso and Benin, with its capital at Lomé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lagoons and sandbars line the shore, a plateau of farmland lies behind, and a range of hills crosses the middle of the country from southwest to northeast, dividing the wetter south from the drier savanna of the north; phosphate rock, cocoa, coffee and cotton are its main exports. Germany claimed the territory as Togoland in 1884, Britain and France divided it after the First World War, the French portion became independent in 1960, and a single family held the presidency from 1967 into the present century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Tunisia": "Tunisia is a country of 163,610 km² (63,170 sq mi) in North Africa, the smallest of the Maghreb states, with roughly 12 million people, bordered by Algeria and Libya with a Mediterranean coast of about 1,300 km (810 miles), and its capital at Tunis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The north is hilly and green, watered enough for grain and olives, the centre is dry steppe, and the south passes into salt flats and the Sahara; the country juts toward Sicily, narrowing the Mediterranean at its middle. Carthage, founded near modern Tunis, contested the western Mediterranean with Rome before its destruction in 146 BCE, the region was later Roman, Arab, Ottoman and from 1881 a French protectorate, independence came in 1956, and protests beginning in 2010 unseated its long-serving president and set off the uprisings across the wider region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Uganda": "Uganda is a landlocked country of 241,038 km² (93,065 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 48 million people, bordered by South Sudan, Kenya, Tanzania, Rwanda and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with its capital at Kampala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is a plateau around 1,100 m (3,600 feet) sitting between the two branches of the Great Rift Valley, with a large share of Lake Victoria in the southeast, the point where the White Nile begins its journey north, and the glaciated Rwenzori range on the western border reaching almost 5,110 m (16,800 feet). The kingdom of Buganda dominated the region before Britain declared a protectorate in 1894, independence came in 1962, the country suffered under the rule of Idi Amin from 1971 to 1979 and the wars that followed, and its economy now rests on coffee, fish and increasingly on oil found near Lake Albert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zambia": "Zambia is a landlocked country of 752,618 km² (290,587 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 20 million people, bordered by eight states and named after the Zambezi river, with its capital at Lusaka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is a high plateau between about 1,000 and 1,400 m (3,300 to 4,600 feet), cooler than its latitude suggests, and the Zambezi forms much of its southern border, where it drops 108 m (354 feet) at Victoria Falls, one of the largest sheets of falling water in the world, before being held back by the Kariba dam. The Copperbelt in the north has been mined for a century and copper still provides most of the country's export earnings; the region became the British territory of Northern Rhodesia, independence came in 1964, and one-party rule lasted until multiparty elections in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zimbabwe": "Zimbabwe is a landlocked country of 390,757 km² (150,872 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 17 million people, bordered by Zambia, Mozambique, South Africa and Botswana, with its capital at Harare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A central ridge of high veld above 1,200 m (3,900 feet) runs from southwest to northeast and carries most of the towns and farms, falling away to the hotter low veld along the Zambezi in the north and the Limpopo in the south, with the Eastern Highlands rising near the Mozambican border. The stone city of Great Zimbabwe, built between the 11th and 15th centuries at the centre of a gold-trading state, gives the country its name; Britain's chartered company took the territory in 1890, a white minority government declared independence unilaterally in 1965, and majority rule came in 1980 after a long guerrilla war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"New_Zealand": "New Zealand is an island country of 268,021 km² (103,483 sq mi) in the southwest Pacific about 2,000 km (1,200 miles) southeast of Australia, with roughly 5.3 million people and its capital at Wellington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It consists of two large islands and many smaller ones: the North Island, warmer and volcanic, with geysers and hot springs, and the South Island, split lengthwise by the Southern Alps, which rise above 3,700 m (12,000 feet) and feed glaciers and deep fiords. It lies on the boundary of two tectonic plates, so earthquakes are frequent, and its long isolation produced a fauna dominated by birds with no native land mammals except bats; Polynesian settlers arrived around 1300 and became the Māori, Britain and Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi in 1840, and the country has been self-governing since the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Papua_New_Guinea": "Papua New Guinea is a country of 462,840 km² (178,700 sq mi) in Oceania with roughly 10.8 million people, occupying the eastern half of the island of New Guinea together with about 600 smaller islands, with its capital at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A spine of rugged mountains rises above 4,500 m (15,000 feet) along the main island and separates coastal lowlands and swamps from densely forested highland valleys, terrain so broken that many communities were effectively isolated from one another until recent decades. That isolation helped produce more than 800 living languages, more than any other country, and agriculture in its highlands is among the oldest known anywhere; Germany and Britain divided the territory in the 19th century, Australia administered it for most of the 20th, and independence came in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Fiji": "Fiji is an island country of 18,272 km² (7,055 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 930,000 people, made up of about 330 islands of which some 110 are inhabited, with its capital at Suva on Viti Levu, the largest island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two main islands are volcanic and mountainous, rising above 1,300 m (4,300 feet) and dividing a wet windward side of rainforest from a drier leeward side of grassland and sugar cane, while the outer islands are mostly low coral. Settled by Austronesian voyagers more than three thousand years ago, it became a British colony in 1874, and the indentured labourers brought from India to work its sugar estates left a large Indo-Fijian population; independence came in 1970, several coups followed from 1987, and tourism, sugar and remittances now carry the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solomon_Islands": "Solomon Islands is a country of 28,896 km² (11,157 sq mi) in the southwest Pacific with roughly 840,000 people, a double chain of about 990 islands stretching some 1,500 km (930 miles) east of Papua New Guinea, with its capital at Honiara on Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The larger islands are volcanic, steep and covered in rainforest, ringed by coral reefs and lagoons, and the country sits on an active plate boundary that brings frequent earthquakes and tsunamis; logging, fishing and palm oil supply most of its exports. Settled from Papua and the wider Pacific thousands of years ago, the islands became a British protectorate in 1893, saw some of the fiercest fighting of the Pacific war around Guadalcanal in 1942 and 1943, gained independence in 1978, and endured serious civil unrest between 1998 and 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vanuatu": "Vanuatu is an island country of 12,189 km² (4,706 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 330,000 people, a Y-shaped chain of about 80 islands lying east of Australia and north of New Caledonia, with its capital at Port Vila on Efate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is intensely volcanic, with several active cones including one of the world's most accessible lava lakes, and it lies in the cyclone belt on an active plate boundary, which is why it regularly ranks as the country most exposed to natural hazards. Its people speak more than a hundred languages along with Bislama, a creole that serves as a common tongue; Britain and France governed the islands jointly as the New Hebrides from 1906, an unusual arrangement with two parallel administrations, and independence came in 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Samoa": "Samoa is an island country of 2,842 km² (1,097 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 220,000 people, made up chiefly of the two volcanic islands of Savai'i and Upolu together with several smaller ones, with its capital at Apia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands are mountainous and forested, built by volcanoes that were active into the 20th century, with narrow coastal plains where most villages stand and coral reefs offshore; the surrounding ocean and a warm wet climate shape a way of life still organised around extended families and their chiefs. Settled by Austronesian voyagers around three thousand years ago and a centre from which much of Polynesia was peopled, the islands were divided between Germany and the United States in 1899, New Zealand administered the western part after 1914, and Samoa became in 1962 the first Pacific island country to gain independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tonga": "Tonga is an island country of 747 km² (288 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 100,000 people, an archipelago of more than 170 islands of which about 36 are inhabited, spread over a long north-south stretch of ocean, with its capital at Nuku'alofa on Tongatapu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands come in two kinds, low raised coral platforms in the east and volcanic peaks in the west, several still active; the country sits beside the Tonga Trench, one of the deepest parts of the ocean, and a submarine eruption in 2022 was among the most violent recorded anywhere. A unified kingdom was established in the 19th century, and Tonga is the only Pacific island nation never to have been formally colonised, though it accepted British protection from 1900 to 1970; it remains a constitutional monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kiribati": "Kiribati is an island country of 811 km² (313 sq mi) of land in the central Pacific with roughly 130,000 people, made up of 33 coral atolls and reef islands scattered across some 3.5 million km² (1.4 million sq mi) of ocean, with its seat of government on Tarawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only country to lie in all four hemispheres, straddling both the equator and the date line, and almost none of its land rises more than a few metres above the sea, which makes rising ocean levels and saltwater in its groundwater a threat to its habitability. Settled by Micronesian voyagers thousands of years ago, the islands were a British protectorate and then colony from the late 19th century, saw heavy fighting at Tarawa in 1943, and became independent in 1979; fishing licences sold to distant fleets provide much of the government's income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Federated_States_of_Micronesia": "The Federated States of Micronesia is an island country of 702 km² (271 sq mi) of land in the western Pacific with roughly 110,000 people, made up of 607 islands spread across about 2,700 km (1,700 miles) of ocean north of the equator, with its capital at Palikir on Pohnpei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a federation of four states, Yap, Chuuk, Pohnpei and Kosrae, whose islands range from high volcanic peaks with dense forest and heavy rainfall to low coral atolls, and whose people speak several distinct languages. The islands were ruled in turn by Spain, Germany and Japan and then administered by the United States under a United Nations trusteeship, and they became independent in 1986 under a compact of free association that leaves defence to the United States in exchange for financial support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Marshall_Islands": "The Marshall Islands is an island country of 181 km² (70 sq mi) of land in the central Pacific with roughly 36,000 people, made up of 29 coral atolls and five isolated islands arranged in two long chains, with its capital at Majuro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it stands about two metres (6 ft 7 in) above the sea, so ocean flooding and saltwater in its soil and wells are pressing problems, and its territorial waters cover an area of ocean larger than many countries. Settled by Micronesian navigators who charted the swell with lattices of sticks and shells, the islands were claimed by Spain, then Germany and Japan, and were administered by the United States after 1944; 67 nuclear weapons were tested at Bikini and Enewetak between 1946 and 1958, displacing communities that in some cases have never returned, and independence came in 1986 under a compact of free association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Palau": "Palau is an island country of 459 km² (177 sq mi) in the western Pacific with roughly 18,000 people, an archipelago of more than 340 islands about 900 km (560 miles) east of the Philippines, with its capital at Ngerulmud on Babeldaob.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its people live on a few islands, while the Rock Islands to the south are hundreds of uninhabited limestone mounds undercut by the sea and enclosed by a barrier reef, and the surrounding waters hold exceptionally rich coral ecosystems that the country has protected by closing most of its exclusive economic zone to commercial fishing. Settled from island Southeast Asia thousands of years ago, Palau was held in turn by Spain, Germany and Japan, saw heavy fighting in 1944, was administered by the United States under a United Nations trusteeship, and became independent in 1994 under a compact of free association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nauru": "Nauru is an island country of 21 km² (8 sq mi) in the central Pacific just south of the equator, with roughly 12,000 people, which makes it the third smallest state in the world by area and the smallest republic; it has no official capital, and government offices stand in the district of Yaren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island is a single raised coral platform ringed by a narrow fertile coastal strip where almost everyone lives, with a central plateau of jagged limestone pinnacles left behind after a century of phosphate mining stripped roughly four-fifths of the surface. Germany annexed it in 1888, Australia, Britain and New Zealand administered it after 1914, independence came in 1968, and the phosphate wealth that briefly gave it one of the highest incomes per head in the world was largely exhausted by the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tuvalu": "Tuvalu is an island country of 26 km² (10 sq mi) in the central Pacific with roughly 11,000 people, made up of nine low coral atolls and reef islands spread over about 700 km (430 miles) of ocean, with its seat of government on Funafuti; it is the fourth smallest state in the world and among the least visited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing on it rises more than about 4.6 m (15 feet) above sea level and most of the land is far lower, so tidal flooding already reaches inland several times a year and the country is among the most exposed anywhere to rising seas, a point its governments have pressed in international climate negotiations. Settled by Polynesian voyagers, the islands were a British protectorate from 1892 as part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands, separated from the Gilberts in 1975 and became independent in 1978; fishing licences and revenue from its internet domain provide much of its income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Canada": "Canada is a country of 9,984,670 km² (3,855,100 sq mi) in northern North America, the second largest in the world by area, with about 41 million people, bordered by the United States to the south and northwest, and its capital at Ottawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is boreal forest, tundra and the ancient rock of the Canadian Shield, with the Rocky Mountains in the west, the Great Lakes and the Saint Lawrence in the southeast, and a coastline on three oceans that is the longest of any country, so the great majority of its people live in a narrow band near the southern border. First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples lived there long before French and British colonies were founded from the 17th century; the British colonies federated in 1867, gained full legislative independence by 1931, and the country has two official languages, English and French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_States": "The United States is a federal republic of 9,830,000 km² (3,800,000 sq mi) in North America with about 340 million people, bordered by Canada and Mexico, made up of 50 states and a federal capital district at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land runs from the Atlantic coastal plains over the Appalachians to the central Great Plains, the Rocky Mountains and the Pacific coast, and it also takes in Alaska in the far northwest and the Hawaiian islands in mid-ocean. Thirteen British colonies declared independence in 1776 and secured it in 1783; the country then expanded west at the expense of Native American nations, was torn between 1861 and 1865 by a civil war over slavery, and in the 20th century became the largest economy and the dominant military and cultural power in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mexico": "Mexico is a country of 1,964,375 km² (758,449 sq mi) in southern North America with about 130 million people, bordered by the United States, Guatemala and Belize, with coasts on the Pacific and the Gulf of Mexico and its capital at Mexico City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> High plateaus lie between two long mountain chains, the Sierra Madre Occidental and the Sierra Madre Oriental, with volcanoes above 5,000 m (16,000 feet), desert across the north, tropical forest in the south and the flat limestone shelf of Yucatán in the east. It was the seat of the Olmec, Maya, Zapotec and Aztec civilisations before the Spanish conquest of 1519 to 1521 made it the centre of New Spain; independence came in 1821, a revolution begun in 1910 remade the country, and it is now the most populous Spanish-speaking nation in the world.",
"Guatemala": "Guatemala is a country of 108,889 km² (42,042 sq mi) in Central America with roughly 19 million people, bordered by Mexico, Belize, Honduras and El Salvador, with coasts on both the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at Guatemala City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A chain of volcanoes runs above the southern highlands, where most people live and where earthquakes are frequent, while the north holds the low limestone forest of the Petén and the cool Cuchumatanes rise in the west. It was the heartland of the Maya, whose cities at Tikal and elsewhere flourished for centuries before Spanish conquest in the 1520s; independence came in 1821, a civil war from 1960 to 1996 killed some 200,000 people, most of them Maya, and today about two fifths of the population is Indigenous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belize": "Belize is a country of 22,966 km² (8,867 sq mi) on the Caribbean coast of Central America with about 420,000 people, bordered by Mexico and Guatemala, and its capital at Belmopan, though the largest city is Belize City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Low swampy plain and pine savanna cover the north, the Maya Mountains rise in the south, and offshore lies the Belize Barrier Reef, the longest reef system in the northern hemisphere, strung with atolls and hundreds of small cays. Maya cities such as Caracol stood here long before English and Scottish woodcutters settled the coast in the 17th century for logwood and mahogany; it became the colony of British Honduras, took its present name in 1973, gained independence in 1981, and is the only Central American state whose official language is English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"El_Salvador": "El Salvador is a country of 21,041 km² (8,124 sq mi) on the Pacific coast of Central America with about 6 million people, bordered by Guatemala and Honduras, and its capital at San Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the smallest and most densely settled state on the Central American mainland, a land of two volcanic ranges and a central plateau of rich ash soils where coffee grows and most people live, shaken often by earthquakes and eruptions. Pipil and other Indigenous peoples farmed the valleys before Spanish conquest in 1524; independence came in 1821, land concentrated in few hands through the coffee boom, and a civil war from 1980 to 1992 drove hundreds of thousands abroad, whose remittances remain a mainstay of the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Honduras": "Honduras is a country of 112,492 km² (43,433 sq mi) in Central America with roughly 11 million people, bordered by Guatemala, El Salvador and Nicaragua, with a long Caribbean coast and a short Pacific one on the Gulf of Fonseca, and its capital at Tegucigalpa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is rugged forested mountain, with the broad valleys and banana plantations of the north coast, the sparsely peopled rainforest and wetlands of the Mosquitia in the east, and reefs along the Bay Islands offshore. The Maya city of Copán, famous for its carved stelae and hieroglyphic stairway, stood in the west before Spanish conquest from 1524; independence came in 1821, and from the late 19th century American fruit companies shaped much of its politics and economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nicaragua": "Nicaragua is a country of 130,373 km² (50,337 sq mi) in Central America with about 7 million people, the largest state on the isthmus by area, bordered by Honduras and Costa Rica, with coasts on the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at Managua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A line of active volcanoes runs down the Pacific side beside Lake Nicaragua, the biggest lake in Central America, while the wide Caribbean lowland is thinly settled rainforest and swamp where Miskito and Creole communities have long lived apart from the Spanish-speaking west. Spanish conquest began in 1524 and independence came in 1821; the Somoza family ruled from 1936 until the Sandinista revolution of 1979, and the contra war of the 1980s left the country among the poorest in the hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Costa_Rica": "Costa Rica is a country of 51,100 km² (19,700 sq mi) in Central America with about 5 million people, bordered by Nicaragua and Panama, with coasts on the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at San José.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Volcanic ranges run the length of the country and enclose a temperate central valley where most of the population lives, and although it covers a very small share of the world's land it holds an outsized share of its species, with about a quarter of its territory under some form of protection. Spanish settlement from the 1560s was thin and poor, independence from Spain came with the rest of Central America in 1821 and Costa Rica declared itself a separate republic in 1848, and after a short civil war the army was abolished in 1948, since when the country has been a stable democracy living on coffee, bananas, microchips and ecotourism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Panama": "Panama is a country of 75,320 km² (29,080 sq mi) at the southern end of Central America with about 4.5 million people, bordered by Costa Rica and Colombia, and its capital at Panama City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It occupies the narrowest neck of the Americas, an S-shaped isthmus of forested mountains and long coasts on both the Caribbean and the Pacific, broken only by the roadless rainforest of the Darién at its eastern end. Spanish treasure crossed it from the 16th century, and after separating from Colombia in 1903 with United States backing, the country was cut through by the Panama Canal, opened in 1914 and handed to Panamanian control at the end of 1999, which with banking and shipping registry underpins its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cuba": "Cuba is an island country of 109,884 km² (42,426 sq mi) in the northern Caribbean with about 9.4 million people, the largest island state in the region, lying south of Florida and west of Hispaniola, with its capital at Havana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is low rolling farmland and grassland well suited to sugar cane and tobacco, with the Sierra Maestra rising above 1,900 m (6,200 feet) in the southeast, long white beaches, and hurricanes crossing it most years. Taíno peoples lived there before Columbus landed in 1492 and Spain built a plantation colony worked by enslaved Africans; independence came in 1898 after a long struggle and United States intervention, and the revolution of 1959 brought a one-party communist state that survived decades of American embargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jamaica": "Jamaica is an island country of 10,991 km² (4,244 sq mi) in the Caribbean with about 2.8 million people, lying south of Cuba, with its capital at Kingston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A limestone spine runs down the middle, rising to the Blue Mountains above 2,200 m (7,200 feet) in the east and falling away to the cockpit country of sinkholes and steep hummocks in the west, ringed by coastal plains and beaches. Taíno inhabitants were nearly wiped out after the Spanish arrived in 1494; the English took the island in 1655 and made it one of the richest sugar colonies in the world with enslaved African labour, and after emancipation in 1834 and independence in 1962 it became known worldwide for reggae music and its sprinters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Haiti": "Haiti is a country of 27,750 km² (10,710 sq mi) occupying the western third of the island of Hispaniola in the Caribbean, with roughly 12 million people, bordered by the Dominican Republic, and its capital at Port-au-Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two mountainous peninsulas enclose the Gulf of Gonâve, the interior is steep and heavily deforested, and the island sits on active faults that produced the catastrophic earthquake of 2010. It was the French sugar colony of Saint-Domingue, the richest in the Caribbean, until enslaved people rose in 1791 and defeated French, Spanish and British forces to declare independence in 1804, making it the first Black republic and the second independent state in the Americas, though a crushing indemnity paid to France and long periods of dictatorship and instability followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dominican_Republic": "The Dominican Republic is a country of 48,671 km² (18,792 sq mi) occupying the eastern two thirds of the island of Hispaniola in the Caribbean, with about 12 million people, bordered by Haiti, and its capital at Santo Domingo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four mountain ranges cross it, including the Cordillera Central with Pico Duarte at 3,098 m (10,160 feet), the highest point in the Caribbean, and between them lie fertile valleys, dry southwestern plains and a saltwater lake below sea level. Santo Domingo, founded in 1496, was the first lasting European city in the Americas and the base from which Spain colonised the region; the country won independence from Haiti in 1844, endured the dictatorship of Rafael Trujillo from 1930 to 1961, and now lives largely on tourism, mining and remittances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"The_Bahamas": "The Bahamas is an island country of 13,878 km² (5,358 sq mi) of land in the western Atlantic with about 400,000 people, made up of some 700 islands and more than 2,000 cays scattered over shallow banks southeast of Florida, with its capital at Nassau on New Providence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands are flat limestone, none rising much above 60 m (200 feet), surrounded by clear shallow water, coral reefs and blue holes, and lying in the path of Atlantic hurricanes. Lucayan people were taken away as slaves within a generation of Columbus's landfall here in 1492; British settlement began in 1648, the islands sheltered pirates and later blockade runners and rum smugglers, and since independence in 1973 tourism and offshore finance have carried the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Barbados": "Barbados is an island country of 430 km² (170 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 280,000 people, lying well east of the main arc of the Lesser Antilles, with its capital at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unlike its volcanic neighbours it is a low cap of coral limestone on uplifted seabed, rising gently to 336 m (1,100 feet) at Mount Hillaby, with porous ground that gives it caves, underground streams and few rivers. The English settled it in 1627 and turned it into the first great sugar island of the Caribbean, worked by enslaved Africans whose descendants form most of the population; it became independent in 1966 and replaced the British monarch with a president in 2021, becoming a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Trinidad_and_Tobago": "Trinidad and Tobago is a country of 5,130 km² (1,980 sq mi) at the southern end of the Caribbean with about 1.4 million people, made up of two main islands lying just off the coast of Venezuela, with its capital at Port of Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trinidad broke from the South American mainland in geological time, so its hills, swamps and forests hold continental plants and animals, and it also holds Pitch Lake, the largest natural asphalt deposit in the world, while smaller Tobago is a volcanic ridge fringed with reefs. Spain held Trinidad from 1498 and Britain from 1797, while Tobago changed hands many times; after emancipation, indentured labourers from India were brought in, and since independence in 1962 oil and natural gas have made the country one of the wealthier states in the region and the birthplace of calypso and the steelpan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Antigua_and_Barbuda": "Antigua and Barbuda is an island country of 442 km² (171 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 95,000 people, made up of two main islands and the tiny uninhabited Redonda, with its capital at Saint John's on Antigua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Antigua is a low island of worn volcanic hills and limestone with deeply indented bays and many beaches, and it is dry enough that water has always been scarce, while flat Barbuda to the north is a coral platform ringed by lagoons and seabird colonies. The English settled Antigua in 1632 and built sugar estates worked by enslaved Africans, and English Harbour became a base for the Royal Navy in the Caribbean; independence came in 1981, and tourism now provides most of the country's income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dominica": "Dominica is an island country of 750 km² (290 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 67,000 people, lying between the French territories of Guadeloupe and Martinique, with its capital at Roseau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the youngest and most rugged islands of the Lesser Antilles, a wall of forested volcanic peaks rising above 1,400 m (4,600 feet) and streaming with rivers, hot springs and the second largest boiling lake in the world, and its exposed position makes hurricane damage severe. Its steep terrain helped the Kalinago hold out against European settlement longer than anywhere else in the region, and a Kalinago territory survives on the east coast; France and Britain contested the island through the 18th century, and independence came in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Grenada": "Grenada is an island country of 344 km² (133 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 115,000 people, made up of one main island and the southern Grenadines of Carriacou and Petite Martinique, with its capital at Saint George's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island is volcanic, with forested ridges rising above 800 m (2,600 feet), a crater lake at Grand Etang, and rich soils that made it a leading producer of nutmeg and mace, which is why it is often called the Isle of Spice. The French seized it from the Kalinago in 1650 and Britain took it in 1763; independence came in 1974, and after a leftist government was overthrown from within, United States and Caribbean forces invaded in 1983 and constitutional rule was restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Kitts_and_Nevis": "Saint Kitts and Nevis is a country of 261 km² (101 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 48,000 people, the smallest sovereign state in the Americas by both area and population, made up of two volcanic islands separated by a narrow channel, with its capital at Basseterre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Kitts is dominated by the forested cone of Mount Liamuiga at 1,156 m (3,793 feet), its lower slopes once terraced in cane fields, while rounder Nevis rises to a single cloud-capped peak, and both are fringed with beaches and reefs. English settlers arrived in 1623 and made these the first English colonies in the Caribbean, sharing Saint Kitts with the French for decades and building a sugar economy on enslaved labour; independence came in 1983, sugar production ended in 2005, and tourism and offshore services replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Lucia": "Saint Lucia is an island country of 617 km² (238 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 180,000 people, lying between Martinique and Saint Vincent, with its capital at Castries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is volcanic and mountainous, best known for the Pitons, two steep lava spires rising straight from the sea on the southwest coast beside a collapsed crater with sulphur springs, and its wet interior is thick with rainforest. France and Britain fought over the island so often that it changed hands fourteen times before Britain kept it in 1814, and the two colonial languages left a French-based creole spoken alongside English; independence came in 1979, and bananas and tourism became the mainstays of the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Vincent_and_the_Grenadines": "Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is a country of 389 km² (150 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 100,000 people, made up of one large island and a chain of some 30 smaller ones running south towards Grenada, with its capital at Kingstown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saint Vincent is steep and green, crowned by the active volcano La Soufrière at 1,234 m (4,049 feet), which erupted destructively in 1902 and again in 2021, while the northern Grenadines are low, dry and reef-fringed and draw sailors and visitors. The Kalinago and the Garifuna, of mixed Indigenous and African descent, resisted European settlement into the 1790s, when Britain deported most Garifuna to Central America; independence came in 1979, and bananas, tourism and remittances now sustain the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Colombia": "Colombia is a country of 1,141,748 km² (440,831 sq mi) in northwestern South America with about 52 million people, bordered by Panama, Venezuela, Brazil, Peru and Ecuador, the only South American state with coasts on both the Caribbean and the Pacific, and its capital at Bogotá.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes split into three ranges as they enter the country, enclosing the valleys of the Cauca and Magdalena where most cities stand, while the east is flat savanna and Amazon rainforest and the Pacific coast is among the wettest places on Earth. Muisca and other peoples worked the gold that fed the El Dorado legend before Spanish conquest from 1499; independence was won in 1819 under Simón Bolívar, and a long internal conflict involving guerrillas, paramilitaries and the drug trade led to a peace agreement in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Venezuela": "Venezuela is a country of 916,445 km² (353,841 sq mi) in northern South America with roughly 28 million people, bordered by Colombia, Brazil and Guyana, with a long Caribbean coast and its capital at Caracas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its landscapes range from Andean peaks and the oil-rich basin of Lake Maracaibo to the wide Orinoco plains and the ancient sandstone tabletops of the Gran Sabana, where Angel Falls drops 979 m (3,210 feet), the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world. Spanish settlement began in the early 16th century, independence was won under Simón Bolívar by 1821, and oil discovered in the 1910s made it wealthy before political crisis and economic collapse after 2013 drove nearly 8 million people to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guyana": "Guyana is a country of 214,969 km² (83,000 sq mi) on the northeastern shoulder of South America with about 840,000 people, bordered by Venezuela, Brazil and Suriname, and its capital at Georgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all its people live on a narrow coastal strip that lies partly below sea level and is protected by seawalls and drainage canals built in the Dutch period, while the interior is rainforest, savanna and the sandstone highlands where the Potaro river drops 226 m (741 feet) at Kaieteur Falls. Dutch settlement from the 17th century gave way to British rule in 1814, and sugar plantations were worked first by enslaved Africans and later by indentured labourers from India, whose descendants are the largest ethnic group; independence came in 1966, large offshore oil discoveries since 2015 have transformed the economy, and Venezuela maintains a long-standing claim to the Essequibo region that is before the International Court of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Suriname": "Suriname is a country of 163,820 km² (63,251 sq mi) on the northeastern coast of South America with about 640,000 people, bordered by Guyana, Brazil and French Guiana, and its capital at Paramaribo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the smallest sovereign state on the continent and one of the most forested countries in the world, with more than nine tenths of its land under rainforest and almost all its people living on the coastal plain, behind which lie granite hills and a vast reservoir created by the Afobaka dam. The Dutch took it from England in 1667 and ran a brutal plantation economy on enslaved African labour, from which escapees formed the Maroon communities that still live in the interior; after emancipation, workers came from India, Java and China, giving the country an unusually mixed population, and independence followed in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ecuador": "Ecuador is a country of 257,217 km² (99,312 sq mi) in western South America with about 18 million people, bordered by Colombia and Peru, with a Pacific coast and its capital at Quito, and it also governs the Galápagos Islands some 900 km (560 miles) offshore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The equator crosses it, giving it its name, and two Andean ranges run down the middle with snow-capped volcanoes such as Chimborazo, whose summit is the point on the Earth's surface farthest from the planet's centre, while lowland banana and cacao country lies west and Amazon rainforest east. Quito was a northern seat of the Inca Empire before the Spanish conquest of the 1530s; independence came in 1822 and the country left Gran Colombia in 1830, and oil, bananas, flowers and shrimp now dominate its exports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peru": "Peru is a country of 1,285,216 km² (496,224 sq mi) in western South America with about 34 million people, bordered by Ecuador, Colombia, Brazil, Bolivia and Chile, with a Pacific coast and its capital at Lima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three sharply different zones divide it: a narrow desert coast where the cold Humboldt current feeds one of the world's richest fisheries, the high Andes with peaks above 6,700 m (22,000 feet) and Lake Titicaca on the Bolivian border, and the Amazon lowland that covers more than half the country. It was the seat of Norte Chico, one of the oldest urban societies in the Americas, and later of the Inca Empire, whose capital Cusco and mountain estate at Machu Picchu still stand; Spain conquered it from 1532 and ruled from Lima, and independence was declared in 1821 and secured in 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bolivia": "Bolivia is a landlocked country of 1,098,581 km² (424,164 sq mi) in central South America with about 12.6 million people, bordered by Brazil, Peru, Chile, Argentina and Paraguay, with its constitutional capital at Sucre and its seat of government at La Paz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The western third is high Andean plateau, holding Lake Titicaca and the vast salt flat of Uyuni at around 3,650 m (12,000 feet), while the east falls away to tropical lowland, dry Chaco scrub and the Amazon basin. The silver of Potosí funded the Spanish empire for two centuries and cost enormous numbers of Indigenous and African lives; independence came in 1825, the Pacific coast was lost to Chile in a war ending in 1884, and a majority Indigenous population saw one of its own elected president in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Paraguay": "Paraguay is a landlocked country of 406,752 km² (157,048 sq mi) in central South America with about 7 million people, bordered by Bolivia, Brazil and Argentina, and its capital at Asunción.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Paraguay river cuts it in two: the east is rolling grassland and remnants of Atlantic forest where nearly all the population lives, while the west is the Chaco, a hot thorn-scrub plain with few inhabitants, and the Paraná river on the Brazilian border drives the enormous Itaipú hydroelectric dam. Jesuit missions organised Guaraní communities under Spanish rule, independence came in 1811, and the War of the Triple Alliance from 1864 to 1870 killed a large share of the population; Guaraní remains an official language spoken by most people alongside Spanish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chile": "Chile is a country of 756,102 km² (291,932 sq mi) along the western edge of South America with about 19.5 million people, bordered by Peru, Bolivia and Argentina, and its capital at Santiago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stretches more than 4,000 km (2,500 miles) from north to south while rarely exceeding 200 km (120 miles) in width, running from the Atacama, the driest desert on Earth, through a fertile Mediterranean centre to Patagonian fjords, glaciers and the cold channels near Cape Horn, with the Andes along its whole eastern flank and frequent great earthquakes offshore. Mapuche communities in the south held out against Spanish rule for centuries; independence was won by 1818, nitrate and later copper made the country a mining economy, and a military coup in 1973 was followed by 17 years of dictatorship before the return of democracy in 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Argentina": "Argentina is a country of 2,780,400 km² (1,073,500 sq mi) in southern South America with about 46 million people, the second largest state on the continent, bordered by Chile, Bolivia, Paraguay, Brazil and Uruguay, and its capital at Buenos Aires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes form its western edge and rise at Aconcagua to 6,961 m (22,840 feet), the highest summit outside Asia, while the fertile pampas grassland of the centre carries the cattle and grain that long made the country rich, and the south is the windswept plateau of Patagonia. Spanish settlement grew slowly until independence in 1816, after which mass immigration from Italy and Spain reshaped the population; the 20th century brought the rise of Peronism, a military dictatorship from 1976 to 1983 that killed and disappeared thousands, and repeated economic crises since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uruguay": "Uruguay is a country of 176,215 km² (68,037 sq mi) in southeastern South America with about 3.4 million people, bordered by Brazil and Argentina, facing the Atlantic and the wide estuary of the Río de la Plata, with its capital at Montevideo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely rolling grassland with low ridges, no mountains and a mild climate, so cattle and sheep ranching has shaped the land and the economy since colonial times, and nearly half the population lives in the capital. Spain and Portugal contested the territory before it became independent in 1828 as a buffer between its two large neighbours; early in the 20th century it built one of the first welfare states in the region, endured a military dictatorship from 1973 to 1985, and has since been known for stable democracy and liberal social laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lake_Turkana": "Lake Turkana is a long, narrow lake in the arid Rift Valley of northern Kenya, its far northern tip crossing into Ethiopia, and it is both the largest permanent desert lake and the largest alkaline lake in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About nine-tenths of its water arrives from the Ethiopian highlands down the Omo River, and since the lake has no outlet the water is brackish and takes on a green cast from algae, which has earned it the name Jade Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was called Lake Rudolf until 1975, and the dry fossil beds along its shores, Koobi Fora in the east and West Turkana in the west, have yielded one of the richest records of early hominins anywhere; national parks on the lake were added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Badlands": "Badlands are dry, thinly vegetated tracts of country in which soft sedimentary rocks such as clay, silt and shale have been carved by running water and wind into a maze of steep gullies, sharp ridges and flat-topped hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They develop where rain falls seldom but hard on ground too loose and impermeable to hold much plant cover, so that each downpour strips the surface faster than soil and vegetation can rebuild it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constantly retreating slopes lay bare fresh layers of rock, which makes badlands prized hunting ground for fossils, and the name translates a French phrase for the eroded country of the northern Great Plains of North America, which in turn echoed a Lakota expression meaning bad land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lomekwi_3": "Lomekwi 3 is an archaeological site in the badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where flaked stone artifacts lay in deposits dated to about 3.3 million years ago, the oldest such tools known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was found in 2011 by a team led by Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis and published in 2015, and its assemblage of roughly 150 cores, flakes and anvils was produced by holding the core in both hands and striking it down onto a stationary block on the ground, a heavy method that gave large thick flakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because that date lies some 700,000 years before the oldest Oldowan tools and before the genus <i>Homo</i> appears, the finds are taken by many as evidence that toolmaking began among earlier hominins, while others question how securely the artifacts and their age have been established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gona,_Ethiopia": "Gona is a research area of roughly 500 km² (200 sq mi) of fossil-bearing badlands in the Afar Region of northeastern Ethiopia, cut by several tributaries of the Awash River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations there in the early 1990s recovered thousands of flaked stones dated to about 2.6 million years ago, for many years the oldest known tools of the Oldowan, some of them lying among broken animal bones that show carcasses were being cut up and cracked open for marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its deposits span more than six million years and have also yielded fossils of <i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i> and <i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>, an unusually complete <i>Homo erectus</i> pelvis, and early Acheulean handaxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lomekwian": "The Lomekwian is the name proposed in 2015 for the stone tool industry represented by the artifacts of Lomekwi 3, a site west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya dated to about 3.3 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its knappers worked large blocks against heavy stones, mostly by bringing the block down in both hands onto an anvil set on the ground or by holding it steady on an anvil while striking it from above with a hammerstone, and they left big thick flakes together with pieces battered from use in pounding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the industry rests on a single excavated assemblage and a long gap separates it from the next known tools, its standing as a distinct tradition is debated, and some archaeologists would rather wait for further sites before adopting the name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australopithecus_afarensis": "<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> is an extinct hominin species that lived in eastern Africa between roughly 3.9 and 2.9 million years ago and is known from the remains of more than 300 individuals found in Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It walked upright on short legs, with a broad pelvis and an arched foot, yet kept long arms, curved fingers and a brain averaging about 446 cubic centimetres, close to a third of the modern human average, and adults stood between roughly 1.1 and 1.5 metres (3 ft 7 in to 4 ft 11 in) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The species was named in 1978 by Donald Johanson, Tim White and Yves Coppens after the Afar region of Ethiopia, and its best-known remains are the partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy and the many individuals recovered together at a single locality at Hadar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kenyanthropus_platyops": "<i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i> is an extinct hominin known chiefly from KNM-WT 40000, a badly shattered but reconstructed skull found in 1999 at Lomekwi, west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, in deposits about 3.5 to 3.2 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meave Leakey and her colleagues named it in 2001, taking the species name from Greek words for 'flat face', since the cheekbones sit far forward and the muzzle is unusually flat for so early a hominin, while the molars are small and the braincase ape-sized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it deserves a genus of its own is disputed: some researchers place it within <i>Australopithecus</i>, and others hold that the crushing and distortion of the skull are too severe to support a new species at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hominini": "A hominin is any member of Hominini, the group within the primate family Hominidae that contains modern humans together with the extinct species on the human side of the split from the other African apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its most common use it takes in <i>Homo</i> along with earlier genera such as <i>Australopithecus</i>, <i>Paranthropus</i> and <i>Ardipithecus</i>, whose members are marked out above all by habitual upright walking on two legs, and of them all only <i>Homo sapiens</i> survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the group's boundary falls is a matter of classification rather than of fact, and the word is easily confused with hominid, which once carried almost exactly the meaning hominin now has and today covers all the great apes and their ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mosaic_evolution": "Mosaic evolution is the pattern in which the different parts of an organism change at different rates and at different times, so that a lineage can carry newly evolved features alongside others left much as they were in its ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It follows from natural selection acting on particular structures and functions rather than on the body as an indivisible whole, which is why fossils of transitional forms so often combine traits once assigned to separate stages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palaeontologists invoke it for animals such as <i>Archaeopteryx</i>, feathered and winged yet toothed and long-tailed, and for early hominins whose hips and feet were remodelled for upright walking long before their brains grew large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Raymond_Dart": "Raymond Dart was an Australian-born anatomist who spent his career in South Africa, heading the anatomy department of the University of the Witwatersrand in Johannesburg from 1922 until 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1925 he described the fossil skull of a child from the Taung lime works as a new species, <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>, arguing that it stood between apes and humans and that Africa, rather than Asia or Europe, was the continent on which humans had emerged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His claim met years of scepticism from established scholars, and although later discoveries bore out his reading of Africa's place in human origins, his further idea that these creatures were hunters who made tools of bone, tooth and horn has not been sustained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Taung_Child": "The Taung Child is the fossilized skull of a young <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>, recovered in 1924 from lime workings at Taung in South Africa and dated to roughly 2.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It preserves the face, much of the braincase and the jaws with the milk teeth and first molars in place, from which the child's age at death is put at three or four years, and the limestone that filled the braincase hardened into a natural cast of the brain's outer surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Raymond Dart's description of it in 1925 was the first announcement of an early hominin from Africa; puncture damage around the eye sockets has since suggested to some researchers that the child was killed by a large bird of prey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lucy_(Australopithecus)": "Lucy is a partial skeleton of <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>, catalogued as AL 288-1 and dated to about 3.2 million years ago, found in 1974 at Hadar in the Afar region of Ethiopia by a team led by Donald Johanson and Maurice Taieb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several hundred fragments make up one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known, including much of the pelvis, a knee and a lower jaw, and the shape of the hip and leg bones shows that this small-brained female, about 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in) tall, walked upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was nicknamed after the Beatles song playing in camp on the night of the discovery and is also known as Dinkinesh, Amharic for you are marvellous; the bones are kept at the National Museum of Ethiopia in Addis Ababa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hadar,_Ethiopia": "Hadar is a cluster of fossil localities in the badlands of the Afar region of northeastern Ethiopia, where the Awash River cuts through lake and floodplain sediments laid down between about 3.45 and 2.95 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognized in 1970 by the French geologist Maurice Taieb and excavated from 1973 onwards by an international team, it has produced hundreds of <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> fossils, among them the partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy and the remains of at least 13 individuals gathered at one spot, known as the First Family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its younger levels have also yielded an upper jaw of early <i>Homo</i> dated to about 2.35 million years ago along with simple stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Laetoli_footprints": "The Laetoli footprints are trails of hominin footprints preserved in hardened volcanic ash at Laetoli in northern Tanzania, dated to about 3.66 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best-known trail, uncovered in 1978 by Mary Leakey's team, runs some 27 metres (89 feet) and holds about 70 prints left by three individuals moving in the same direction, the smallest walking beside the largest while the third set its feet in the largest one's tracks; the impressions show a big toe held in line with the other toes and a stride that struck the heel first and pushed off from the toes, much as a barefoot modern human walks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are usually attributed to <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>, whose bones occur in the same beds, and are treated as the earliest direct evidence that upright walking was habitual, though prints at a nearby locality have been argued to belong to a second hominin with a different, cross-stepping gait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Genus": "A genus, plural genera, is a rank in the classification of living and fossil organisms that sits above the species and below the family, grouping together species judged to be closely related by descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name forms the first half of a species' two-part scientific name and must begin with a capital letter, while the rules of zoological naming also ask that genus and species names be set in a type face different from the surrounding text, usually italics, as in <i>Homo sapiens</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two-part names were popularized by Carl Linnaeus in the 18th century, and because a genus expresses a judgement about relationships rather than a measurable boundary, taxonomists regularly disagree about which species belong together and split or merge genera as new evidence appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Anatomy": "Anatomy is the branch of biology concerned with the structure of living things: the shape, position and relationships of the bones, muscles, organs and tissues that make up a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is studied at every scale, from the gross anatomy visible to the naked eye and explored by dissection down to the microscopic anatomy of tissues and cells, and comparing the same structures across different species shows how bodies have been remodelled over the course of evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A specialist in the subject is called an anatomist, and because structure and function are so closely tied, the discipline underpins medicine, surgery, veterinary science and the study of fossil remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Johannesburg": "Johannesburg is the largest city in South Africa and the capital of Gauteng province, spread across the Highveld plateau at about 1,750 m (5,740 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in 1886 after gold was found on the Witwatersrand, the ridge of hills beneath it, and the rush that followed turned a mining camp into the economic centre of the country, drawing workers from across southern Africa and giving rise to the migrant labour system and to segregated townships such as Soweto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is none of South Africa's three national capitals, though the Constitutional Court sits at Constitution Hill in Braamfontein, and the Cradle of Humankind, the richest source of hominin fossils anywhere, lies to the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Taung": "Taung is a town in the North West province of South Africa, lying south-west of Johannesburg towards the eastern edge of the Kalahari; its name means 'place of the lion', from the Setswana word <i>tau</i> and a chief who bore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quarrying for lime at the nearby Buxton works cut into ancient tufa and calcrete deposits, and in 1924 blasting there released the fossil skull of a young hominin, which Raymond Dart described the following year as the first known member of <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fossil deposits at the quarry were added to a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005, and the name of the town is now attached to the specimen itself.",
"Australopithecus_africanus": "<i>Australopithecus africanus</i> is an extinct hominin species known from southern Africa between roughly 3.3 and 2.1 million years ago, and the first early hominin ever described from the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It walked upright on two legs and carried a brain averaging about 460 cc, a little larger than a chimpanzee's, with a lightly built face, small canines and thickly enamelled cheek teeth suited to a varied plant diet, while its long arms and curved finger bones suggest that climbing still mattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Raymond Dart named the species in 1925 from a child's skull found at Taung, and further remains from the cave sites of Sterkfontein, Makapansgat and Gladysvale have kept it at the centre of debate over which australopith, if any, gave rise to <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Africa": "Africa is the world's second-largest continent, covering about 30.3 million km² (11.7 million sq mi) with its islands and holding some 1.5 billion people in 54 internationally recognised states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only continent to reach well into both the northern and southern temperate zones, so its climate belts run outward from equatorial rainforest through savanna to the Sahara in the north and the Kalahari and Namib in the south, and it is split from the Red Sea to Mozambique by the East African Rift, which has stretched the crust so thin that eastern Africa is primed for continental breakup and whose basins hold a world-famous fossil record of human evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest traces of the human lineage and of <i>Homo sapiens</i> come from the continent, which also saw early states such as Egypt, Aksum, Mali and Great Zimbabwe, was drained of people by the Atlantic and Indian Ocean slave trades, was partitioned among European powers in the late 19th century, and regained independence across almost all of its territory between the 1950s and the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Afar_Region": "The Afar Region is one of the regional states of Ethiopia, a hot, arid lowland in the north-east of the country named for the Afar people, many of whom herd camels, goats and cattle across it; its administrative seat is the purpose-built town of Semera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the Afar Triangle, where three arms of the Earth's crust are pulling apart, and takes in the Danakil Depression, a salt-crusted basin more than 100 m (330 feet) below sea level and among the hottest inhabited places on Earth, along with active volcanoes such as Erta Ale and the lower course of the Awash River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same rifting steadily exposes layer upon layer of ash-dated sediment, and localities within the region, among them Hadar and the Middle Awash, have yielded some of the most important early hominin fossils known, including the skeleton nicknamed Lucy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Awash_River": "The Awash is a river of about 1,250 km (777 miles) in Ethiopia, rising in the highlands west of Addis Ababa and running north-east down the Great Rift Valley into the Afar lowlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches no sea: its basin is endorheic, and the river ends in a chain of saline lakes near the border with Djibouti, the last of them Lake Abbe, losing much of its water on the way to evaporation and to the irrigation schemes that make it the most heavily used river in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its middle and lower valley cuts through thick sediments interleaved with datable volcanic ash, exposing an exceptional record of hominin fossils and early stone tools, and the Lower Valley of the Awash was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ardipithecus_kadabba": "<i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i> is an extinct hominin species known from the Middle Awash area of Ethiopia and dated to roughly 5.8 to 5.2 million years ago, close to the time when the human and chimpanzee lineages are thought to have separated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is represented by a modest set of fossils, mostly teeth and jaw fragments together with a few hand, foot, arm and collarbone pieces from at least five individuals; the teeth keep a partially working version of the arrangement apes use to sharpen the upper canine against the lower premolar, close to that of a female ape, while a single toe bone has been read as a sign that it already pushed off in an upright stride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yohannes Haile-Selassie published the material in 2001 and, with Gen Suwa and Tim White, raised it to a species of its own in 2004, and the small sample leaves both its status and its position in the human lineage uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ardipithecus_ramidus": "<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i> is an extinct hominin species from Ethiopia dated to about 4.4 million years ago, described in 1994 by Tim White, Gen Suwa and Berhane Asfaw from fossils collected at Aramis in the Middle Awash and moved to a genus of its own the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 100 specimens are now known, among them a partial female skeleton nicknamed Ardi that was announced in 2009 after fifteen years of painstaking recovery from crumbling bone, and together they show a mixture of traits: a small brain of roughly 300 to 350 cc, canines reduced towards the human pattern, a pelvis that suggests upright walking on the ground, and a divergent, grasping big toe suited to climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its anatomy has been used to argue that the last common ancestor of humans and chimpanzees was not itself chimpanzee-like, a reading that other researchers dispute, as they do the reconstruction of the wooded habitat in which it is said to have lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"KNM-WT_40000": "KNM-WT 40000 is a fossil hominin cranium found in 1999 by Justus Erus, a member of a team led by Meave Leakey, at Lomekwi west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, in sediments dated to about 3.5 million years ago; its catalogue number records the Kenya National Museums and the West Turkana collecting area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The skull is mostly complete but came out of the ground in two pieces, the braincase separated from the face and the whole considerably distorted, and its striking features are a tall, flat face with high cheekbones and unusually small, thickly enamelled teeth, which the team judged different enough from other early hominins to warrant a new genus and species, <i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>, published in 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other researchers have argued that the distortion makes the shape of the face unreliable and that the specimen may belong within <i>Australopithecus</i>, so its identity remains contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Quaternary": "The Quaternary is the most recent period of geological time, running from about 2.58 million years ago to the present and made up of the Pleistocene and Holocene epochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is defined by the repeated growth and collapse of great ice sheets: dozens of glacial and interglacial swings, paced by slow changes in the Earth's orbit, which moved sea level up and down by more than 100 m (330 feet) and redrew coastlines, rivers and the ranges of plants and animals again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its base was fixed in 2009 at a rock section at Monte San Nicola in Sicily, and the period covers the whole span in which the genus <i>Homo</i> spread out of Africa and across the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Antarctica": "Antarctica is the southernmost continent, about 14 million km² (5.4 million sq mi) of land centred on the South Pole and buried beneath an ice sheet that averages close to 1.9 km (1.2 miles) thick and leaves only about 0.2% of the rock exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the coldest, driest and windiest of the continents, and the lowest air temperature ever measured on Earth, −89.2 °C (−129 °F), was recorded at Vostok Station on 21 July 1983; so little snow falls over the high interior, under 50 mm (2 inches) of water a year, that it counts as a desert, yet the ice already there holds some 60% of the world's fresh water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nobody lives on it permanently and no state governs it: the Antarctic Treaty, signed in Washington in 1959 and in force since 1961, sets the territorial claims aside, reserves the continent for peaceful purposes and keeps scientific research there free and open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Greenland": "Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.2 million km² (850,000 sq mi) between the Arctic and the North Atlantic, some 81% of it buried under an ice sheet more than 3 km (1.9 miles) thick at its deepest that holds the second-largest store of ice on the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 57,000 people live there, most of them Inuit and nearly all in coastal towns on the ice-free southwest, the capital Nuuk the largest of them at roughly 19,000; fishing is the country's single most important trade and the interior is empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Norse farmers brought agriculture to the southern fjords in the late 10th century and had abandoned them by the early 15th, Denmark reasserted control in 1721, and the island won home rule in 1979 and self-government in 2009, remaining part of the Kingdom of Denmark after leaving the European Economic Community in 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Holocene": "The Holocene is the current epoch of geological time, beginning about 11,700 years ago as the last glacial phase ended, when the ice sheets drew back, sea level rose by tens of metres and forests spread over ground that had been tundra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate has been mild and unusually steady by the standards of the preceding two million years, and within it people took up farming independently in at least eleven parts of the world, built the first cities and states, and grew from a scattered few million to billions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Geologists divided it formally in 2018 into three ages, the Greenlandian, the Northgrippian and the Meghalayan, whose boundaries are fixed in Greenland ice cores and in a stalagmite from a cave in north-eastern India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Cryogenian": "The Cryogenian is a period of geological time lasting from roughly 720 to 635 million years ago, within the Neoproterozoic era and long before animals with hard skeletons appear in the rocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is named for the most severe glaciations known in the Earth's history, the Sturtian and the Marinoan, when glacial deposits formed even at tropical latitudes and ice may have reached almost around the planet, a state often called Snowball Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each freeze is thought to have ended as volcanic carbon dioxide built up in an atmosphere from which ice had shut off the usual weathering, and the thaws left distinctive carbonate layers in the rock record, followed within tens of millions of years by the first unmistakable animal fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Equator": "The equator is the imaginary circle drawn around the middle of the Earth, halfway between the poles, which splits the globe into a northern and a southern half and marks the zero point from which latitude is measured. It is the planet's longest line of latitude, about 40,075 km (24,901 miles) around, and because the Earth bulges outward there, ground on it stands some 21 km (13 miles) farther from the centre of the planet than the poles do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Along it the Sun passes almost directly overhead twice a year, day and night stay close to 12 hours long all year round, and the line crosses the territory of 13 countries, in 11 of them over land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Milankovitch_cycles": "Milankovitch cycles are the slow, regular changes in the Earth's orbit and axial tilt that alter how sunlight is spread over the planet through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three of them matter most: the orbit stretches and rounds again over roughly 100,000 years, the tilt of the axis rocks between about 22.1 and 24.5 degrees every 41,000 years, and the axis wobbles so that the seasons shift around the orbit over some 19,000 to 23,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Milutin Milanković calculated their effect on summer sunlight at 65 degrees north and argued that they pace the advance and retreat of ice sheets; his theory was ignored for some fifty years, until deep-sea sediment cores published in 1976 found those same rhythms in the record, and it is now central to explaining the ice ages, although it does not by itself account for the strength of the 100,000-year swings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sicily": "Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean, about 25,700 km² (9,920 sq mi) off the toe of the Italian peninsula across the narrow Strait of Messina, with about 4.8 million inhabitants and Palermo as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is hilly and dry, farmed for wheat, olives, citrus and vines, and its eastern side is dominated by Mount Etna, the most active volcano in Europe, whose summit stands at 3,357 m (11,010 feet) and whose recorded eruptions reach back to 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greeks and Phoenicians settled its coasts from the 8th century BCE, Carthage and Rome fought over it until it became Rome's first province in the 3rd century BCE, and Byzantines, Arabs, Normans and Spanish and Bourbon kings ruled it in turn before it joined the new Italian kingdom in 1860; it is today an autonomous region of Italy.",
"Northern_Hemisphere": "The Northern Hemisphere is the half of the Earth lying north of the equator, taking in all of Europe and North America, most of Asia and Africa and the northern part of South America. Land makes up about 39% of its surface, a high proportion for the planet, and it is home to roughly 87% of the world's people; the Arctic Ocean sits at its pole, ringed by the tundra and boreal forest of the far north. Summer there falls around June and winter around December, the Sun stands in the southern part of the sky at midday outside the tropics, and the Coriolis effect deflects moving air and water to the right, so that tropical cyclones spin anticlockwise while the great ocean gyres turn clockwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Southern_Hemisphere": "The Southern Hemisphere is the half of the Earth lying south of the equator, containing Antarctica and Australia, most of South America, about a third of Africa and wide stretches of the Pacific, Atlantic and Indian oceans. Only about 19% of its surface is land and roughly an eighth of the world's people live there, so ocean governs its weather and holds its temperatures to a narrower range through the year; an unbroken belt of water around 60° south carries the strongest sustained winds on the planet. Summer falls around December and winter around June, the Coriolis effect deflects moving air and water to the left, so that tropical cyclones spin clockwise while the great ocean gyres turn anticlockwise, and its night sky is oriented by constellations such as the Southern Cross, since no bright star marks the southern pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"George_Washington": "George Washington was an American planter and soldier who commanded the Continental Army in the Revolutionary War and became the first president of the United States, serving from 1789 to 1797.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He presided over the convention that drafted the Constitution in 1787 and, as president, shaped the practical workings of the new executive office, from the cabinet to the conduct of foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His decision to step down after two terms set a precedent that held for nearly a century and a half, and he remained the enslaver of hundreds of people until his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"John_Adams": "John Adams was a Massachusetts lawyer and political writer who argued for American independence, served as the first vice president, and was the second president of the United States from 1797 to 1801.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His single term was dominated by an undeclared naval conflict with France, which he settled by negotiation rather than full war, and by the Alien and Sedition Acts, which restricted immigrants and punished criticism of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was the first occupant of the White House and spent a long retirement writing letters, including a famous correspondence with Thomas Jefferson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Thomas_Jefferson": "Thomas Jefferson was an American statesman and the principal author of the Declaration of Independence, who served as the third president of the United States from 1801 to 1809.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His presidency roughly doubled the territory of the country through the Louisiana Purchase of 1803 and sent the Lewis and Clark expedition to the Pacific, while his second term was consumed by an embargo intended to keep the young republic out of the European wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A prolific writer, architect and founder of the University of Virginia, he also enslaved more than 600 people over the course of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"James_Madison": "James Madison was an American political thinker and the fourth president of the United States, in office from 1809 to 1817.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was the leading architect of the Constitution of 1787, a principal author of the Federalist essays written to win its ratification, and the drafter of the amendments that became the Bill of Rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As president he led the country through the War of 1812 against Britain, a conflict in which British troops burned the Capitol and the president's house in 1814 and which ended with no change of territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"James_Monroe": "James Monroe was an American statesman and the fifth president of the United States, serving from 1817 to 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A veteran of the Revolutionary War who had been a diplomat and secretary of state, he governed during a stretch of one-party dominance known as the Era of Good Feelings, broken by the financial panic of 1819 and by the fierce dispute over slavery that produced the Missouri Compromise of 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His annual message of 1823 set out what later became known as the Monroe Doctrine, warning European powers against new colonization in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"John_Quincy_Adams": "John Quincy Adams was an American diplomat and the sixth president of the United States, in office from 1825 to 1829.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As secretary of state he negotiated the treaty that transferred Florida from Spain and drafted much of the substance of the Monroe Doctrine, and he reached the presidency in 1825 when the House of Representatives chose him after no candidate won a majority of electoral votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Defeated after one term, he then spent seventeen years in the House of Representatives, attacking the rule that tabled antislavery petitions and arguing the <i>Amistad</i> case before the Supreme Court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Andrew_Jackson": "Andrew Jackson was an American general and the seventh president of the United States, in office from 1829 to 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Famous for defeating a British army at New Orleans in 1815, he built the coalition that became the Democratic Party and championed a politics of the common white man, expanding the reach of the presidency in a long fight against the Second Bank of the United States and in a confrontation with South Carolina over nullification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also signed the Indian Removal Act of 1830, under which tens of thousands of Native Americans were forced west on marches remembered as the Trail of Tears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Martin_Van_Buren": "Martin Van Buren was an American politician and the eighth president of the United States, in office from 1837 to 1841.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A New York party organizer of rare skill, he helped invent the modern national party machine and served as secretary of state and vice president before winning the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His single term was consumed by the Panic of 1837 and the long depression that followed, and he lost his campaign for re-election; in 1848 he ran again as the candidate of the Free Soil Party, which opposed extending slavery into the western territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"William_Henry_Harrison": "William Henry Harrison was an American army officer and the ninth president of the United States, whose term in 1841 lasted only 31 days, the shortest in the country's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had made his reputation as governor of Indiana Territory and as the commander who defeated Tecumseh's confederacy at Tippecanoe in 1811 and a combined British and Native American force at the Thames in 1813, and he won the presidency in a boisterous campaign built on that military fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fell ill soon after his inauguration and died in office, becoming the first president to do so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"John_Tyler": "John Tyler was an American politician and the tenth president of the United States, serving from 1841 to 1845.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was the first vice president raised to the office by a president's death, and by insisting that he was president in full rather than a caretaker he set a precedent later written into the Constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A states' rights Virginian who vetoed his own party's banking bills and was expelled from it, he secured the annexation of Texas in 1845 and, at the end of his life, sided with the Confederacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"James_K._Polk": "James K. Polk was an American politician and the eleventh president of the United States, in office from 1845 to 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Tennessee protégé of Jacksonian politics and former Speaker of the House, he entered office pledged to a single term and to a programme of expansion, and he carried it out: a treaty with Britain in 1846 fixed the Oregon boundary, while the war with Mexico from 1846 to 1848 brought California and the Southwest into the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory he added reopened the question of whether slavery would spread west, a dispute that dominated the following decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Zachary_Taylor": "Zachary Taylor was an American career soldier and the twelfth president of the United States, in office from 1849 until his death in 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He spent four decades in the army, commanded on the frontier, and became a national figure through victories in the war with Mexico, notably at Buena Vista in 1847, after which he was elected president with almost no political experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Although a Louisiana slaveholder, he resisted extending slavery into the newly won western territories, and he died suddenly in July 1850 while that quarrel was still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Millard_Fillmore": "Millard Fillmore was an American politician and the thirteenth president of the United States, in office from 1850 to 1853 after rising from the vice presidency on the death of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He signed the Compromise of 1850, a package of laws that admitted California as a free state and organised the western territories, but which also included a harsh Fugitive Slave Act that obliged citizens across the country to assist in the capture of escaped slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Denied his party's nomination in 1852, he ran again in 1856 as the candidate of the anti-immigrant Know Nothing movement and carried only one state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Franklin_Pierce": "Franklin Pierce was an American politician and the fourteenth president of the United States, in office from 1853 to 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A New Hampshire Democrat chosen as a compromise candidate, he sought to quiet the argument over slavery by enforcing existing law and looking abroad for expansion, buying a strip of land from Mexico in the Gadsden Purchase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead his support for the Kansas–Nebraska Act of 1854, which let settlers decide the question for themselves, unleashed violent struggle in Kansas and split his party so badly that it refused to renominate him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"James_Buchanan": "James Buchanan was an American diplomat and politician who served as the fifteenth president of the United States from 1857 to 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came to the office with a long record in Congress, as minister to Russia and Britain and as secretary of state, and he believed the quarrel over slavery could be settled by the courts and by admitting Kansas under a proslavery constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the winter after the election of 1860 seven southern states declared their secession while he was still in office, and he left the crisis unresolved for his successor; he was also the only president who never married.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Abraham_Lincoln": "Abraham Lincoln was an American lawyer and the sixteenth president of the United States, in office from 1861 until his assassination in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in a Kentucky log cabin and largely self-taught, he was elected on a pledge to halt the spread of slavery, and his election prompted seven southern states to secede and form the Confederacy, with four more joining once the fighting began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He led the Union through four years of fighting, issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863, defined the war's purpose in the Gettysburg Address, and pushed through the amendment abolishing slavery before being shot in Washington in April 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Andrew_Johnson": "Andrew Johnson was an American politician and the seventeenth president of the United States, in office from 1865 to 1869 after taking over on the assassination of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Tennessee tailor turned senator, he was the only southern senator to stay loyal to the Union in 1861, and as president he offered the defeated Confederate states lenient terms while resisting measures to protect the rights of freed people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His fights with Congress led the House of Representatives to impeach him in 1868, and the Senate acquitted him by a single vote; his administration also purchased Alaska from Russia in 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ulysses_S._Grant": "Ulysses S. Grant was an American general and the eighteenth president of the United States, in office from 1869 to 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As commanding general of the Union armies he pressed the campaigns that ended the Civil War and received the surrender of the main Confederate army at Appomattox in April 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As president he supported the amendment guaranteeing Black men the vote, used federal force to break the Ku Klux Klan, and pursued a peaceful settlement with Britain, while his two terms were also marked by corruption scandals among his appointees and by the depression that followed the panic of 1873.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Rutherford_B._Hayes": "Rutherford B. Hayes was an American lawyer and soldier who served as the nineteenth president of the United States from 1877 to 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wounded Civil War officer and governor of Ohio, he took office only after the disputed election of 1876 was settled by a special electoral commission, and whether a bargain with southern politicians went with it is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then ordered the last federal troops protecting Reconstruction governments back to their barracks, worked for a professional civil service against the wishes of party bosses, and kept a promise to serve only one term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"James_A._Garfield": "James A. Garfield was an American politician and the twentieth president of the United States, whose term in 1881 lasted only about six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A college teacher, Civil War general and long-serving congressman from Ohio, he was nominated unexpectedly at a deadlocked convention and won a very narrow popular majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was shot in a Washington railway station in July 1881 by a disappointed office-seeker and died in September of infection from his wounds, and public anger at the killing helped push Congress to reform the awarding of government jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chester_A._Arthur": "Chester A. Arthur was an American lawyer and party official who served as the twenty-first president of the United States from 1881 to 1885, having become president when his predecessor was assassinated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had risen through the New York political machine as collector of the port's customs house, and few expected reform from him, yet he signed the Pendleton Act of 1883, which created a merit examination system for federal jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His term also brought a modernised steel navy and the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, which barred Chinese labourers from immigrating, and ill health and party hostility kept him from a second nomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Grover_Cleveland": "Grover Cleveland was an American politician who served as the twenty-second and twenty-fourth president of the United States, from 1885 to 1889 and again from 1893 to 1897.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A former mayor of Buffalo and governor of New York, he built his reputation on honest administration and used the veto more than any earlier president, opposing what he saw as favours to private interests and defending the gold standard and low tariffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His second term opened with the financial panic of 1893 and the deep depression that followed, during which he sent federal troops to break the Pullman railway strike of 1894, a decision that divided his own party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Benjamin_Harrison": "Benjamin Harrison was an American lawyer and the twenty-third president of the United States, in office from 1889 to 1893.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Indiana senator and Civil War officer, and the grandson of the ninth president, he won the presidency in 1888 with a majority of electoral votes although he trailed in the popular count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His single term saw the first federal law against business monopolies, a steep protective tariff, a large expansion of pensions for Union veterans, and the admission of six new western states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"William_McKinley": "William McKinley was an American politician and the twenty-fifth president of the United States, in office from 1897 until his assassination in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An Ohio congressman known for high protective tariffs, he won office promising prosperity and put the currency firmly on a gold basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1898 he led the country into war with Spain, a brief conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and gave the United States the Philippines, Puerto Rico and Guam, while Hawaii was annexed the same year; six months into his second term he was shot by an anarchist in Buffalo and died eight days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Theodore_Roosevelt": "Theodore Roosevelt was an American politician, writer and naturalist who served as the twenty-sixth president of the United States from 1901 to 1909, taking office at 42 as the youngest person ever to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He used the presidency to break up large industrial combinations, regulate railways and food, and set aside vast tracts of forest and land as public reserves, while abroad he expanded the navy and secured the route for the Panama Canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1906 for mediating an end to the war between Russia and Japan, and ran again in 1912 on a third-party ticket after breaking with his successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"William_Howard_Taft": "William Howard Taft was an American lawyer and judge who served as the twenty-seventh president of the United States from 1909 to 1913 and later as chief justice of the Supreme Court from 1921 to 1930, the only person to hold both offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the presidency he had governed the Philippines and served as secretary of war, and in office he brought more antitrust suits than his predecessor, pressed for a presidential budget that Congress refused him, and encouraged American investment abroad in a policy nicknamed dollar diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A quarrel over tariffs and conservation split his party in 1912 and he finished third in that election, though he regarded his years on the court as the happiest of his career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Woodrow_Wilson": "Woodrow Wilson was an American scholar and politician who served as the twenty-eighth president of the United States from 1913 to 1921, having been a professor and president of Princeton University and governor of New Jersey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His first term produced the Federal Reserve system, a lower tariff, a federal income tax and new laws on trade and labour, and it also extended racial segregation within the federal service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He kept the country out of the First World War until 1917, then led it in, set out war aims in his Fourteen Points, and campaigned for the League of Nations, which the Senate refused to join after a stroke left him gravely weakened in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Warren_G._Harding": "Warren G. Harding was an American newspaper publisher and politician who served as the twenty-ninth president of the United States from 1921 until his death in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An Ohio senator with an easy manner, he won a landslide by promising a return to normal life after war and upheaval, then cut taxes and spending, raised tariffs, put the government's spending into a single presidential budget for the first time and hosted a naval conference in Washington that limited the size of the great powers' fleets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died suddenly in San Francisco in August 1923, and in the months that followed investigations exposed the Teapot Dome affair, in which his interior secretary had taken bribes for oil leases on public land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Calvin_Coolidge": "Calvin Coolidge was an American politician and the thirtieth president of the United States, in office from 1923 to 1929 after succeeding to the office on the death of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Vermont-born lawyer who became governor of Massachusetts, he was famous for his silence and thrift, and he governed on the belief that the government should tax and spend as little as possible while business ran the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His years coincided with the prosperity of the 1920s and included the immigration law of 1924, which sharply restricted arrivals from southern and eastern Europe, and a law granting citizenship to Native Americans; he chose not to run again in 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Herbert_Hoover": "Herbert Hoover was an American mining engineer and administrator who served as the thirty-first president of the United States from 1929 to 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had made a fortune in mining and won worldwide respect for organising food relief in Europe during and after the First World War, then served as secretary of commerce before winning the presidency by a wide margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven months into his term the stock market collapsed, and the Great Depression deepened through his presidency; he favoured voluntary cooperation and loans to banks and states over direct federal relief, signed a steep tariff in 1930 that worsened world trade, and lost the election of 1932 in a landslide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Franklin_D._Roosevelt": "Franklin D. Roosevelt was an American politician and the thirty-second president of the United States, in office from 1933 until his death in 1945 and the only person elected to the office four times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stricken by a paralytic illness in 1921 that left him unable to walk unaided, he entered the presidency in the depths of the Great Depression and answered it with the New Deal, a broad programme of relief, public works, banking regulation and social insurance that reshaped the federal government's role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then led the country through most of the Second World War, building the alliance against Germany and Japan, and died in April 1945 weeks before the fighting in Europe ended; a constitutional amendment soon limited presidents to two terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Harry_S._Truman": "Harry S. Truman was an American politician and the thirty-third president of the United States, in office from 1945 to 1953 after becoming president on the death of his predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Missouri farmer and shopkeeper who rose through the Senate, he took over months before the end of the Second World War, ordered the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, and oversaw the founding of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then shaped the early Cold War with aid to Greece and Turkey, the Marshall Plan for European recovery, the Berlin airlift and the creation of NATO, ordered the desegregation of the armed forces in 1948, and sent American troops to fight in Korea from 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Dwight_D._Eisenhower": "Dwight D. Eisenhower was an American general and the thirty-fourth president of the United States, in office from 1953 to 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As Supreme Commander of the Allied Expeditionary Force he planned and directed the invasion of Normandy in June 1944 and the campaign that defeated Germany, and he was sought as a candidate by both parties before running as a Republican.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As president he ended the fighting in Korea by armistice in 1953, cut spending on conventional forces while building up nuclear weapons to deter the Soviet Union, launched the interstate highway system in 1956, sent federal troops to enforce school desegregation at Little Rock in 1957, and warned in his farewell address of the growing power of a military-industrial complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"John_F._Kennedy": "John F. Kennedy was an American politician and the thirty-fifth president of the United States, in office from 1961 until his assassination in 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A decorated naval officer in the Pacific war and senator from Massachusetts, he was the youngest man elected to the office and the first Catholic to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His short presidency included the failed invasion of Cuba at the Bay of Pigs, the confrontation with the Soviet Union over missiles in Cuba in October 1962 that came close to nuclear war, the first nuclear test-ban treaty, the pledge to land astronauts on the Moon and a civil rights bill sent to Congress in 1963; he was shot dead in Dallas that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Lyndon_B._Johnson": "Lyndon B. Johnson was an American politician and the thirty-sixth president of the United States, in office from 1963 to 1969 after taking over on the assassination of his predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Texas schoolteacher turned congressman, he had been an unusually powerful Senate majority leader, and he used that mastery of legislation to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which dismantled legal segregation and protected Black voters, along with the Great Society programmes that created Medicare and Medicaid and expanded aid to schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the same time he greatly escalated the war in Vietnam, and the resulting division at home led him to announce in March 1968 that he would not seek another term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Richard_Nixon": "Richard Nixon was an American politician and the thirty-seventh president of the United States, in office from 1969 to 1974 and the only one to resign the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A California congressman and senator who had served eight years as vice president, he entered the White House in 1969 with ending the war in Vietnam as his most pressing task, and American forces left in 1973 under a negotiated agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His foreign policy reopened relations with China in 1972 and produced the first arms-limitation treaties with the Soviet Union, while at home his administration created the Environmental Protection Agency; the Watergate break-in and the cover-up that followed forced him to resign in August 1974 to avoid near-certain removal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Gerald_Ford": "Gerald Ford was an American politician and the thirty-eighth president of the United States, in office from 1974 to 1977, and the only person to hold the office without having been elected either president or vice president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A long-serving Michigan congressman and House minority leader, he was appointed vice president in 1973 under the constitutional provision for filling a vacancy, and became president the following year when his predecessor resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He granted that predecessor a full pardon a month later, a decision that cost him public support, and his short term was marked by high inflation and recession, the signing of the Helsinki Accords in 1975 and the fall of Saigon that same year; he narrowly lost the election of 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Jimmy_Carter": "Jimmy Carter was an American politician and the thirty-ninth president of the United States, in office from 1977 to 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A naval officer turned peanut farmer and governor of Georgia, he ran as an outsider promising honest government, and made human rights an explicit part of American foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His achievements included the Camp David Accords of 1978, which led to a peace treaty between Egypt and Israel, treaties transferring the Panama Canal, the creation of the energy and education departments and the protection of large areas of Alaska, while double-digit inflation and the seizure of American hostages in Iran cost him the election of 1980; he spent his long retirement on election monitoring, disease programmes and housebuilding, and received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Ronald_Reagan": "Ronald Reagan was an American film actor and politician who served as the fortieth president of the United States from 1981 to 1989, having been governor of California and, at 69, the oldest person elected to the office up to that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He cut income taxes sharply, pressed to roll back federal regulation and supported tight money to break double-digit inflation, presiding over a deep recession followed by a long expansion and by large federal deficits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Abroad he greatly increased military spending and confronted the Soviet Union, then negotiated with Mikhail Gorbachev and signed the 1987 treaty eliminating intermediate-range nuclear missiles; his second term was shadowed by the Iran-Contra affair, in which officials secretly sold arms to Iran and diverted the proceeds to rebels in Nicaragua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"George_H._W._Bush": "George H. W. Bush was an American politician and the forty-first president of the United States, in office from 1989 to 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A navy pilot shot down in the Pacific war, he later served as congressman, ambassador to the United Nations, director of central intelligence and vice president for eight years before winning the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His term coincided with the end of the Cold War: the Berlin Wall fell, Germany was reunified with his support, and the Soviet Union dissolved, while he assembled a broad coalition that drove Iraqi forces out of Kuwait in the war of 1991, and at home he signed a landmark law on the rights of disabled Americans and, facing large deficits, accepted a tax increase that angered his party, and he lost the election of 1992 amid a weak economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Bill_Clinton": "Bill Clinton is an American politician who served as the forty-second president of the United States from 1993 to 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A former governor of Arkansas, he presided over a long economic expansion in which the federal budget moved from deficit into surplus, and his record includes the North American Free Trade Agreement, a rise in the minimum wage, and a 1996 law that replaced the main federal cash welfare programme with block grants to the states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In foreign affairs he sent forces to Haiti and the Balkans and brokered the 1995 agreement that ended the war in Bosnia, and in 1998 the House of Representatives impeached him over his conduct in a lawsuit and an investigation; the Senate acquitted him in 1999 and he served out his term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"George_W._Bush": "George W. Bush is an American politician who served as the forty-third president of the United States from 2001 to 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A former governor of Texas and the son of the forty-first president, he won the very close election of 2000 after a disputed Florida count that the Supreme Court ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His presidency was defined by the attacks of 11 September 2001, after which he launched a war in Afghanistan, created a homeland security department and broad surveillance powers, and ordered the invasion of Iraq in 2003 on the basis of weapons claims that proved unfounded, and he also cut taxes, expanded federal testing in schools and drug coverage for the elderly, and began a large programme against AIDS in Africa, and his final months were dominated by the financial crisis of 2008 and the bank rescue that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Barack_Obama": "Barack Obama is an American politician who served as the forty-fourth president of the United States from 2009 to 2017 and was the first Black person elected to the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in Hawaii to a Kenyan father and an American mother, he taught law at the University of Chicago and served in the Illinois legislature and the Senate before winning the presidency during the financial crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He answered that crisis with a large stimulus package and a rescue of the car industry, signed the Affordable Care Act in 2010 extending health insurance to millions, and tightened regulation of banks; abroad he ordered the operation that killed Osama bin Laden in 2011, wound down the war in Iraq, restored relations with Cuba, and joined the Iran nuclear agreement and the Paris climate accord; he received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Donald_Trump": "Donald Trump is an American businessman and politician who served as the forty-fifth president of the United States from 2017 to 2021 and returned to the office as the forty-seventh president in 2025, the first person since the 19th century to win two non-consecutive terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A New York property developer and television personality with no previous public office, he won the 2016 election on a programme of restricting immigration, cutting taxes and repealing the Affordable Care Act, and his first term brought a large tax cut in 2017, three appointments to the Supreme Court and agreements normalising relations between Israel and several Arab states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was impeached twice by the House of Representatives, in 2019 over dealings with Ukraine and in 2021 over the attack on the Capitol, and was acquitted by the Senate on both occasions; he lost the 2020 election, disputed the result, and won again in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Joe_Biden": "Joe Biden is an American politician who served as the forty-sixth president of the United States from 2021 to 2025 and was the oldest person to take the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He represented Delaware in the Senate for 36 years, chairing its judiciary and foreign relations committees, and served two terms as vice president before winning the presidency in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In office he oversaw a mass vaccination campaign against COVID-19, signed a large relief package, a bipartisan infrastructure law, a semiconductor manufacturing act and a 2022 law directing major spending to clean energy, and organised Western military and economic support for Ukraine after the Russian invasion; he completed the withdrawal of American troops from Afghanistan in 2021, and in July 2024 he ended his campaign for a second term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Middle_Stone_Age": "The Middle Stone Age is the middle division of the African Stone Age, running from roughly 300,000 to about 40,000 years ago, with gradual boundaries that shift from one region to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its assemblages are marked by prepared cores, points and blades, the knapper shaping a block of stone so that a flake of planned size and edge came away in a single blow, and some of the earliest directly dated examples, about 315,000 years old, lay with the oldest known fossils of <i>Homo sapiens</i> at Jebel Irhoud in Morocco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name comes from a paper of 1928 by the archaeologist John Goodwin, elaborated the next year in <i>The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa</i>, his joint work with Clarence van Riet Lowe, which set out an Earlier, a Middle and a Later Stone Age for the continent in place of schemes that had leaned on European frameworks and imported French terminology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Skhul_Cave": "Skhul, in full Mugharet es-Skhul, is a small cave and rockshelter on the slope of Mount Carmel in northern Israel, standing in the Wadi el-Mughara or Valley of the Caves, also known as Nahal Me'arot, about 3 km (1.9 miles) inland from the Mediterranean shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American student Theodore McCown dug it for two months in 1931 and three months in 1932, most of the work covering the ground immediately in front of the shelter rather than the cave itself, as part of the seven seasons at the Mount Carmel caves that Dorothy Garrod led between 1929 and 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Middle Palaeolithic deposits, whose main phase of occupation is dated to roughly 130,000 to 100,000 years ago, yielded early <i>Homo sapiens</i> remains, of which at least four and possibly seven are interpreted as deliberate burials, one skeleton apparently clasping a wild boar's jaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Qafzeh_Cave": "Qafzeh is a cave on the slope of Har Qedumim, also called Jebel Qafzeh, in Lower Galilee in northern Israel, above the bank of the Nahal Mizra creek at the edge of the Jezreel Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was first excavated by René Neuville and Moshe Stekelis between 1933 and 1935, and dug again by Bernard Vandermeersch from 1965 to 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Middle Palaeolithic layers, dated to roughly 100,000 to 90,000 years ago, have produced the largest collection of hominin remains of that period in south-western Asia, 27 individuals in all, along with purposeful human burials, hearths, ochre and marine shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Skhul_and_Qafzeh_hominins": "The Skhul and Qafzeh hominins are the human fossils recovered from two caves in northern Israel, Skhul on Mount Carmel and Qafzeh in Lower Galilee, and they have been read as marking an early expansion of <i>Homo sapiens</i> out of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chronometric dating puts the main occupation at Skhul at about 130,000 to 100,000 years ago and Qafzeh at about 110,000 to 90,000, and the Skhul individuals are anatomically somewhat more primitive than those from Qafzeh, though the preserved faces of both are short and flat like our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of the dead were buried on purpose, one Skhul skeleton with a wild boar's jaw seemingly clasped in its arms and an adolescent of about 12 or 13 at Qafzeh laid on its back in a pit with two deer antlers on the chest, and both caves yielded perforated marine shells that bear the wear of hanging on a string and traces of ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Interglacial": "An interglacial is a warm phase of a glacial cycle, an interval of low land-ice extent and correspondingly high sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a sea-level definition eleven have been identified in the last 800,000 years, each typically lasting 10,000 to 30,000 years, and the onset of one involves rapid, non-linear responses of ice volume, carbon dioxide and temperature to astronomical forcing, while its end is a slower business, a global sequence of changes rather than a single switch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alternations of this kind, between cold glacial periods with ice on the northern continents and warmer intervals with little or none, first appeared about 2.6 million years ago at the start of the Quaternary, and the present interglacial is the Holocene, which began about 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Stone_Age": "The Late Stone Age is the last of the three divisions of the African Stone Age, running from about 40,000 years ago into historical times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest phase is generally informal, combining prepared cores with bipolar technique and microlithic work, and fully microlithic industries such as the Wilton appear only much later, between about 8,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three-stage scheme it belongs to was laid out by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe in 1929, and the label is applied well beyond southern Africa, reaching the hunter-gatherers of Taforalt in Morocco between 15,000 and 13,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Denisovan": "Denisovans are an extinct group of archaic humans named after Denisova Cave in southern Siberia, where the finger bone of a juvenile was excavated in 2008 and its genome sequenced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome showed a population sharing a common origin with Neanderthals, from whom they separated more than 390,000 years ago, and the two groups are known to have met: one girl whose remains come from the same cave had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They contributed some 4 to 6 percent of the genetic material of present-day Melanesians, which suggests the group may once have been widespread in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ochre": "Ochre is a blanket term for a variety of rocks characterised by a red or yellow colour or streak and containing a high proportion of iron oxides, taking in everything from soil lumps to ore minerals and from shale and siltstone to hardened clay and weathered dolerite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yellow ochres usually derive their colour from goethite and red ochres from haematite, and both often contain other components such as quartz, clays, gypsum or mica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologically it survives as pieces modified by grinding, flaking, scraping and pitting to produce a red powder, and it was also an ingredient of compound adhesives, mixed with plant gum, used to haft stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Blombos_Cave": "Blombos Cave is a South African site set in an ancient wave-cut cliff formed in calcified sediments of the Bredasdorp Group, about 100 m (330 feet) from the Indian Ocean and 34.5 m (113 feet) above sea level, extending some 6 to 7 metres (20 to 23 feet) from its drip line to the back wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> First excavated in 1991, its Middle Stone Age sequence runs more than 4 metres (13 feet) deep and falls into three phases, the earliest dated by optically stimulated luminescence and uranium-thorium to between 101,000 and 94,000 years ago, with Still Bay layers in a maximum range of about 78,000 to 71,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among its finds are 41 pierced marine tick shells covered in red ochre and two engraved ochres, and an abalone shell used to store and mix a red ochre compound of ochre, bone, charcoal, quartz and quartzite, with a quartzite cobble fitted into it as grinder and percussor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Tar": "Tar is an adhesive synthesised by the dry, or destructive, distillation of organic material, commonly birch bark or pine wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making it needs no pottery: experiments have produced usable quantities by heating rolled bark under a mound of embers and ash, in a pit, or inside a raised earthen structure, at temperatures ranging from about 250 °C (480 °F) to over 500 °C (930 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the earliest transformative technologies known, at least 200,000 years old, and the oldest evidence consists of two unretouched flakes partly covered in birch bark tar from Campitello in Italy, with a minimum age of 191,000 years, made at a time when only Neanderthals were present in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Eurasia": "Eurasia is the landmass made up of Europe and Asia, and one of the six commonly recognised geological continents, along with Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica and Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those continents are spatially isolated by geologic or bathymetric features, and tectonic plate boundaries provide the basis for continent-continent boundaries such as the one between Africa and Eurasia, so the customary line drawn along the Ural Mountains is a boundary of convention rather than of geology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hominins reached it early: cut-marked bones from Grăunceanu in Romania are dated to more than 1.95 million years ago, and hominins were present at Dmanisi in Georgia by 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sulawesi": "Sulawesi is the largest island of Wallacea, about 174,000 km² (67,200 sq mi), a biogeographically distinct zone of oceanic islands lying between continental Asia and Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lowland tower-karst region of some 450 km² (170 sq mi) on its south-western peninsula holds around 300 caves and shelters with rock art on their walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranium-series analysis of a small speleothem overlying a painted pig at Leang Tedongnge gave that image a minimum age of 45,500 years, which its excavators called the earliest known representational work of art in the world, though a narrative scene at Leang Karampuang has since been dated to at least 51,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Swabian_Jura": "The Swabian Jura is an upland region of south-western Germany whose caves have produced the oldest portable art objects and musical instruments of humankind, and six of them — Geissenklösterle, Sirgenstein and Hohle Fels in the Ach valley, Bockstein, Hohlenstein and Vogelherd in the Lone valley — were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its early Aurignacian dates to around 42,000 years ago on the radiocarbon record, and the ivory figurines from these caves are the oldest portable art known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the finds are a lion-headed human figure 31.1 cm (12.2 inches) tall, carved from the tusk of a mammoth and recovered from the Stadel cave in the Hohlenstein, and a flute 21.7 cm (8.54 inches) long made from the bone of a griffon vulture, with four preserved finger holes, found at Hohle Fels in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Chauvet_Cave": "Chauvet-Pont d'Arc is a decorated Upper Palaeolithic cave in the Ardèche, in southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was found on 18 December 1994 by Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel and Christian Hillaire, who felt a cool draught coming out of the rock and dug their way through to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A high-precision chronological model places human presence there in two phases, an Aurignacian one about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago and a Gravettian one about 31,000 to 28,000, which makes its drawings among the oldest known, although a minority of specialists dispute that chronology on stylistic grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lascaux": "Lascaux is a decorated cave in the Dordogne, in south-western France, discovered on 12 September 1940 by four boys, two of whom became its guides once it was opened to visitors in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is traditionally divided into seven sectors — the Hall of the Bulls, the Axial Gallery, the Passageway, the Nave, the Chamber of the Felines, the Apse and the Shaft — and carries some 600 representations of animals, images of horses by far the most numerous, followed by equal numbers of stags and aurochs and then by ibexes and bison, with a single human figure among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dates measured on reindeer bone put the occupation between 21,500 and 21,000 years before present, and the cave, recognised by UNESCO in 1979, has been shut to tourists since 1963, visitor numbers of up to about 1,800 a day having altered its temperature, light and carbon dioxide and brought on successive outbreaks of green, white and black staining on its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cave_of_Altamira": "The Cave of Altamira lies at Santillana del Mar in Cantabria, in northern Spain, and its painted ceiling was the first Palaeolithic cave art the world had to argue about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranium-series dates on the calcite crusts over its images show a sequence spanning more than 20,000 years, from a red sign of at least about 35,550 years ago, in the Aurignacian, to Magdalenian work near the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authenticity was rejected by the leading prehistorians of the day, and Émile Cartailhac only recanted in 1902, in an article he titled <i>Mea culpa d'un sceptique</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sungir": "Sungir is an open-air Upper Palaeolithic camp on the Klyazma river at the north-eastern edge of Vladimir, about 197 km (122 miles) east of Moscow, found in the 1950s and dug by Otto Bader between 1957 and 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its graves are the most elaborate Upper Palaeolithic burials currently known: one man lay under about 3,000 mammoth ivory beads with twelve pierced fox canines across his forehead and 25 ivory arm bands, while two children of about 10 and 12 lay head to head under roughly 5,000 and 5,400 more, beside 16 ivory spears, five of them double-pointed and ranging from 0.27 to 2.47 m (11 in to 8 ft 1 in).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burials date to about 34,000 calendar years ago, and the genomes of four of the dead show that none was a close relative of another, not even the two children sharing a grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aurignacian": "The Aurignacian is an Early Upper Palaeolithic technocomplex of Europe, dated to circa 42,000 to 32,000 calendar years ago and subdivided into a Proto- and an Early Aurignacian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is characterised by distinct lithic and bone technologies and tool types, and the ivory figurines from its levels in the Swabian Jura, where the early Aurignacian dates to around 42,000 years ago on the radiocarbon record, are the oldest portable art known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people painted as well as carved: the earlier of the two phases of human presence at Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago, is Aurignacian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gravettian": "The Gravettian is an Upper Palaeolithic technocomplex dated to circa 34,000 to 24,000 calendar years ago and found in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is characterised by distinct lithic and bone technologies and tool types, is often described as having numerous regional variants, or facies, and is marked by an abundance of elaborately buried individuals across its geographic range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its portable art includes the small female figurines conventionally called Venuses, among them the limestone statuette from Willendorf in Austria, which is roughly 30,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solutrean": "The Solutrean is an Upper Palaeolithic industry confined to south-western France and the Iberian Peninsula and occurring within a moderately short chronological range, circa 25,000 to 19,000 calendar years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That timespan roughly matches the course of the Last Glacial Maximum, the coldest stretch of the last glacial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its defining innovation is a suite of techniques for shaping stone points by unifacial and bifacial invasive flat retouch, which leaves very fine grazing removals with no clear point of impact and yields foliate, shouldered and tanged forms, above all the laurel leaf, with pressure flaking entering only at the final stage of shaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Magdalenian": "The Magdalenian is the last Upper Palaeolithic industry of western Europe, running from roughly 21,000 to 14,000 years before present and taking its name from the rock shelter of La Madeleine in the Dordogne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is marked by the richness of its toolwork in bone and cervid antler and by the abundance of its art, and its barbed harpoon heads change steadily through the sequence, from very small teeth to a single row of barbs and then to two, which is why archaeologists use them to place a site within the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Magdalenian groups spread north as the ice drew back, and Gough's Cave in Somerset records their presence in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Western_Europe": "Western Europe is a conventional grouping of countries in the western part of the European continent, and the United Nations standard for statistical use places nine in it — Austria, Belgium, France, Germany, Liechtenstein, Luxembourg, Monaco, the Netherlands and Switzerland — while stating that such assignments are for statistical convenience and imply no assumption about the political affiliation of the countries concerned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In prehistory the term marks out a region with a sequence of Upper Palaeolithic industries of its own, and ancient genomes connect the human groups that lived there before and after the Last Glacial Maximum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A 23,000-year-old individual from Cueva del Malalmuerzo in Granada, in southern Iberia, links those earlier and later populations and supports reading Iberia as a refugium through the coldest phase of the last glacial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bronze": "In archaeometallurgical literature bronze is a copper alloy which mostly consists of tin, made in antiquity by adding tin to copper to improve the mechanical and chemical characteristics of the metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tin makes copper more fluid and easier to cast, and at about 10 per cent it makes the metal harder and stronger than additions of arsenic or zinc do, with deliberate alloying usually taken to be indicated by a tin content above 1 per cent, or in some cases above 5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alloy became known in the late 4th and early 3rd millennium BCE in Mesopotamia and western Iran, but its spread was uneven: much of sub-Saharan Africa, the arctic tundra, island Southeast Asia and Oceania had no bronze metallurgy until late in their history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iron": "Iron was won from its ores in a bloomery, where charcoal was burned as a source of heat and carbon to provide the temperature and the reducing atmosphere needed to turn the ore into metal, giving a spongy mass called a bloom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gangue minerals in the ore, semi-reduced iron oxides and furnace materials formed slag, which had to be driven out by repeated heating and hammering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holding the metal at high temperature in a carbon-rich reducing atmosphere carburised it into steel, graded by carbon content as low, medium at 0.3 to 0.4 per cent, high above 0.8 and ultra-high above 1.2; in much of sub-Saharan Africa iron production begins early and without a preceding age of bronze at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chronology": "A chronology is an ordering of events in time, and it is essential for all archaeologies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One that draws on every strand of dating information available — radiocarbon measurements, stratigraphy, coins, typology — is bound to be more reliable than one resting on a single strand, and Bayesian modelling is the method by which those strands are combined into a single estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The advent of radiocarbon dating rewrote archaeological chronologies around the world, and calibration followed; where a boundary has to be fixed exactly, it is defined at a chosen point in a reference section, as the base of the Holocene is at 11,700 calendar years before AD 2000 in a Greenland ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Swabia": "Swabia is a historical region of south-western Germany named after the Suebi, who settled there and who from the 6th to the 12th century were increasingly identified with the Alemanni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name first denoted their settlement land, the stem duchy of Alemannia, and then the duchy of Swabia that grew out of it, a province with fixed frontiers against Bavaria to the east, Franconia to the north, Italy to the south and Burgundy to the south-west, which the duke had to defend against his neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The duchy was extinguished for good with the execution of Conradin in 1268, and the name now covers parts of Baden-Württemberg together with the Bavarian administrative district of Bavarian Swabia, consolidated in 1803 as the Bavarian Province of Swabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Scandinavia": "Scandinavia is a region of northern Europe whose human history begins late, because the ice of the last glacial period had to withdraw before people could follow it north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pioneers arrive in southern Scandinavia in the Late Palaeolithic and are grouped into the Hamburg, Bromme and Ahrensburg cultures, the Hamburg people hunting reindeer in an Arctic tundra landscape and the Ahrensburgians following the retreating ice between about 10,700 and 9000 cal BCE, while an ice sheet blocked any passage between the Oslofjord and Sweden until roughly 9000 cal BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming societies followed, and the Late Neolithic of southern Scandinavia, from 2350 to 1700 BCE, is marked by the reintroduction of metal, copper alloys in particular, and by the sudden spread of elaborate bifacial flint tools and weapons, of which the daggers are the outstanding objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jens_Jacob_Worsaae": "Jens Jacob Asmussen Worsaae, born at Vejle on 14 March 1821 and dead at Hagestedgård near Holbæk on 15 August 1885, was a Danish archaeologist who took Christian Jürgensen Thomsen's classification of antiquities by material and put it to the test against contextual evidence from stratigraphic excavations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the antiquities commission in 1841, was appointed inspector for the preservation of antiquities in 1847 after a journey through the British Isles, succeeded Thomsen as director of the collections in 1866, and served as minister for church and education from 1874 until he withdrew in 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His <i>Danmarks Oldtid, oplyst ved Oldsager og Gravhøie</i> of 1843, translated six years later as <i>The Primeval Antiquities of Denmark</i>, carried into Britain the same division of the antiquities into three classes, those belonging to the periods of Stone, and Bronze, and Iron, that had been adopted in arranging the Danish monuments, and what his testing established was a method as much as a sequence: prehistory became a discipline defined by typology, stratigraphy and technology rather than by written texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hesiod": "Hesiod is an early Greek poet who put himself into his own verse: he came from Ascra, a small village in Boeotia, to which his father had migrated from Cyme on the coast of Asia Minor, and he quarrelled with his brother Perses over their inheritance, accusing him of laziness and injustice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two poems are his, the <i>Theogony</i>, which opens with the Muses of Mount Helicon initiating him as a shepherd and telling him they know how to say many falsehoods that look like genuine things but can also proclaim true things, and the <i>Works and Days</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Works and Days</i> sets out five successive races of men — gold, silver, bronze, a god-like race of heroes, and the iron race the poet puts himself in — each worse than the one before, a scheme of moral decline in which the fourth is not a metal at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Oceania": "Oceania is the region of the Pacific which the United Nations standard for statistical use divides into four parts: Australia and New Zealand, Melanesia, Micronesia and Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Melanesia takes in Fiji, New Caledonia, Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu; Micronesia takes in Guam, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands, the Federated States of Micronesia, Nauru, the Northern Mariana Islands and Palau; and Polynesia takes in American Samoa, the Cook Islands, French Polynesia, Niue, Pitcairn, Samoa, Tokelau, Tonga, Tuvalu and Wallis and Futuna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such groupings are made for statistical convenience and imply no assumption about political affiliation, and the region is one of those where the conventional sequence of ages fits badly, since it had no bronze metallurgy until fairly late in its history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mesopotamia": "Mesopotamia is the land of the Tigris and Euphrates, a river system rising in the Taurus Mountains and running parallel to the Zagros, which built the vast low-gradient alluvial plain of southern or lower Mesopotamia out of its own sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest network of city-states emerged there about 5,000 years ago, knit together by shared cultural traditions and economic interests, and during the Uruk period settlement grew denser and more hierarchical around urban institutions, with Uruk itself passing 250 hectares (620 acres) and at least 25,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing appears in the region toward the end of the 4th millennium BCE, which is the boundary conventionally drawn between prehistory and history, though the traditional date of 3200 BCE is itself more or less a convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chert": "Chert is a silica-rich sedimentary rock built of cryptocrystalline quartz, so fine-grained that the crystals cannot be resolved by eye; the chert studied in one comparison of knapping stones proved to be composed entirely of length-fast chalcedony, which is itself quartz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rocks of this kind — chert, flint, silicified shale and other silica-rich stones — were the ones commonly chosen for making stone tools, which is presumed to be due to their predictable fracturing properties and good knapping qualities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quality is commonly related to the mineralogical structure and purity of a given material, and stones whose mechanical properties are the same in every direction are generally reckoned better for toolmaking; where such rock lies in chalk it has been quarried into modern times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Flint": "Flint is one of the silica-rich sedimentary rocks — with chert, silicified shale and their relations — that were commonly chosen for making stone tools, a preference presumed to be due to their predictable fracturing properties and good knapping qualities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its working quality is commonly related to the mineralogical structure and purity of the material, grain size and shape among them, and stones whose mechanical properties are the same in every direction are generally reckoned the better ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is dug where it lies in chalk, and the trade outlasted the Stone Age by thousands of years: at Brandon in Suffolk gun-flints were still being struck by 26 men and boys working in four shops in April 1878, a manufacture the Geological Survey described as carried on continuously from what geologists called Neolithic times down to the present day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Obsidian": "Obsidian is a naturally forming glass, a type of igneous rock, produced only by the rapid cooling of lava; it is found in volcanic areas where the magma is rich in silica and the lava has cooled without forming crystals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is often jet black but can also be brown, tan or green, and although it is a strong material it can be easily shaped by chipping away flakes in the process called knapping, giving an edge that can be honed exceptionally thin — so thin that a modern obsidian scalpel cuts between cells rather than tearing through tissue as a steel one does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was quarried for toolmaking at Obsidian Cliff in Yellowstone more than 11,000 years ago, and carried extraordinary distances: obsidian from there and from Bear Gulch in Idaho reached earthworks in the Scioto River Valley of Ohio, over 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Conchoidal_fracture": "Conchoidal fracture is the smooth, shell-like way a homogeneous brittle solid breaks under a blow, and in the study of stone tools it is treated as Hertzian fracture, the mechanics worked out for a load pressed onto an elastic surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glass is the standard medium in knapping experiments precisely because it fractures conchoidally, and a hard-hammer blow leaves a clear point of initiation and a well-formed bulb of percussion where a soft hammer, working by bending fracture, leaves a more diffused one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rocks that break this way — the silica-rich sedimentary stones and their relations — are the ones commonly chosen for toolmaking, presumed to be due to their predictable fracturing properties, and materials with the same mechanical properties in every direction are generally reckoned the better ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Handaxe": "A handaxe is a large cutting tool made by striking a big flake and then shaping it with smaller flakes struck around the edges, a technique early humans began using by about 1.76 million years ago; handaxes and other large cutting tools are what characterise the Acheulean toolkit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their distribution underlies the so-called Movius Line, drawn out of Hallam Movius's work in the 1940s, and their frequent symmetry has been argued to be an indicator of good genes displayed in courtship, a proposal that has received credible criticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they were for is still argued about: experiments show bifaces capable of numerous woodworking activities, butchery trials found no strong correlation between symmetry and effectiveness at butchery, and their suitability for digging appears to have been largely overlooked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Debitage": "Debitage is the material detached from a core in the course of knapping, taken together — the flakes and the debris of percussion flaking rather than the finished tool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In descriptions of a prepared-core industry the word covers both the flakes and the cores they came from, as when an assemblage is characterised by tanged tools, Levallois and micro-Levallois debitage, and side-scrapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not waste to an archaeologist but evidence, and it can be measured against the pieces the knapper was aiming at: preferential Levallois flakes proved thicker across their surface area as a whole, and of relatively even thickness, when compared with the debitage flakes struck alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Gunflint": "A gunflint is a shaped piece of flint held in the lock of a firearm, which crashes down on a steel plate so that the resulting shower of red-hot sparks ignites gunpowder held in a shallow pan on the side of the lock and sets off the main charge inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making them kept flint knapping alive as a trade long after the Stone Age: at Brandon in Suffolk 26 men and boys were still at work in four shops in April 1878, having been 36 in 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Geological Survey, publishing its memoir on the industry that year, described a manufacture continuously carried on from what geologists have called Neolithic times down to the present day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Musket": "A musket is a long-barrelled shoulder firearm, and in its flintlock form it employed a simple firing mechanism relying on a piece of flint crashing down on a steel plate, the resulting shower of red-hot sparks igniting gunpowder held in a shallow pan on the side of the lock and setting off the main charge inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Loading it was a drill rather than an action: the sequence prescribed for United States troops in the middle of the 19th century ran to twelve separate motions, and the Mexican drill to eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ignition depended on a knapped stone, so it kept a Stone Age craft in industrial employment, and the Geological Survey could describe gunflint making in 1879 as a manufacture carried on continuously from what geologists have called Neolithic times down to the present day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Subsistence": "Subsistence is how a society gets its living from its surroundings, whether by hunting, fishing and gathering or by agriculture and livestock farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is reconstructed from what people left behind and from what they were made of: use-wear showing that crescent-shaped sickles were mainly used for harvesting cereals, charred cereal remains, the bones of game animals, pollen records of crop rotation and fallow, stable isotopes indicating a low intake of marine food, and even dental caries, whose commonness tracks the carbohydrate in a cereal diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not only a question of how much food there is but of how that food is distributed, since resources that are clumped and can be defended — salmon runs above all — underwrote a class system of hereditary nobility, commoners and slaves among foragers who did no farming at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Salmon": "Salmon are migratory fish that gather in great numbers on their spawning runs, and that predictability made them one of the most important wild foods in prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the most commonly depicted fish in Magdalenian art, clearly identifiable from their distinctive features and occasionally shown in enough detail for the species to be named, and stable isotopes from Upper Palaeolithic human remains suggest that aquatic resources were contributing up to half of dietary protein from the middle of the period onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because a run is both abundant and defensible, it can underwrite social forms that farming is usually credited with: along the North Pacific coast, foragers who grew nothing had a class system of hereditary nobility, commoners and slaves, and salmon above all is the resource behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Geology": "Geology is the science of the Earth: the processes that have shaped current and past landscapes, and the way the planet's diverse range of rocks and minerals formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its subject is old — the Earth has been cooling down since it formed 4.6 billion years ago — and its branches run from erosion, deposition and weathering through earthquakes, volcanoes and landslides to fossils and geological time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the discipline needs a boundary in that time to be exact it does not argue from definitions but drives a marker into a rock face, agreeing a point in a chosen section that all later work refers to, as the base of the Quaternary was fixed at Monte San Nicola in Sicily and ratified in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Geological_epoch": "A geological epoch is a formally defined division of the geological timescale, one rank below a period, and the rock body corresponding to it is called a series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its base is fixed by a Global Stratotype Section and Point, a marker agreed at a chosen level in a chosen exposure: the Pleistocene's was moved to Monte San Nicola in Sicily, dated to 2.58 million years ago, when the International Union of Geological Sciences ratified the change on 29 June 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A boundary need not be in rock at all — the base of the Holocene was placed at 1492.45 m (4,896 feet) in the NGRIP Greenland ice core, at 11,700 calendar years before AD 2000, the first such point defined in an ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cenozoic_Era": "The Cenozoic Era is the most recent era of geological time, and the Quaternary Period, with its Pleistocene and Holocene epochs, is the last division within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the interval across which the planet cooled into its present state: carbon dioxide fell from something like 1,000 parts per million in the Early Cenozoic to between 170 and 300 in the Pleistocene, a change driven by shifting plate tectonics, which control both the volcanic emissions that release the gas and the weathering that removes it by exposing fresh rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cooling produced the large-scale glaciation of Antarctica first and then, in the most recent 3 to 5 million years, the growth of the Northern Hemisphere ice sheets, leaving the great ice sheets of Greenland and Antarctica still in place today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Stratum": "A stratum is a distinguishable layer of deposit, and a sequence of them read in order is the oldest tool either geology or archaeology has for telling what came before what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geology fixes its formal time boundaries inside such sequences, agreeing a level in a chosen exposure — the base of the Quaternary sits in the section at Monte San Nicola in Sicily, and the base of the Holocene at a measured depth in a Greenland ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeology uses the same relations as evidence rather than as definition: a chronology that draws on all the dating information available, radiocarbon measurements and stratigraphy and coins and typology together, is bound to be more reliable than one resting on a single strand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Beringia": "Beringia is the region of the Bering Strait and the lands on either side of it, which stood dry during the last glacial sea-level lowstand: the strait is only about 53 m (170 feet) deep, so it was exposed as a land bridge and permitted human migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was flooded about 11,000 calendar years ago as the sea came back up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While it stood it carried the mammoth steppe, an open grassland across which the woolly mammoth ranged; the mainland populations declined about 12,900 years ago in North America and in the early Holocene in northern Asia, under a mixture of habitat change and human hunting, while island populations lasted to about 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Siberia": "Siberia is the great northern expanse of Asia, and its cold has made it the richest source of ancient DNA on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A finger bone of a juvenile excavated in 2008 at Denisova Cave, in the south of the region, was sequenced to reveal the Denisovans, a population sharing a common origin with Neanderthals and contributing genetic material to present-day Melanesians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the north-east a mammoth specimen from Krestovka, 1.2 to 1.1 million years old, held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from an animal, and the same deposits record the steppe mammoth lineage turning into the woolly mammoth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Alaska": "Alaska is the far north-western extremity of North America, divided from Siberia by the Bering Strait, which is only about 53 m (170 feet) deep and stood dry as a land bridge through the last glacial sea-level lowstand until it flooded about 11,000 calendar years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A terminal Pleistocene genome recovered there revealed the first founding population of Native Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its offshore islands held mammoths long after the mainland herds were gone: on St. Paul they survived until 5,600 years ago, give or take a century, and what finally killed them was a shortage of fresh water as the island shrank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mammoth": "Mammoths are extinct proboscideans of the genus <i>Mammuthus</i>, whose body sizes have been estimated by volumetric methods tested against living elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their deep history has been read straight out of ancient DNA: a specimen from Krestovka in north-eastern Siberia, 1.2 to 1.1 million years old, held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from an animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same sequences catch one species turning into another, the Adycha steppe lineage of about a million years ago being directly ancestral to woolly mammoths while the Chukochya specimen of about 700,000 years ago already is one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Woolly_mammoth": "The woolly mammoth, <i>Mammuthus primigenius</i>, stood about 2.7 to 3.5 m (8 ft 10 in to 11 ft 6 in) at the shoulder in its north Siberian form and weighed some 5.5 to 8 tonnes (6.1 to 8.8 tons), with small ears of around 30 cm (12 inches) weighing under a kilogram (2 pounds), a very short tail and a thick coat of hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its country was the mammoth steppe, an open grassland running across Beringia, and the mainland populations declined about 12,900 years ago in North America and in the early Holocene in northern Asia, under a mixture of habitat change and human hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Island populations lasted far longer: the Wrangel Island animals were cut off about 10,000 years ago and died out about 4,000 years ago after more than 200 stable generations, showing inbreeding depression without a genomic meltdown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Elephant": "Elephants are the surviving proboscideans, and genomics has settled that the African ones are two species and not one: forest elephants comprise a lineage distinct from savanna elephants, the two having split from each other some 5 to 2 million years ago, soon after their common ancestor split from the straight-tusked elephant lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Despite hybridisation where their ranges meet, for conservation purposes forest and savanna elephants are appropriately viewed as reproductively distinct units, meeting the definition of the biological species concept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Asian elephant is not their near relative but the mammoths': Asian elephants and mammoths are estimated to have split at about the same time as the split between <i>Loxodonta</i> and the straight-tusked elephants, and the volumetric methods used to weigh extinct proboscideans are calibrated against living elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Smilodon": "<i>Smilodon</i> is a genus of saber-toothed cat of the Americas, one of the genera placed in the machairodontine subfamily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two of its species are much studied and both originate in North America: <i>S. fatalis</i>, with fossil records through the whole American continent, and <i>S. populator</i>, whose records are restricted to South America and which was the more widely distributed of the two there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>S. fatalis</i> is known above all from the asphalt seeps of Rancho La Brea in southern California, which gave it a superb fossil record: its upper canines had crowns about 130 mm (5.1 inches) high and erupted at some 6 mm (0.24 inches) a month, finishing at about 34 to 41 months, and dental microwear puts it closer to the African lion than to a strict flesh specialist, with little evidence that declining prey caused the extinction it shared with the rest of the megafauna about 12,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Last_Glacial_Maximum": "The Last Glacial Maximum is the interval when the ice sheets of the last glacial period stood at their greatest extent, holding those positions between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago — a lowstand plateau some 7,500 years long rather than a single peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So much water was locked into ice that global sea level fell by roughly 130 m (430 feet), with estimates constrained for glacial isostatic adjustment running from 130 to 134 m (430 to 440 feet) below the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Laurentide, Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets all reached their limits then, the Laurentide standing some 3 to 4 km (1.9 to 2.5 miles) thick over the Hudson Bay dome, and deglaciation began 19,000 to 20,000 years ago as summer insolation in the Northern Hemisphere rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Stratigraphy": "Stratigraphy is the study of layered deposits and of the order in which they were laid down, and it is one of the methods by which prehistory became a discipline defined by typology, stratigraphy and technology rather than by written texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On an excavation those relationships are recorded formally — single-context recording and the construction of Harris matrices are standard professional practice — so that a great many sites already carry a large quantity of relative dating information before any sample is measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That information is not superseded by scientific dating but combined with it, since a chronology relying on all the dating evidence available, radiocarbon dates and stratigraphy and coins and typology together, is bound to be more reliable than one relying on a single strand; where geology needs a time boundary to be exact it fixes it inside such a sequence, as the base of the Quaternary is fixed at Monte San Nicola in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Laurentide_ice_sheet": "The Laurentide Ice Sheet was the great ice sheet of North America, known to have initiated over the Arctic and sub-Arctic plateaux of eastern Canada and reaching its maximum extent towards the end of the glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconstructions put it at some 3 to 4 km (1.9 to 2.5 miles) thick over the Hudson Bay dome, with ice-sheet models simulating 4 to 5 km (2.5 to 3.1 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stood at its greatest extent through the Last Glacial Maximum, between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago, alongside the Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets, and the Northern Hemisphere ice of that reconstruction amounts to about 100 m (330 feet) of sea-level equivalent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Fennoscandian_ice_sheet": "The Fennoscandian Ice Sheet was the ice sheet centred on northern Europe, one of the Northern Hemisphere sheets that stood at their limits through the Last Glacial Maximum, between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the dominant part of the Eurasian ice sheet, which underwent the greatest change in area of any Northern Hemisphere sheet between time slices, growing by over 1,000 per cent at the glacial maximum relative to the warmer intervals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its retreat has been mapped in calendar-year isochrons every 1,000 years from 22 to 13 thousand years ago and every 100 years from 11.6 to the final decay of the ice after 9.7 thousand years ago, with an ice-sheet-wide interruption in the retreat during the Younger Dryas between 12.7 and 11.6 thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Wisconsin_glaciation": "The Wisconsin glaciation is the name given to the last glaciation of North America, the advance of the Laurentide Ice Sheet that initiated over the Arctic and sub-Arctic plateaux of eastern Canada and reached its maximum extent towards the end of the glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the state it is named after, the late Wisconsin ice reached its greatest extent between about 18,000 and 15,000 years ago, and permafrost persisted in southern Wisconsin until roughly 14,000 years ago and in the north of the state until about 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its close belongs to the wider Last Glacial Maximum, when the Laurentide stood alongside the Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Doggerland": "Doggerland is the name given, after Bryony Coles in 1998, to the Mesolithic landscape of the southern North Sea, now under water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything known of it has been dredged up, washed ashore or read off seismic data: oil-industry three-dimensional surveys have been reworked into palaeolandscape maps over more than 23,000 km² (8,900 sq mi), and the first find to be recognised was a barbed red-deer antler harpoon brought up in 1931 from the Leman and Ower banks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Storegga tsunami struck it about 8,150 years ago, but the argument since 2020 has been that inexorable sea-level rise, rather than that single wave, took the last of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Great_Britain": "Great Britain is the official collective name of England, Scotland and Wales and their associated islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not include Northern Ireland and should therefore never be used interchangeably with the United Kingdom, whose full name — the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland — refers to the political union of all four; the British Isles is a purely geographical term covering the islands of Great Britain and Ireland together with some 5,000 smaller ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its human history is broken by the ice: after the last glaciation Magdalenian groups moved north as the sheets drew back, and Gough's Cave in Somerset records their presence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Woolly_rhinoceros": "The woolly rhinoceros, <i>Coelodonta antiquitatis</i>, was a cold-adapted megaherbivore, abundant from western Europe to north-east Siberia through the Middle and Late Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genus originated north of the Himalayan–Tibetan uplift around 2.5 million years ago and spread westwards into Europe, the earliest documented immigration being about 460,000 to 400,000 years ago, and its European mitochondrial lineage proves long separated from the Siberian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of the animals of the mammoth steppe, the relatively homogeneous steppe–tundra flora that dominated the Arctic at the Last Glacial Maximum, alongside the woolly mammoth and the steppe bison, and its demise begins about 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cave_bear": "The cave bear, <i>Ursus spelaeus</i>, denned in European caves in such numbers that its remains are often the commonest thing in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chauvet-Pont d'Arc holds about 300 bear hollows scooped into its floor and the remains of some 190 individuals, with one skull set deliberately on a block of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bones are also at the centre of a long argument about the earliest music, since punctured cave bear femora have been read both as flutes and as the bone-crushing damage left by spotted hyenas scavenging bear cubs in the dens, the round-oval punctures matching hyena premolars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Irish_elk": "The Irish elk, <i>Megaloceros giganteus</i>, stands out among the Pleistocene megafauna both for its sheer body size and for its immense antlers, which spanned up to 4 m (13 feet) and weighed up to 45 kg (99 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears in the fossil record around 400,000 years ago and its populations are thought to have ranged from Ireland to Lake Baikal; absent from western and central Europe before the Last Glacial Maximum, it recolonised the north-west in the Late Glacial Interstadial, and its purported final extinction is about 6,900 years ago in western Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Complete mitochondrial genomes give strong support for a <i>Dama</i>–<i>Megaloceros</i> clade, placing it with the fallow deer, and it is among the animals drawn on the walls at Chauvet-Pont d'Arc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saber-toothed_cat": "Saber-toothed cats are the Machairodontinae, an extinct subfamily of the cat family recovered as monophyletic and made up of three major saber-tooth lineages, traditionally the tribes Metailurini, Smilodontini and Homotheriini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genera placed among them include <i>Dinofelis</i>, <i>Megantereon</i>, <i>Smilodon</i>, <i>Homotherium</i> and <i>Xenosmilus</i>, and the large ones sit more peripherally in cranial morphospace than living big cats do, so that their skull shapes are more varied than any living cat's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not confined to one continent: saber-toothed cats are among the carnivores of the Middle Pleistocene fauna at Zhoukoudian in China, alongside giant deer, straight-tusked elephant, woolly rhinoceros and horses of the genus <i>Equus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Weichselian_glaciation": "The Weichselian is the last cold stage of the Pleistocene in northern central Europe, and its name, along with those of the older Elsterian and Saalian glaciations, entered the scientific literature through the Prussian Geological Survey's mapping of Pleistocene deposits from 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In palynostratigraphic terms it opens with the change from boreal vegetation at the end of the Eemian warm stage to subarctic vegetation, and glaciation in northern Germany most probably began about 25,000 years ago, though glaciers may have pushed into the western Baltic between 70,000 and 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice reached Brandenburg in the east and the northern edge of Hamburg further west, tills record three phases of advance named after Brandenburg, Pomerania and Mecklenburg, and the Weichselian Late Glacial began when the ice melted about 14,500 years ago; the international correlation table for the last 2.7 million years carries the stage in a north-west European column set against the British, North American and New Zealand stages and the marine isotope record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Würm_glaciation": "The Würm is the last glaciation of the Alps and their northern foreland, and it takes its place at the young end of the fourfold scheme of Günz, Mindel, Riss and Würm that Albrecht Penck built from local place names in alphabetical order and set out with Eduard Brückner from 1901; the extended version of that scheme, running from Würm back to Biber, has been the basis of field mapping in the Alpine Foreland for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type locality of the last glacial advance, the Würm in the strict sense, is the Birrfeld, a flat tract between the Aare and the Reuss in northern Switzerland, where luminescence dating puts the maximum ice extent at roughly 25,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Continental records place the last most extensive position of ice sheets and glaciers generally between 26,500 and 19,000 years ago, with deglaciation setting in mainly just after 20,000, and Penck's morphostratigraphy of the glacial series, which pairs each glaciation's moraines with a corresponding gravel train, remains applicable almost everywhere to Würmian sediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Devensian_glaciation": "The Devensian is the last cold stage of the British Pleistocene, and the stratotype that fixes it is at Four Ashes in Staffordshire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is divided into three substages: a Late Devensian from 26,000 to 10,000 years before present, a Middle Devensian from 50,000 to 26,000, and an Early Devensian covering the part that precedes 50,000, above which the Flandrian begins at a base defined as 10,000 radiocarbon years before present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These definitions are the compilation of British Quaternary stage definitions drawn up by Philip Gibbard, modified from Frederick Shotton's scheme of 1973, and the international correlation table for the last 2.7 million years carries the Devensian in a British column set against the north-west European, North American and New Zealand stages and the marine isotope record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fennoscandia": "Fennoscandia is a region of northern Europe named in 1898 by the Finnish geologist Wilhelm Ramsay, who argued in a study of the Quaternary of the Kola Peninsula that Kola, together with Norway, Sweden, Finland, Russian Karelia and the Onega area, forms a physico-geographical area sharply differing from its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He coined the name because none existed for that area: northern Europe covers a larger one, and the Baltic Shield leaves out the Palaeozoic mountains of Norway and Scania in southernmost Sweden, both of which Fennoscandia takes in, so the region is wider than the Precambrian shield now generally called the Fennoscandian Shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate modelling of the region places it between 58 and 71° N and from sea level to 2,469 m (8,100 feet), with boreal forest running from the coast towards the mountains as far as about 68 to 69° N, a belt of mountain birch above it, and tundra above the treeline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rhinoceros": "Rhinoceroses are the Rhinocerotidae, a family of the odd-toed ungulate order Perissodactyla with five living species: the black rhinoceros <i>Diceros bicornis</i> and the white rhinoceros <i>Ceratotherium simum</i> in Africa, and the Indian rhinoceros <i>Rhinoceros unicornis</i>, the Javan rhinoceros <i>Rhinoceros sondaicus</i> and the Sumatran rhinoceros <i>Dicerorhinus sumatrensis</i> in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three Asian species are set apart by their tusked incisors, and the family once ranged far beyond its present limits: two genera, <i>Stephanorhinus</i> and <i>Coelodonta</i>, were present in western Europe through the Middle and Late Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient protein sequences recovered from those extinct genera place the African species as the most basal clade among living rhinoceroses, with <i>Dicerorhinus</i> and the two extinct genera forming a clade that groups in turn with the Asian genus <i>Rhinoceros</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Holocene_climatic_optimum": "The Holocene climatic optimum is the warm interval of the early and middle Holocene, running roughly from 9,000 to 5,000 years ago and warmest near 6,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It goes by several other names — the Hypsithermal, the Altithermal, the Holocene Thermal Maximum and the Mid-Holocene Warm Period — a fair sign of how loosely its edges are drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The warmth was neither global nor simultaneous, arriving early and strongly at high northern latitudes but later and more faintly in the tropics, and its peak is dated at individual sites anywhere between 11,000 and 4,000 years ago, a spread usually laid to the North American ice sheet holding temperatures down until it had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Post-glacial_rebound": "Post-glacial rebound is the slow rise of ground that ice sheets once pressed down, still lifting long after the ice has melted, and it forms one part of what geophysicists call glacial isostatic adjustment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crust does not simply compress under an ice load: mantle rock creeps sideways, so the hollow beneath the ice is opened by rock flowing outwards, raising a broad swell called a forebulge around it, and because that rock is extremely stiff the recovery takes thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anders Celsius recorded the water falling in the Gulf of Bothnia in 1743 and took it for a shrinking sea, Thomas Jamieson gave the right cause in 1865, and the land there and around Hudson Bay still rises about a centimetre (0.4 inches) a year while the collapsing forebulge carries the ground beyond the old ice margin down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"8.2-kiloyear_event": "The 8.2-kiloyear event was an abrupt turn to cold, dry conditions about 8,200 years ago, registered in Greenland ice cores and in lake muds, pollen, cave formations and ocean sediments across four continents, and it stands as the sharpest climate reversal of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is usually explained by a sudden release of glacial meltwater into the North Atlantic, from the vast lakes ponded against the shrinking North American ice sheet or from the collapse of the ice saddle over Hudson Bay, which lidded the ocean with fresh water and slowed the overturning circulation that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its signature is sharp enough to serve as a stratigraphic boundary: the horizon dated 8,236 years before 2000 in a Greenland ice core was ratified in 2018 as the base of the Northgrippian, the middle age of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aegean_Bronze_Age": "The Aegean Bronze Age runs from about 3100 to 1050 BCE, the period in which the societies of Crete, the Cyclades and the Greek mainland took up bronze, built towns and then palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists tell it as three parallel regional sequences, Minoan for Crete, Cycladic for the islands and Helladic for the mainland, each cut into Early, Middle and Late and each of those into I, II and III, so that an object can be placed in a phase without any calendar year being claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three scripts appear on Crete: Cretan hieroglyphic remains undeciphered apart from its numerals, Linear A has drawn many proposed decipherments and no accepted one, and Linear B, deciphered by Michael Ventris in 1952, proved to record Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cycladic_civilisation": "The Cycladic civilisation is the Early Bronze Age culture of the Cyclades, the islands of the central Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its phases carry the names Colin Renfrew gave them after type sites rather than numbers, Grotta-Pelos from about 3100 to 2650 BCE, Keros-Syros to about 2400 and Phylakopi I around 2000, and almost everything known of these islanders comes from cemeteries, most of them looted to supply the antiquities market rather than excavated, so they are far better known in death than in life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is above all the islands' own marble, cut into thin-walled vessels and into figures with folded arms, along with lead, silver and copper drawn from Siphnos, Kythnos and the Laurion ores of Attica, and the obsidian quarried on Melos that reached the Adriatic, Crete, western Anatolia and Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cycladic_figurines": "Cycladic figurines are the marble human figures carved in the islands of the central Aegean during the Early Bronze Age, most of them recovered from tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They range from schematic forms, some no more than ovoid pebbles and the most distinctive fiddle-shaped with a long stalk-like neck and no recognisable head, to the canonical folded-arm type, which was never meant to stand upright since its feet and knees are rendered for a figure lying on its back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The white marble is an accident of survival, for they were painted — black and blue for eyes, eyebrows, hair and the pubic triangle, red for necklaces, bracelets and diadems — and where the paint has gone entirely it can leave a 'paint ghost', a smoother patch or a faint outline where the covered stone weathered differently from the bare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Keros": "Keros is a rugged, uninhabited island at the centre of the Cyclades, the largest of the Small Cyclades, lying south of Naxos between Ios and Amorgos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two deposits of deliberately broken Early Bronze Age marble figurines, stone vessels and pottery lie at Kavos on its western tip, holding no human bone and no carving waste, and the fragments do not fit together: most objects are present as a single piece whose remainder is always absent, so the breaking happened elsewhere and one chosen fragment was carried in by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Facing Kavos across a narrow strait, the islet of Dhaskalio was terraced and built up in marble ferried about 10 kilometres (6 miles) from Naxos and worked metal on a scale unmatched in the Cyclades of its day, and radiocarbon places the site between roughly 2750 and 2250 BCE, after which it was never built over again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_Crete": "Early Minoan Crete is the island in the Early Bronze Age, the first of the three Minoan phases, running from about 3100 to 2000 BCE and ending before any palace was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known far better from its dead than its living, above all from the tholos tomb, a round chamber of fieldstones four to thirteen metres (13 to 43 feet) across and entered from the east, of which more than 70 are known from over 45 sites, nearly all around the Mesara plain in the south, while the north-east built rectangular house tombs instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its settlements are few and have repeatedly been reinterpreted downwards in scale, the hilltop warren of Myrtos Fournou Korifi having been read first as one complex run by a central authority and later as five or six ordinary family houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Minoan_civilisation": "Minoan civilisation is the Bronze Age culture of Crete, conventionally divided into four ages named for its great buildings — Prepalatial, Protopalatial, Neopalatial and Postpalatial — running from about 3100 to 1050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its palaces appear around 1900 BCE, are destroyed around 1720 and are rebuilt grander in the Neopalatial age that ends near 1470, and the island was never one kingdom: the eastern valleys and the west held palace states with towns of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its script was Linear A, written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and undeciphered still, and the records kept at Knossos and Chania for up to two centuries after the Neopalatial destructions are in Linear B, which is Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Arthur_Evans": "Arthur Evans was an English archaeologist, Keeper of the Ashmolean Museum from 1884 to 1908, who excavated the Bronze Age palace site of Knossos on Crete from 1900 to 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had come to the island in 1894 chasing a script rather than a palace, having met engraved stones he took for a pre-Phoenician writing, and the two phases he distinguished in it, one pictorial and one linear, grew into the three scripts he named Cretan Hieroglyphic, Linear A and Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave the civilisation he uncovered the name Minoan and published it in the six volumes of <i>The Palace of Minos</i>, and he rebuilt much of the site, restoration work that the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial and that is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Knossos": "Knossos is the largest Minoan palace site, standing on the low hill of Kephala in north-central Crete about six kilometres (four miles) inland from Heraklion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a mound as much as a palace, since an extensive Neolithic settlement lies beneath it and modelled radiocarbon puts the first occupation at 6900 to 6600 BCE, while the Bronze Age building above is arranged around a paved central court with blocks of rooms about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Minos Kalokairinos made a small excavation there in 1878 and took out twelve great store jars, and Arthur Evans dug the site from 1900 to 1931; what stands over the ruins now is largely his restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Minoan_palace": "A Minoan palace is a complex built around a rectangular central court, the building type that defines Crete from about 1900 BCE and is best known from Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court is usually laid out north to south and measures 54 by 27 metres (177 by 89 feet) at the largest, but the plan is centrifugal, since almost all daily activity happened in the blocks of rooms around it rather than out on it, and the supports are round, oval, square or cruciform, tapering upwards or downwards and set in colonnades of alternating types rather than repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word palace is Arthur Evans's inheritance and excavators now often say 'court-centred building' instead, since there are more of these complexes than the handbooks suggest, on a far wider range of scales and not all founded or abandoned together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Throne_Room_at_Knossos": "The Throne Room at Knossos is a small chamber on the west side of the palace's central court, among its cult rooms, uncovered by Arthur Evans in his first season in 1900 and surviving almost intact because it lay only a few centimetres below the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A gypsum throne with a high back of undulating leaf-shaped outline stands 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall against one wall, its seat hollowed to fit a body and raised above the gypsum benches that run round the room, and a sunken tank of closely fitted slabs faces it across the floor, reached by six steps and with no outlet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walls carried a landscape of running water, rushes and palms with a pair of couchant griffins flanking the inner doorway, and the room is later than the palace around it, Evans assigning the whole system to the latest palace epoch and its griffin fresco belonging to Late Minoan II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"
};

